      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-03-06_WidnesExaminer_art0012" n="art0012">
        <head>LOCAL' PARLIAMENT.</head>
        <p>LOCAL' PARLIAMENT.</p>
        <p>COUNCILLOR BROADHURST'S UNEMPLOYED BILL PASSED.</p>
        <p>Councillor Broadhurst's l'nemployed Bill was passed without a division at the meeting of the Parliamentary Debating Society on Tuesday evening. Several amend- ments were brought forward, but not one was carried, and the Bill therefore " passes in, o law" as sub- mitted. The debate ,was resumed by Mr. H. E. HAWTHORNE (C., E. Belfast) who supported the afforestation scheme as a means of providing a large number of people with work, without inflicting the taint of pauperism. Some amusement was created when Mr. Hawthorne attributed remarks to the Member for Burton-on-Trent (Mr. Jarman) which that gentle- man denied uttering. "Mr. Speaker, I never said a word to that effect " protested Mr. JARMAN. "Then you must have thought it " retorted Mr. HAWTHORNE excitedly, and the House roared. Mr. E. ENGLAND (L., Warrington) described the . unemployed question as a great tragedy, and said they ' should all endeavour to find some rational solution. He gave the Bill his unqualified support, and would not vote for any of the amendments because he would not be a party to embarrass the Govermnent in passing what; to his mind was a very great measure: Some people stated that drink was one cause of unemployment. Granted, but when they looked into the subject they did not find that drink was causing any more unemployment to-day than it was twenty years ago—in fact he was inclined to think that less unemployment was to be attributed to it to-day. He believed that unemploy- ment was one of the causes of drinking, fox. men went to the public-house to drown their sorrows. He believed the great secret of unemployment to-day was the invention and improvement of machinery. If they could get men engaged in afforestation, they would not only be providing work but improving, the health of the worker. If there was no return it would be money well spent, but as a matter of fact there' would be a return, though not inunediately. Afforestation would help on kindred trades and all parties should support the Bill, feeling that it would help to ameliorate the lot of those who were in a state of misery.-</p>
        <p>Mr. W. KILDUFF (N., Dublin Harbour) said that as a democrat and a .trade unionist he was in perfect sympathy with the general principles of the BM. It would be a blot on the measure to fix a universal wage of a certain amount. Trade unionists had tried to do that and had met with failure. There were 8,000,000 men in the United Kingdom drawing under £1 per week, and he considered that was a disgrace to civilization. He confessed that it surprised him that such a Bill should ' come from a -Conservative Government.</p>
        <p>Mr. W. RIGBY (N., W. Belfast) moved an amend- ment that the money required for the purposes of the Bill be raised by a tax on ground values. The present land system w,.s one of the principal causes of unemployment., but under the Bill the land would not bear any increased taxation. The people of this country bore twice as much taxation as the ielabitants of the rest of Europe. We in England paid 3d. per head per day. In other European countries they paid lid. per day. That was not the wort of the business, for 83 per cent of English taxation was indirect and fell heavily upon the poor peonle. The Bill would increase taxa- tion indirectly by tat.iing out of the Imperial exchequer £2,000,000 or £3,000,000 per annum, but it would leave the great landonners alone. Land had reaped the fruit of the labours of the workingmen; it htti lived on the enterprise of others. A tax on ground values would relieve industry and lead to improvement in the build- ing trade. Mr. T. RIGBY (N., Cork City), seconded. The Government declined to accept the amendment and on a division it was defeated tiy 30 votes to 21. Mr. W. H. SHERBUtIN (L., Rotherham) moved a further amendment that the price to be paid for the land should not exceed 25 years purchase at the present ratable value of the land as declared by the local authorities. He acknow- ledged that the Bill was well-meant, but it did not go to the root of the matter. (A Voice : "Fiscal reform does the rest.") They hardly looked for a Bill that would go to the root of the matter from the Con- servative side of the House, when they recognised what that party derived a great deal of its strength from—the land monopoly. The amendment, he claimed, would provide a necessary safeguard. In the past pro- gressive measures which had come from the Conserva- tive bepches—and they had Come some times— (laughter)—had bad points of weakness, some of which had altogether nullified the progressive parts. They had had land measures, but when it bad come to the point of purchase they bad found that what was intended for the poor people hire 311 gone into the pockets of the landlords. Mr. JOHNSTO'NM (L , Dumfries) seconded.</p>
        <p>This amendment was rejected by the Government and on a vote being taken it was defeated by 3,1 to 46.</p>
        <p>Mr. RIGBY (N., E. Tipperary) brought forward an amendment that in no c•ise should the rate of pay be less than 21s. per week. He remarked that. in Norfolk, where the Bill would largely be operative, labourers were paid the princely sum of us. to 13s. per week. He did not pretend for a moment that even 21s. was a living wage, but knowing the bad company the President or the Local Government Board had got in his Cabinet— (langhter)—be wanted to make it easy for him. ,Low wages were responsible for the great bulk of our poverty—not drink as Some a the Opposition would say—(Ministerial " Hear, hears ")—rind certainly not Free Trade as the Government would say. (Opposition laughter). Mr. W. NIGHTINGALF (S., NOrManton) seconded. The amendment v. as defeated by 34 to 17.</p>
        <p>S.S. LLOYD (C., Windsor), continuing the general debate, said the Bill was a %veil-thought-out measure and was an earnest attempt to tunelioriato the condition of the people of this country. There would alwa3s be a certain percentage of unemployed, but there was no need to have the grinding poverty which exiqteci at the present time. He claimed that the imposition of tariffs, combined with the present Bill, would effect a greet improvement. One of the causes of unemploy- ment was the setting side to a great extent of the apprenticeship system. We wanted to make England</p>
        <p>an agricultural country again, by making it worth while to cultivate the land, and also to bring up more of the youth of the land as skilled tradesmen. Mr. E. WYNNE JONES (Leader of the Opposition) said he could not understand why a Government that could give preference to the Colonies, reform the House of Lords, and federate the Empire in one Bill, should come down to a mere skeleton like the present measure. He was afraid the Bill only dealt with the question in a tinkering sort of way, but it admitted the right-to-work , Capital had nothing to fear from labour ; the only thing it bad to fear was driving the people to despair. Tbe country was in earnest on this question. The toot of the difficulty was the land monopoly, for so long as people were allowed to withhold the means God bad given for the production of wealth we should have this great problem pressing on the nation. The question should be placed above party and that was why the Opposi- would not rote against the Bill. Once the workers made up their minds that the unemployed question should be solved, a solution would be found. The fault at present was that too low a value was placed on human life.</p>
        <p>Councillor BRoADHURST (President of the Local Government Board) in replying to the discussion con- tended that unemployment was greater in thiscoun- try than in Germany. They were told recently by Captain Crosfleld that there were no slums in Berlin like those in England. The so-called "evils" of Pro- tection had given the Germans all the prosperity they were enjoying at present. It had been denied that there was anything new in this Bill. He never claimed that there was. It was based upon the report of the com- mission appointed by the Conservative Government of 1902, He claimed that the Act would permanently employ 100,000 men and would take in 18,000 men every winter.</p>
        <p>The Bill was then carried without a division.</p>
        <p>REV. E. C. E. CARLETON'S EDUCATION BILL.</p>
        <p>Next Tuesday evening the Minister for Education (Rev. E. C. E. Carleton) will introduce the following Bill" making provision for certain amendments of the present Acts relating to the Education of the young":- 1. Where any voluntary school in urban districts shall have been or shall hereafter be built either by any religious body, by public subscription, or by private persons, and one of the original primary objects of the school be the teaching of denominational religious teaching therein, the local Education Authority as defined by the Education Act, 1902, shall henceforth pay to the body or persons to whom such school belonged on the 31st of December, 1902, in addition to all other sums at present paid, or to be paid under clause 19 hereof, a sum equal to 3 per cent upon the capital value of the school buildings on the 31st of December, 1902 (subject to the deduction hereinafter provided). 2. In any such school as aforesaid the denomina- tional religion for which such school was founded shall Alone he taught and the religious trusts of the original trust deed shall so far as practicable he followed. 3. Teachers appointed to the regular staff of Volun- tary Schools or volunteering to give denominational religious instruction, shall furnish evidence of their fitness to give that denominational instruction. , 4. The expense of teaching religious subjects in accordance with the trusts of any such voluntary school as aforesaid shall be borne by the body or persons to %horn the percentage named in clause I shall be paid. Such percentage shall only be paid so long as the religious trust shall be duly observed. 5. Where it can he shown to the satisfaction of three Commissioners to be appointed by the Judges of the High Court that any subscriptions towards the Building of any such voluntary school as aforesaid have bean made by persons of other religious convictions, or by any limited Company, or public body, with the object of avoiding the 'creation of any school-board or pre- venting a local rate for educational purposes, the capital sum so found to have been subscribed shall be deducted from the value of the School buildings before the percentage named in clause 1 is ascertained. 6. Where any. such School as aforesaid shall not have upon its books the full number of children for which the school is recognised under the latest regula- tions of the Board of Education the percentage named in clause I. shall be reduced pro rata.</p>
        <p>The Local Education Authority shall furnish all necessary accommodation not otherwise provided. 8. The parent of each child attending schools pro- vided under clause 7 shall state in writing whether his child shall receive religious education I by (a) the Church of England, (b) the Church of Rome, (c) the United Free Churches, (d) the Jewish Church, (e) or not at all. The syllabus in such schools shall give opportunity for denominational teaching by the four named bodies but at their own expense. 9. Teachers in Council Schools shall not be subject to any religious test nor be compelled to teach religions subjects, but may so teach if willing to volunteer. 10. No school shall without express sanction of the Board of Education hereafter be built at a greater cost for buildings and furniture thai £ll per head of accommodation if it do not exceed 900 for all depart- ments with an additional 10s. per head for every 100 scholars below 900. 11. if the Board of Education shall sanction any expense beyond that named in clause 10 the local Authority shall place at the disposal of the Managers of all voluntary schools in the area immediately adjoin- ing the new Council school a similar allowance per bead for the improvement of such voluntary schools but for 110 other purpose.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-03-06_WidnesExaminer_art0013" n="art0013">
        <head>SINGLE SCHOOL AREAS.</head>
        <p>SINGLE SCHOOL AREAS.</p>
        <p>12. The above provisions shall not apply to volun- tary schools in rural districts (hereinafter called "Country Schools.")</p>
        <p>13. Clause 8 hereof shall apply to Country Schools whether voluntary or otherwise. _ . . _ _ _ _ .</p>
        <p>14. The head -teacher of a Country School shall be appointed without religious test and clause 9 hereof shall apply to him.</p>
        <p>15: The assistant teachers shall be appointed with a view to the teaching of religion in accordance with the tenets of such of the denominations named in clause 8 as the school shall require and in the event of there not being four assistants they shall be selected according to the requirements of the majority of the scholars as ascertained under Clauses 8 and 13 provided that one assistant shall always he provided for the religious denomination by or for whom the ceuntry school was originally built.</p>
        <p>The payment of all teachers named in clause 15 shall be by the Education Authority. 17. Recognized persons of each of the 4 denomina- tions named in ClauseB (not exceeding 3 for each) shall he allowed the right of entry into cetuntry schools within the hours appointed for teaching religious sub- jects shall be allowed to teach the same.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-03-06_WidnesExaminer_art0014" n="art0014">
        <head>GERAL.</head>
        <p>GERAL.</p>
        <p>18. Education in all Elementary Schools shall be free.</p>
        <p>19. One uniform Parliamentary Grant of £2 7s. fid. per child in average attendance shall be given (provided that the school be sufficient) in place of the various grants given at present; any further cost to be borne by the rates.</p>
      </div>
