      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-03-06_WidnesExaminer_art0016" n="art0016">
        <head>TEACHERS' CONFERENCE.</head>
        <p>TEACHERS' CONFERENCE.</p>
        <p>MR. J UPSON ELECTED PRESIDENT OF THE LANCASHIRE ASSOCIATION.</p>
        <p>Overcrowding and its Remedy.</p>
        <p>The Lancashire County Association of the National Union of Teachers, which has the distinction of being the strongest county organization of teachers in the country. held its annual conference at the Wycliffe Hall, Warrington, on Saturday, when about 200 delegates from all parts of Lancashire were present. The morning was devoted to meetings of the various sections—County Boroughs, Non-County Boroughs, and Administrative County—and the officials for the year were chosen. Reports containing the record of useful work covering a wide field were submitted. In the afternoon the annual meeting of the Repre- sentative Assembly was held under the presidency of Mr. W. Carr (Morecambe), who was supported by Mr. J. Upson (President-elect), Mr. Henry E. Storey (treasurer) and Mr. S. Cornell (secretary).</p>
        <p>ABSENTEES FROM THE PUBLIC MEETING. MESSRS. PICKLES AND SHARPLES EXPLAIN</p>
        <p>Before the usual business commenced, Mr. G. Sharples, of 'Manchester, and Mr. A. R. Pickles, of Burnley, who were announced to address the public meeting in the Parr Hill the previous evening, tendered explanations of their hbsence.</p>
        <p>The CHAIRMAN said it was unfortunate that Messrs. Pickles and Sharpies were not at the meeting, as by their absence the teachers lost an opportunity of get..ing into intimate and close touch with he Secretary to the Board of Education. However, their non-attend- ance was absolutely unavoidable, and no charge of neglecting their duties could be brought against them. Mr. UPSON emphasised the local sense of disappoint- ment and soreness or fee ing at the absence of the two speakers whom they pre-eminently wished to hear. It was extremely unfortunate. especially as Mr. Trevelynn expressed to him (Mr. Upson) his own sense of dis- appointment at the absence of the two geLtlemen whom he believed to be the mouthpieces of the teachers gener- ally, arid from whom he expected to hear-something in an authoritative way as to the teachers' position on matters of education. After the direction, in which Mr. Trevelyan's speech went, their absence was doubly regrettable, and the members of the Association natur- ally felt bitterly disappointed that the Executive of the N.U.T. was not represented at the first meeting held under the auspices of the County Association at which a member of the Government bad been present.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-03-06_WidnesExaminer_art0017" n="art0017">
        <head>FINANCIAL.</head>
        <p>FINANCIAL.</p>
        <p>Mr. H. E. STOREY (treasurer). submitted the balance sheet which showed that. during the year 1908 the balance in hand had been increased from £l4l 7s. 3d, to £142 Da. 7d. Subscriptions were received in respect of 8,288 members, the strength of the Warrington branch being 200. THE NEW PRESIDENT.</p>
        <p>The CHAIRMAN, in calling upon Mr. Upson to succeed him in the presidential chair, thanked the whole of the members and officials of the Association for the loyal support they bad given him during his year of office. He believed he might say, without fear of contradiction, that there had not been a single jarring note in the year's work, and not One unkind word had been spoken. It afforded him peculiar pleasure to ask Mr. Upson to take the chair, because they were fellow- students at Westminster Training College some twenty-six or twenty-seven years ago. .rhey spent two happy years there together And he (Mr. Carr) was delighted when NI r,Upson was chosen for the presidency. In him they would have a man who would carry the association to a higher point of efficiency and useful- ness thin ever it had reached before. He was a man of wide and varied experience, a man of parts who knew not only bow to guide Out:bevy to lead. (Applause). Mr.UPSON then tookiho chair and was enthusiasti- cally greeted. RETIRING PRESIDENT THANKED.</p>
        <p>MR. MEREDITH (Ulverston), in proposing a vote of thanks to the retiring President, said the criticisms levelled by the association at the proposals of the Government in the late Education Bill, the efforts made with regard to the half-time question and the supply and training of teachers and the conference of teacher representatives on educational bodies were good evidences that the operations of the association had been well directed. It was, however, necessary that something should be done in the direction of securing more general correlation of schools and scholars, with the view to enibling children in the elementary schools to take full advantages of the opportunities afforded. One of the greatest triumphs of the association, with which Mr. Carr was closely associated, was the arrange- ment of the annual conference of the N.U.T..at More- cambe, the value of which it MS impossible to exagger- ate. During the twenty-five years or more that Mr. Carr had been a qualified teacher he had come to the front very much in educational matters and he had taken first place in the public esteem in his own district as an expert adviser on such questions. Mr. Lambert (I I nd ey) seconded. Mr. Pickles, in supporting said that in 1882 when he went up to Westminster College for the Scholarship examination, a raw youth from Lancashire, the first young man he met was Mr. Carr, who was staying there during Scholarship week to take care of the candidates. Mr. UPSON said he had known Mr. Carr longer than anyone present and there were two qualities in his character which had brought him very near to his (Mr. Upson's) heart. The first was his absolute loyalty to his friends and to teachers. Twenty-six years ago Mr. Carr stood between him (Mr. Upson) and very severe treatment by the College authorities. (Laughter.) He would not go into details—(laughter)—but the magnifi- cent, way in which Mr. Carr defended him when he was in a corner had made him never forget the splendid qualities he then showed. Another of Mr. Carr's characteristics was his strength, and he had shown admirable firmness and tact in contro!ling the meetings. The vote was very cordially passed and Mr. Carr briefly responded. ELEC LION OF OFFICERS. A LADY VICE-PRESIDENT.</p>
        <p>Mr. G. WALKER (Bolton) moved the election of Mr tleredith, of Ulverston, to the vice-presidency and Mr TAYLOR seconded.</p>
        <p>Miss LIVESEY (Wigan), said she did not wish to throw discord into the meeting, hut it seemed to her that ladies were first everywhere except at a meeting of teachers.(Laughter;lThere they took the last placemless it was a case of working for the benevolent and orphan fund or promoting bazaars and whist drives. (Laugh- ter.) During the six years' existence of the association they had only had one lady officer, and it was now her pleasure to propose that Miss Conway, of Liverpool, ho elected vice-president. Mr. KIRKPATRICK (Manchester) seconded, and Miss Woof) (Manchester) supported the amendment. A vote by ballot was taken and resulted in Miss Conway being elected by 62 votes to 57. Miss CONWAY, who said the result, was a great surprise to her, expressed the hope that she would have the backing of every member of the Association when she had to fulfil the great responsibity of the presidency. Mr. HENRY E. STOREY (Manchester) was re-elected treasurer ; Mr. Hughes (Eccles), Press Secretary ; Mr. S. Cornell (Preston), secretary ; Miss Livesey (Wigan), general secretary of the Ladies' Bureau ; Messrs. Albert J.S idebottom and John E. Turner,aud ito rs. A hearty vote of thanks was accorded to all the retiring officers on the proposition of Mr. J. A. Storey (Salford) seconded by Mr. Brook (Dunkirk.)</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-03-06_WidnesExaminer_art0018" n="art0018">
        <head>PRESIDENT'S ADDRESS.</head>
        <p>THE PRESIDENT'S ADDRESS.</p>
        <p>Mr. Upson, in the course of his —presidendia address said :</p>
        <p>It is common knowledge that a large number Of men and women who have been expensively trained by the local authorities and the State have at this moment absolutely no employment. Their numbers will be added to in a few weeks by about 5,000 acting teachers who will receive certificates, and still further in July by about four thousand, who will leave • the Training Colleges. Optimists are saying that we shall muddle through,' but muddling through means broken purposes, disappointed hopes, loss, and pain, and as a nation we have no right to muddle through at the cost of human suffering and blighted lives. Nor was there any reason to do so, yet we had no foresight shown, no method of control, and no careful provision for the future. The demands of the moment have been met by panic methods, and inevitable confusion has followed. When the demand for teachers as a result of legislation exceeds the supply there is an immediate lowering of the standard of examinations (especially in the case of acting teachers) and when the supply exceeds the demand there is a tendency to raise the standard and as a consequence we have teachers holding certificates nominally of the same value, and actually of the same commercial value, yet widely differing in value as a standard of attainment and efficiency. As teachers we hold strongly that our certificates should show an approximate standard of uniformity in educational and scholastic value, and therefore the final examination for the teacher's certificate should not in one year scarcely rise in value above a Standard VII. examination and within a year or two approach in difficulty the examination for a University degree in honours. In ordpr that there should be no playing fast and loose with the standard of the teacher's certificate I have long and strongly held the opinion that the examination for its issue should he entirely in the hands of the N.U.T. What sufficient reason can be given against suck) a course? Many professions have this power and it could not possibly be charged against us that we should lower the standard of the examination. The chartered accountant, the lawyer, the chemist, stand high ,in the public estimation and their certificates issued by the examining bodies of their own societies 1</p>
        <p>are universally accepted as bona-fide proofs of the standard of attainment reached by the holders of these certificates and of their general uniform value. In trying to answer the question as to how the supply of teachers may approximately he made to meet the demand will you allow me a local illustration? When the present Warrington Education Committee came into power as a consequence of the Education Act of 1902 an estimate was made as to the number of teachers who would leave the service of the Committee every year (by promotion, marriage, &amp;c.) With the very restricted experience of the Committee it was estimated that the annual loss in these ways would be 35. Permis- sion was obtained from the Board of Education to hold our own examination for candidates end on the result of that examination the top 35 (subject of course to physical fitness)were chosen to be pupil teachers. But by experience it was found that the estimate of 35 was too high and that 20 vacancies were nearer the mark to he filled annually. Fewer candidates were therefore selected, and the Committee accepted for training as pupil teachers only a few more than they were prepared to employ as certificated teachers. I don't mean to say that the Committee was pledged to employ the same people as it accepted and trained, but that approxi- mately the same number of certificated teachers would be requirad annually as pupil teachers who were accepted. Now what is possible to one Education Committee is in this matter possible to all, and still more, is possible to the State. The Board of Education could approximately ascertain bow many teachers are annually required and only accept about the same number of bursars or pupil teachers, or some definite number higher than that to allow liberty of choice to managers and authorities in making appointments and to allow for contingencies. Yearly there would be an ascertained number of candidates required. These could be chosen as candidates for the Civil Service are chosen from those who did the best in the examination. The examining body then would have no voice in the number to be examined for admission, or in the number to be finally examined for the certificate, but would only see that the candidates reached a certain definite standard of attainment., A variation of this method although not in principle, would be only to allow each authority that number of candidates which experience had proved to be necessary to fill up the normal vacancies in the service of that authority. The candidates having been chosen—and it should be a condition of choice that they should ultimately pro- ceed to a Training College—would at the end of their training be pretty sure of employment and the State would be assured that the long and costly process of t-aining through which the the teacher tutd passed would not have been in vain. And this I would say should be the only entrance into the profession. This method I admit would not touch the present difficulty, that must be met in other ways, but I contend it would prevent the recurrence of such difficulties in the future, and would render impossible that state of things when we find at one time authorities clamour- ing for teachers and clamouring in vain, or at another time hundreds of certificated teachers with breaking hearts searching in vain for employment. And now may I turn to a phase of the actual work and life of the teacher in the schools for a moment or two. I want as your President to utter a word of warning against the growing practice of some Educa- tion Authorities of hampering the teacher by the imposition of other than educational work,of such con- stant intrusion into the schools for so many ether than educational purposes, of so much inspection and examination by local officials, even of doing so much for the teacher as to rob him of iditiative and largely of responsibility and to make him a mere part of educational machinery.</p>
        <p>In many districts teachers are required to take part, in the medical inspection of children, to collect statistics of various kinds,to keep large numbers of stock, store, and other books, to make minute and elaborate returns of almost every conceivable kind (and many of them unnecessary) to accept schemes of work drawn up by theorists in offices, and I have lately heard of a scheme to provide even uniform Time Tables for Infant Schools in one great area. And so the time and energy of the teacher is largely taken up by routine and cleric .1 work, and he is gradually divorced from the work of his life, the grey mists settle down, and one of the most terrible calamities that can overtake a man—a loss of interest in his work—comes upon him.</p>
        <p>I do not say that this state of things obtains to any great extent, but that it does prevail to some extent and that the tendency is for it. to increase. In your name I wish to utter a warning before it is too late.</p>
        <p>In conclusion may I say that in spite of all these difficulties which I have outlined awl in spite of the shortsightedness of many who have control of education as to the true function of the.teacher, in spite of dis- couragements' of hindrances, of misunderstandings and suspicions, of partial success and frequent failure, we still regard our calling as providing the widest scope in the world for true human service,andig the strength and confidence inspired by that belief, we labour on, knowing</p>
        <p>"That unto him who worlcs, and feels he works The golden year is ever at the doors."</p>
        <p>Mr. H. CosNErr (Liverpool) in proposing a vote of thanks to Mr. Upson for bis address, said their new President was a most able exponent of the teachers' position and Warrington was exceedingly fortunate in having the advice and services of such a man. Some years ago he served on the Town Council and he now filled the even more difficult position of representing the teachers on the Warrington Education Committee. That afternoon he had revealed himself and they felt they had got into touch with a personalty they could trust. He spoke directly and gave them a feeling of strength. They had every confidence in the man at the helm. (Hear, hear.) Mr. Upson had touched upon a very difficult question—the supply and training of teachers—and no doubt they would obtain great benefit from his guidance in dealing with it. He bad shown breadth of view which would enable them to sustain the opinions they had as a body with a purpose. Mr. F. FLETCHER (Manchester) seconded. Mr.Upson, he said, came to the meeting twelve months }lgo as an almost unknown man. He had that afternoon given them an intensely interesting and practical address from a practical teacher, and some points he had touched upon were of the utmost importance. The question of the supply of teachers loomed largely before them at the present time. The number of Pertificated teachers, fully qualified, who were walking the streets looking for employment in all kinds of ways—some taking up temporary places as shop assistants—was a Bcandnlong shame. And yet there were in the schools people who were working without any qualification at all. The question wanted taking in hand, as well as another matter which Mr. Upson called attention to— the side doors by which some teachers could enter the profession. Then there was the tendency to impose upon the teachers' duties which were really outside their legitimate work. The teacher's work was to teach the child and not to attend to all kinds of outside duties.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-03-06_WidnesExaminer_art0019" n="art0019">
        <head>OTHER BUSINESS.</head>
        <p>OTHER BUSINESS.</p>
        <p>It was decided to grant. the Secretary an honorarium of £3O for his services during the past year.</p>
        <p>A proposal by Mr. H. E. SToREY that the President should he presented with a suitable badge of office, Was defeated by 38 votes to 26. _ _</p>
        <p>Mr. LAMBERT (Hindley) brought forward a series of alterations to rules, which would involve considerable changes in the machinery of the organization. Mr. Lambert claimed that they bad only one object—to improve and make more effective the position of the Association.</p>
        <p>After considerable discussion the question was adjourned.</p>
        <p>Miiml</p>
      </div>
