      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-03-06_WidnesExaminer_art0027" n="art0027">
        <head>no 7 75„ 7 , 74' – ° 4-Lt In ?,4 r V V fa. inasllls</head>
        <p>no 7 75„ 7 , 74' - ° 4-Lt In ?,4 r V V fa. inasllls</p>
        <p>SALYATION ARMY.</p>
        <p>COLONEL WI-RTEIORE'S VISIT TO WARRINGTON</p>
        <p>Hugh arranged . , Whatmore, Field-Secretary of Great t'llene, who visited Warrington on Sunday to speak Salvati 'indef ati tings anged by the local corps of the gabi_ 911 Anny, is one of the most prominent and u workers' of that wonderful organization. twentY-seven years since the Colonel became a Yationist. Prior to that he was an optician by parent professio_ u and he entered the Army through th corps e 'the A at Whitechapel, London. At that time .--11nY was a much-despised organization, but now Pr,'l9udices have been lived down and the good work 411(41 is done amongst the poor and down-trodden by he devoted men and women who have enrolled them- -B,.?res under its banner, is freely recognized. Colonel %hatinore has had a wide and varied experience in Other lands as well as amongst the slum dwellers in -,:ngland. For some time he was in charge of work at iihicago and occupied a prominent position at the nited States headquarters. He pioneered the Army's Work in Italy and remained in that country for two 'rears. Afterwards he was second-in-command in Sweden and on returning to England he undertook the work of various sections of the Army. One of the io • • ost important appointments he held was the West of England command, his district atending from Swindon in a straight line to weYraouth, thence to Land's End, and including the 'whole of Wales- -a huge area for one man to have eontrol over. At the Present time Colonel Whatmore is the third officer of the United Kingdom and he is "Ponsible for the care, healtb, salaries and general Well-being of nearly 5,000 officers. He has two Assistant secretaries in London •to help him in his '1.,1,e,1'0,11s duties. The colonel piloted General 'Booth uls last motor tour and is now engaged in mapping the details of another tour to take place in spring. he General is in his eightieth year but. finds great .; ealthfulness in these t long journeys, although they .114'c:dye addresses at 'a great many public meetings. The vitrary embraces such a vast number of towns and ages that it has to be worked out with the exactness " Bradshaw." Colonel Whatmore is of a mild genial disposition but his addresses have the true ittg of sincerity. aturally the visit. of such a distinguished officer 711sed great interest locally, and Warrington •thevationists did everything in their power to make To occasion a red letter one in the history of the '0,1138. The Colonel was accompanied by Brigadier Ro-e'rge Holmes, who is also an effective speaker and a .!o°l organiser. In the morning there was a Holiness I).llveation. at the Salvation 'Hall, Academy Street. eth., Ivis le the most interesting - gathering of the day Cej tliat held in the Parr Hall in the afternoon, when • 'it.`ollel Whatmore delivered a lecture on "Drink and te;11. eVils." Before the meeting a procession of Nt Peranee workers was formed at the top of Winwick 1 ,eet, and .headed by the Salvaiion Army band, Pa-11..ehed along the 'principal ,thoroughfares to the the r Hall. The Good Templars were represented by '131.„ niembers of several local lodges, including' • ri s'• Joseph Richardson, Chief Templar of the War- rrligton Ideal Lodge (4488), Bro. K. Blase, Chief 4111,, 1?..,_1ar of the Bonial Branch Lodge, Sister Mrs. NI-au', of they Temperance Mission Lodge, and Bro. R. !ad', of the Widnes Consolation Lodge. The oZhabites also joined in the procession, the chief chiceerrs_being :—Wycliffe Tent, Bro. George Colley, j. uler ; Bro. Garnett, Past Chief Ruler ,• Bro. wood- tiradley,•secretary. Charity Tent: Bro. Little- -1,11 Past Chief Ruler; Bro. W. Wright, secretary. e 'toekton Heath and Sankey Bridges tents were _B° represented. The proceedings in the hall were Presided over by the Mayor (Alderman Forshaw), also° 'was accompanied by the Mayoress. There were anri present on the platform :—Colonel Whatmore St u Brigadier Holmes, Alderman Burton, Councillors c eel, Skelland, Dickenson. and W. B. Roberts, ,rc•iptain Waring, Mrs. Simpson, Dr. and Mrs. Adams. Lee band, under the conductorship of Bandmaster c__et. hag made great progress and is undoubtedly a are?it to the Corps. Several selections were given in pleasing manner. cre'hie NIAvoR offerel a few words of encoursgement to pie_ °es' Salvationists. They would all have been old 2sed, he said, if they could have seen "grand (4_133_ an of the Salvation Army—(applause)—but the lierat was doing his good and noble work abroad. reteiT sail congratulated him very heartily upon his .anul'eav rY from a very serious operation—(applause)— for hoped and prayed that God would spare I,i:tat :sr many years to continue his useful labours. Ladysmith Day and he (the Mayor) had been to rpn with the Warrington Territorials. He was ,cDoleuunterdY _that they not only served their King aril ut that they served their God. Speaking of our good ba ooat and his Majesty King, he was very pleased indeed to Majesty recently sent a substantial donation _to the Salvation Army. (Applause.) "I "Po added the Mayor "that some of his weathly</p>
        <p>dition of my left lung was becoming serious. "After twenty-two weeks of great Debility, I fortunately came across a report of a clse cured by Dr. Williams' Pink Pills after other remedies bad failed. So I sent for a supply of Dr. Williams' Pink Pills.</p>
        <p>ALL HIS SUFFERINGS DISPELLED BY DR. WILLIAMS' PINK FILLS. "Before I had used the contents of a few boxes I experienced a marked steadiness in my nerves, and I was encouraged by this to per- severe. Before long I felt great relief from pain. My breathing was better, and I wa§ :Able to walk about with ease. Every dose of Dr. Williams' Pink Pills seemed to infuse new life into me. My blood %vas renewed, and I felt a pleasant glow all over my body. Then my appetite returned and food caused no pain or flatulence. I put on flesh rapidly, and every trace of my troubles vanished." Dr. Williams' Pink Pills have cured Anzemia (Bloodlessness), Indigestion, Headaches, Back- aches; Palpitations, and Debility; also the worst cases of Eczema and- other 'Skin Disorders, Rheumatism, the effects, of • Overwork and Worry,' Sciatica, Nervous Disorders dreaded by men, and the aches and ills of women. Sold by most dealers, but avoid substitutes, and look for the seven words on every package, 'Dr. Williams Pink Pills for Pale People." Or send 25. gd. for one box, or 135. gd. for six boxes, to Dr. Williams' Medicine Co., 46, Holborn Viaduct, London. Remember, the pills that CURE are</p>
        <p>subjects will follow his example and do likewise." (Hear, bear.) Solomon said "A word fitly spoken is like apples of gold in pictures of silver,' and the Salvation Army was carrying out that text. There was no doubt that it stretched out a helping hand to all sorts and conditions of people—men, women and children, the outcasts and the fallen—and endeavoured to bring them to lead good and righteous lives. The Salvation Army, he considered, was one of the greatest blessings not only of this country, but of thu world, because it got at people who were untouched by other religious bodies. A country without religion was nothing, for</p>
        <p>" 'Tis religion that can give Sweetest pleasures while we live, 'Tis religion can supply Solid comfort when we die."</p>
        <p>lie hoped and earnestly prayed, as he was sure they all did, that the blessing of God would he upon the good and useful work of the salvation Army. (Applause). Colonel WHATMORE dealt with his subject in a simple yet striking way and delivered an address which iss listened to with close attention. Councillor STEEL, in proposing a vote of thanks to. the Mayor, said his Worship was upholding the high traditions of his office 9nd had not spared himself in responding to the numerous calls upon his services, Referring to the comparatively small attendanct. Councillor Steel said he thought if those were all the teetotallers they had in Warrington it was time they began to bestir themselves and get a greater number,so that when Colonel Whatmore came again to address the Salvation Army and the teetotal brethren in Warring- ton, the hall would be packed to suffocation. Alderman BURTON in seconding said the Mayor and ibyoresi were never so happy as when engaged in some good work, irrespective of creed or sect. The vote was supported by Brigadier HOLMES and heartily passed. In the evening a great Salvation meeting in the Parr Hall brought Col Whatmore's visit to a successful close Our readers are reminded that the annual selt-denial week in connection with the Army commences to-day. (Saturday.) The Warrington Corps raised £BO last year and an effort is being made to increase the con- tribution by at least £5. The money will-be spent in training young people who go from Warrington to become Army officers, in sending missionaries abroad, and in supporting the social institutions and general work of the Army. Adjutant Christmass, alto bris charge of the work in Warrington, has held 32 appoint- ment 3 during his 23 years experience in the Army, but he is a new-comer to the North of England.</p>
        <p>An Opponent for Sir John Brunner. The annual meeting of the .Northwich Division Conservative and Unionist Association was held at Northwich on Saturday, Mr. W. H. Verdin presiding. A sub-committee had had under 'consideration the selection of a candidate, and it was reported that i had been decided that the division should be fought at the next election, and a really strong Candidate would be forthcoming. The negotiations preliminary to the final selection .of a candidate were left in the hands of a special committee.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-03-06_WidnesExaminer_art0028" n="art0028">
        <head>IMMUNim.-__</head>
        <p>IMMUNim.-__</p>
        <p>BAD REATH.</p>
        <p>THE TONGUE IS THE INDEX</p>
        <p>Your Breath is Foul. You do not perceive it yourself. You are like a person who is always among the oils and varnishes—you get used to what is to many people a most obnoxious smell. It is most disagreeable to those with whom you cone in contact. A slight Headache, a )ad Breath, and a Coated Tongue ak the first symptoms of Stomachic Disease. Take time by the forelock and intercept its germination ; arrest the germs ere they develop Into disease. " I am delighted," writes Mr. V. Gordon, 77, Colwick Vale, Colwick, Notts, ," with the benefit I have...derived from Dr. Morse's Indian Root Pills and am perfectly satisfied with the result. They are a fine medicine, and I can confidently recommend them to anyone suffering from Constipation, Indigestion, and all stomach troubles. I have always advised my friends to take them, and the most gratifying results have been obtained." A Pill in time may save days of suffering. Look at your tongue in the looking-glass every night and morn!ng, and if it is at all discoloured, take 'a couple of Dr. Morse's Indian Root Pills. They will remove the objectionable matter from the stomach, regulate the system, and sweeten the breath. One or two of these Pills taken once or tVtice a week will ensure a well-regulated system, fortified against all disease. Dr. Morse's Indian Root Pills get at the cause by cleansing the stomach, purifying the blood, and aiding the digestion and assimilation of food, and are a positive and permanent cure for Indigestion, Biliousness, Constipation, Impure Blood, and Female Ailments. Sold by Chemists and Stores, price 1/1+ pet bottle, or The W. H. Comstock Co., Ltd, 21, Farringdon- Avenue, London, g.C.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-03-06_WidnesExaminer_art0029" n="art0029">
        <p>At the establishment of the Missions to Searn men in the Victoria Dock , Road, London, Where seamen ashore may comfortably bzrth ' and he well catered for, Mr. George Ball holds a position, and is well known there. His states- ment is therefore of special interest.</p>
        <p>ONCE A VICTIM OF ACUTE NERVOUS DISORDERS.</p>
        <p>" For many years," he said, "I was pale and exceedingly weak, ,and the butt of every illness that was going. In time my nervous system was completely disordered, and I avoided company; also I was easily excitable and unable to settle calmly to any occupation.</p>
        <p>"As time went on my nervous ccndition caused me considera, c anxiety. It seems foolish I will admit, but such was the. weal: state of my nerves then that I could not remain anywhere in the dark. If I awoke in the night my nerves became so agitated that I was unable 10,g:A a wink .of seep until morning. "1 consulted a Specialist, and he told me That I was suffering from Acute Neurasthenia and a %veal: heart. I followed his prescriptions for three or four months; but I sank to the Worst stages of Nervous Byeakdown. "Different remedies disoriganised my stomach that I could not digest a mouthful of food. I,Yhat little I ate would remain on my chest, lermenting and tearing me with pain. " These troubles caused mischief to my lungs, and I had many symptoms of Consumption. _ -</p>
        <p>- " Then at last to my dismay the doctor who attended me for some time said that the con-</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-03-06_WidnesExaminer_art0030" n="art0030">
        <head>Examiner Saturday, March 6. 1909.</head>
        <p>The Examiner Saturday, March 6. 1909.</p>
        <p>point—those of O'Neill and Bradshaw wire open to question. Thomas also hit the upright with a shot at goal. If the ball had gone over—as events turned out—Warrington would at least have drawn. There was no score till three-quarter time. Then Leeds got five points by a sudden break-away. War- rington were still game, and by stupendous efforts on the part of Taylor and the forwards they nearly pulled the match out of the fire. Taylor broke through the defence with a heroic effort, and if the forwards had had an atom of go9d fortpne to reward their labours they would have won the game rigkt on time. Fate and the Leeds foll back decreed otherwAe. There was only ,` inches " between victory and defeat, but even " inches " suffice. _ _ _</p>
        <p>There were certain conclusions quite unavoidable. Leeds were the spoiled children of Fortune on Satur- day. They caught Warrington disorganised through injured players and they had all the luck of the match in the bargain. On the day's play they hardly deserved to win, for Warrington had much the better of the game and with only one of the three absentees in the back division they would have gained the day. At full strength I feel sure the home side would have triumphed by something like 20 points. Taylor just lacked the support that either Fish, Dickenson and Brooks could have given him to turn the tables on the visitors.</p>
        <p>If the result of the Cup-Tie on Saturday had been different, Warrington's interest in the Competition might not haye extended very far. Judging from the draw Warrington would have been fated t,) go to Hunslet, which is the graveyard of the hopes of many visiting teams. Of course, Warrington would not have been without their backers at Hunslet, but personally I think they would merely have made their exit from the second round iosteai of the first. Meanwhile Leeds can view the prospeci of a bumper gate with their near rivals with some equanimity, for they will at least net some Kudos as solid compensation for a probable</p>
        <p>reverse.</p>
        <p>The result of the draw for the second round of the Cup took place at Manchester or Tuesday, and resulted as follows:</p>
        <p>Broughton Rangers v. Salford. Leigh v. Wakefield Trinity. Swinton or Rochdale Hornets v. Oldham. Halifax v. Beverley. Runcorn v. Hull. Hunslet v. Leeds. Huddersfield v. Barrow. Wigan v. Keighley. _ _ . _ _</p>
        <p>- Tho matches will be played on ground of the club the first-named on March 13.</p>
        <p>The Wigan Rug'ty Football Club have been successful in signing on T. McCabe, a member; of the Australian team, !and formerly connected with the Wigan and Widnes clubs. Warrington are in close touch with two prominent Australian backs, and I ant informed that there is a good prospect of their being induced to don the "primrose and 'glue." It is almost certain that the great Messenger will refuse all oilers and return to the warm land of the Kangaroo. In many respects it would be a shame to rob Australia of the greatest Rugby player she has ever produced.</p>
        <p>The Australia v. England match at Everton on Wednesday, resulted in another defeat for the Colonials by 14 points to 7. Messenger, Brook, and Dickenson wore prominent absentees from the game, which attracted about 5,000 spectators.</p>
        <p>To-day Warrington resume Lmtzue football, and With the smart of S tturday still on them they may possibly smite the Hornets of Rochdale. They usually win at the Athletic grounds, and it is in their favour that Rochdale had a severe cup struggle on Wednesday with Swinton. The Hornets are however a better side than they have been for some seasons.</p>
        <p>The Warrington executive have decided to drop Fish, end his place will he taken by Bradshaw. Team : Tilley: Bradshaw, Dickenson (cant.), Tre. hearne ; Brooks, Jenkins : Nill, Dawell, Thomas Shugars, Boardman, Belton. Reserve: Hoekenhull and Hewitt. The Warrington Combination team meet Rochdale A at Wilderspool with the following : Mintyre : Landor. Jordon, Cartwright. Hockenhull ; Lees, Daintith ; P. Boat damn, Naylor, Hewitt, Chesters and Harmer.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-03-06_WidnesExaminer_art0031" n="art0031">
        <head>NORTHERN UNION CUP.</head>
        <p>THE NORTHERN UNION CUP.</p>
        <p>FIRST ROUND.</p>
        <p>RESULTS OF SATURDAY'S MATCHES. .Leeds 1 1 _5 Warrington* 0 t. 3</p>
        <p>Hunslet* Keighley Broughton R. Wigan* Leigh* Huddersfield*</p>
        <p>Wakefield Trinity 2 3 13 Bradford* , 0 1 3 Runcorn* Hull</p>
        <p>Rochdale H.* 0 1 13 Swinton Halifax* Barrow* Oldham* Salford* Beverley* 2 1 7 Ebbw Vale</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-03-06_WidnesExaminer_art0032" n="art0032">
        <head>Warrington's ill-Luck. Leeds Snatch a Victory.</head>
        <p>Warrington's ill-Luck. Leeds Snatch a Victory.</p>
        <p>The result of the cup-tie at Warrington was a severe blow to the home club, who sustained an unexpected reverse by 5 points to 3at the hands of Leeds. War- rington were all along confidently expecting victory, until the morning of the match when it transpired that the three" star backs of the team would be unable to play owing to injury. The absentees were Fish, Dickenson, and Brooks, and their inability to play undoubtedly cost the side the snatch. Fish sprained his ankle while playing in a charity match last Thursday while Dickinson and Brooks were damaged at Halifax earlier in the week. Leeds were at full strength with the exception that Birch was ipjured, and Jarman appeared in the forwards in his place. It was a favour- able day for the match, and the attendance of 6,000 persons was quite satisfactory. The receipts amounted to £129. The substitutes on the Warrington side, were Lees and Bradshaw, threequarters, and Lloyd, half-back the teams turning nut as follows : Warrington—Tilley, back; 'rreharne, Lees, Taylor, and Bradshaw, three-quarter backs; Lloyd and Jenkins, half hacks ; Dowell, O'Neill, Shugars, Thomas, Belton, and Boardman, forwards. Leeds—Young, back; Desborough, Goldthorpe,Ware, and Fawcett, three-quarter backs ; Ward and Jones, half backs ; Jarman, Webster, Townend, Harrison, WhittaLer, and Hughes,forwards. Referee, Mr. H. Edmanson, Bradford. Leeds won the toss, and Warrington started the game, strong forward play being the feature of the opening stages. The Lancastrians were the first to defend, Jones's kicking being useful, and there was some fine combination between Ward, Jones Ware. Goldthorpe, and Desborough. The last-named player was brought down by the heels by Bradshaw just as he appeared a likely scorer. Young was then called upon to stop a strong rush of the Warrington forwards, and did so cleverly, changing the Leeds defence into attack by means of a huge kick. Warriugton now warmed to their work and raised the hopes of their supporters by some dashing forward play. Leeds were driven to the line and subjected to severe pressure. For twenty minutes there was only one side in it. O'Ne,ll got over from a forward pass, and was recalled. Then Thomas picked up at the back of the scrum and forced his way over, but this point was also negatived. Much of the passing of the backs revealed the weakness caused by the absence of the star players, but one round resulted in Taslor sending Bradshaw over at the corner, but this transfer was also ruled to be forward, and Leeds escaped luckily. Before Leeds could get clear, Thomas had a penalty shot at goal, and the ball struck one of ths uprights Bud rebounded. Treharne also made a great effort to get in on the left, but was tackled low by Fawcett a yard from the line. Just before the interval Jarman raised the siege by running to centre. Half- time, no score. The game re-opened quietly, the first interesting item being a threatening movement between Fawcett and Goldthorpe, but the latter spoiled the chance by passing forward. Lloyd and Jenkins then outwitted Ward and Jones, but were cleverly stopped by Ware, and Leeds had a long period of still defence, in which their tack- ling was distinctly good. Leeds got the lead twenty minutes front the re-start in an unexpected way. 'raylor tried to field a high pass, but only touched the ball, which dropped into the hands of Ware. The latter had a clear course and sprinted down to Tilley. The full back tackled his man gamely, but could not prevent the pass to Whittaker who ran under the posts unopposed. Young easily added a goal. For a time Warrington appeared disheartened, and some had blunders were made by the three quarters. Near the close however they made a desperate effort and six minutes from time Taylor get possession near centre. He ,an with the utmost determination and shaking off all tacklers be grounded at the corner amid a perfect roar of cheering Thomas tried hard to land a goal, but the angle was too much frr him. There, was still a chance, and Leeds were dearly beaten. The home forwards worked like demons and rashed the ball right to the line. A try seemed almost certain, when Young dived among the oncoming feet and saved pluckily. A huge punt cleared Warrington out and Leeds got to the other end just as the final came leav- ing th nu lucky winners bp the narrow margin of a goal and a try (5 points). to Warrington one try (3 points).</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-03-06_WidnesExaminer_art0033" n="art0033">
        <head>WHY WARRINGTON LOST.</head>
        <p>WHY WARRINGTON LOST.</p>
        <p>[By °vast.]</p>
        <p>The casual visitor to Warrington on Saturday evening would have thought he had stumbled upon a town in mourning. A greater collection of sad and dispirited looking townsmen it would have been difficult to find anywhere. Smiles were as rare as roses in March. The cause was not difficult to find, for Warrington's defeat in the first round of the Cup-ties on their own ground created a shock which the sport-loving public could not recover from immediately. If anything had seemed certain when the cup draw came out, it was that War- rington would easily pass into the second round at the expense of Leeds—A team not quite in the same class on the season's play. But Leeds won. Why? This question is also easy to answer. Warrington turned out minus their three best backs, and were so disorganised that the visitors were abla to snatch a victory by five points to three. Could Fate have dealt a worse blow than to deprive Warrington of the services of men like Fish, Dickenson and Brooks on the morning of the match? Hardly. They had all been selected to play, and at the laxt minute were found unfit. Never in the history of the Club has the team been so hard hit on the eve of a match of such crucial moment. Hope almost fled the hearts of their supporters when he news became known. Nlany ant dee? were the revilings against Fate. But was Fate really to blame? On the answer to this question hangs the real cause of Warrington's sad downfall.</p>
        <p>I say unhesitatingly that Fate, Bad Luck and Co., were not really to blame in this instance. There was over confidence on Warrington's part—on the Club's part—in the ability to beat L3eds anyhow, or the full cup team would never have been endangered in the match at Halifax on Tuesday. It can safely be affirmed that had the final tie been in view,several crack players would not have donned the jersey at Halifax. The playing of the full team there was blunder, No. 1, and Fate was innocent. The result was that Dickenson and Brooks both sustained injuries which rendered them unfit for Saturday's cup-tie. Blunder No. 2 happened on Thursday, when one or two of Warrington's Cup team turned out in a charity match against Karno's comedians. It is said that they 'were forbidden to do so. The fact remains that Fish played and got lamed for his folly. There is no ques- tion that he personally deserves the severest censure. What the justly incensed supporters of the club are asking is: Why was Fish not prevented from playing by those in authority? Perhaps it is an unfair ques- tion, but it certainly requires an answer. Blunder No. 3 was suspected before the match; and quite obvious afterwards. Lees ought never to have been chosen to play centre. He is not a three-quarter, and he is a player who ispast his best. There was no justification for playing him anywhere but at half when such men as Jordan and Cartwright were avail- able for Dickenson's piece. Lees was .the.failure of the afternoon, and plenty of people ovenly expressed their fear that he would be. Unkind things are now being said about "cup-tie experiments." Memory brings to mind the recollection that on the last occasion when Warrington fell in the first round, the experiment of playing a half at three-quarter was tried, and failed. It is not a pleasing coincidence that Lloyd was in both these unlucky teams as a substitute. On Saturday, however, he did all that could be expected of him.</p>
        <p>Having discussed the causes which led up to such a weak team doing duty, we can turn to the match itself. Here there is no doubt Warrington were unlucky. Disorganised as the backs were, the side would have won with the smallest slice of luck. The forwards realised their responsibility and played such superb football that Leeds were impotent for more than half the game. Three times was the ball carried over the Leeds line before the interval, and three times was the referee's verdict against the claim for a try. Per- sonally, I thought that Thomas scored a legitimate</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-03-06_WidnesExaminer_art0034" n="art0034">
        <head>Leeds Grateful.</head>
        <p>Leeds Grateful.</p>
        <p>Mr. J. Goldthorpe, the secretary of the Leeds F.C., has addressed the following letter to Mr. Thomas Pemberton (treasurer of the W.F.C.) : The Pavilion, Headingley, March 2ad.—Dear sir,—We aro highly sat;sfied with all your gate arrangements, and every- thing is in order. Permit :no to thank you on behalf of our Committee and myself for your kindness and courtesy to us on Saturday last. It may interest you to know that our spectators thought yours WAS the fairest crowd that they had ever had the pleasure of mixing with.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-03-06_WidnesExaminer_art0035" n="art0035">
        <head>WHAT MEMBERS OF THE CLUE THINK.</head>
        <p>WHAT MEMBERS OF THE CLUE THINK.</p>
        <p>Dear" Oyalist,"—in the excitement of publishing the first issue of your new edition, please do not forget to reserve space to tell the Committee of the Warrington Football Club what the members think of them. You, doubtless, will have heard some hard things said about them and perhaps they will realize their position when put into cold print. I wonld suggest an extraordinary meeting be held to discover who was responsible for the selection of Lees as a centre three-qnarter. With all duo respect to that player,he is not a three-quarter. Way were Jordan and Landor overlooked in preference to him? Es it because they are locals? Toe members would perhaps have some awkward questions to ask with regard to these players were a meeting called. Ii is freely rumoured that Landor stood out some time ago for better pay and that is the reason he does not get selected for the Ist team. 'When we remember the ‘Vigan match st home this season, how he marked Leytham and was absolutely the best man on our side, one thinks there if evidently some motive in keeping him off the team. We all thought that after the Batley match a few seasons ago the Committee (4 of whom still hold that position), would have been taught a lesson. This, apparently, is not the ease and it rests with the members to try to cure them of this experimenting in Cup-ties. I, for one, shall not vote for one of the present committee at the next meeting and if all the members were of the same mind, we should have an entirely new committee,rnen of influence in the town—not consisting of any old players who played for nothing and exrct everyone else to do likewise in these days. It was whispered that Fish had got an engagement with Karnos during the week—if this is correct I would suggest the committee man who proposed Lees as centre should go with him. Wishing you success with your new issue and anxiously awaiting Saturday's edition.—Yours truly,</p>
        <p>FED UP.</p>
        <p>Dear" Ovalist,"—What are we to think of the Com- mittee of the Warrington Football Club, after the selection of the three-qnarter line to oppose Leeds last Saturday? I myself say that it was not fair to Sammy Lees, to the rest of the team, nor to the spectators, and also that it was an injustice to our "A" team • centre three-quarters. No doubt Lees did his best. He is not to blame—those of the conindttee that selected him are the ones to be blamed. They will no doubt say it would have been alright had we won. Nothing of the kind. It was h risky thing to do in any case. But for the great game our forwards played, we should have been humiliated on our own ground. I do not pnt all the blame on Lees. Our half-backs played wretchedly, but it is a fact that with a capable centre to have played with Taylor the match %mild have been won with the chances they got. We have an "A" team second to none, yet from the Committee's point of view we have not a centre three-quarter to be trusted to plsy with the Ist team. I should like to know what the "A " team is for? Why could not. Jordan be played with Bradshaw and Trehearne kept in his own place with Taylor? Going back a few seasons it similar case occurred at. Batley, also I think in the first round, only that a half-back was played wing three-quarter. This lost us the match. A certain committee man got all the blame and at the Feneral meeting was dropped. Therefore to be consistent it behoves the members to do the same with those responsible in this case. There is another little matter, Mr. " Ovalist." After a gruelling match at Oldham it seemed a great mistake to take a full team to Halifax (with the exception of an injured player). The way both Brooks and Dickenson have been played this season (and a Cup-Tie in the same week) ought to have shown the committee they wanted resting. Would any other club have played two such men four days before an important Cup-Tie? If this is a sample of our Com- mittee having done their best then it is time someone else tried to do better. Thanking you in anticipation of your inserting this and apologising for taking so much valuable space.—Yours truly,</p>
        <p>A MEMBER.</p>
        <p>P.S.—Last Saturday's team selection makes it appear that the Committee transferred Dan Isherwood know- ing at the time we had no other centre to fall back on, whereas with a little tact and sinking of differences be would still be wearing the primrose and blue.</p>
        <p>Dear " Ovalist,"—Will you please find me space for a few lines re Saturday's Cup Tie? Firstly I should like to know how the Committee came to select a half- back fo4 a centre three-quarter position for I think a greater mistake could not be made than the selection of last Saturday. With all due respect to Lees, Le is not at all suitable for a three-quarter back, being short of speed. The blame for the loss of the match in my opinion rests entirely with the Committee, for they had several juniors more suitable to call upon for the position.</p>
        <p>Secondly how is it that players take part in a comic match a few days before a very important Cup-Tie? If the players is that match took the law into their own hands and played without first getting the sanction of the Committee they ought to be made to suffer for the same, and I for one ask the Committee to deal with these players aocordiugly.—Yours truly WIREPULLER.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-03-06_WidnesExaminer_art0036" n="art0036">
