      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-03-06_WidnesExaminer_art0037" n="art0037">
        <head>WARRINGTON BOARD OF GUARDIANS.</head>
        <p>WARRINGTON BOARD OF GUARDIANS.</p>
        <p>The Feeding of School Children.</p>
        <p>The fortnightly meeting of the Warrington Board of Guardians was held at the Workhouse on Wednesday, the Rev. T. R. Matthews presiding. There were also present :—Mrs. Simpson, Mrs. Collingwood, Miss Broadbent. Rev. M. Ryan, Messrs. G. Mather, W. Furness, W. Gill, A. J. Jolley, W. C. Brooks, M. W. Seton, J. Backhouse, R. Forshaw, A. Brooks, P. Longton, A. Bottomley (clerk), R. W. Peacock, (assistant clerk), and J. F. Awbery (master). DR. CORKER'S SALARY.</p>
        <p>The Finance Committee considered an application from Dr. Corker, resident assistant medical officer. for an increase of salary. By three votes to two they decided_to grant an advance from £lOO to £l2O.</p>
        <p>Mr. JoLLEV, in moving the adoption of the minutes, said that personally he regretted that the amount suggested was not larger. lie had no hesitation in saying that since the appointment of Dr. Hodgkinson as medical officer and Dr. Corker as assistant, the work of the department hed bean better done than ever it had been in the history or the place. He was under the impression, which was shared by some members of the Board, that when Dr. Corker applied for an increase five or six months ago the feeling of the Board was that, while several thought the application was a little early, Dr. Corker's splendid services should receive something like adequate recognition. The suggested figure certainly erred on thaside of modesty. Mr. &amp;Tom seconded. Mr. FURNESS moved that the recommendation be referred back to the committee. It seemed to be almost accepted as a condition that an officer should come there for a small salary, acquire his experience at the expense of the Board, and, at the end of twelve months, be allowed to take his departure. That seemed, on the face of it, to be unbusinesslike, and, as time went on, such a policy would involve large expense on the rates. He suggested that the Finance Committee should grant Dr. Corker an increase of £25 on the enoerstandine that that was to be the maximum. Repeated applications would thus be obviated.</p>
        <p>Mr. GILL seconded. Mr. MATHER said he supported the amendment, but not from the same point of view as Mr. Furness. Mr. JOLT.Ev accepted the amendment and the matter was therefore referred back.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-03-06_WidnesExaminer_art0038" n="art0038">
        <head>RATES</head>
        <p>THE RATES</p>
        <p>The Finance Committe bad before them the Clerk's estimate of expenditure for the half-year ending Nlichmlmas, 1909. It was resolved 'that a call for £15,043 be levied upon the overseers. In-maintenance at the Workhouse is estimated to cost £3,500, as against £3,333 19s. 4d. for the corresponding half-year of 1908 and out relief (including non-resident poor) £3,000 as against £2,877 17s. Gd. Mr. JOLLEY, in moving the approval of the minutes, said the Finance Committee were perfectly satisfied tint it would not be safe to make any lower call than that suggested. Looking ahead for the next twelve months, it would be extremely unwise to anticipate any smaller call for common fund purposes than an equiva- lent to 7d, in the £ for the whole of the year. Mr. FURNESS said it was a matter of congratulation to the ratepayers that in face of the large expenditure which the Board had had to incur in out-relief in the last two or three months, they had been able to keep the rate at the present figure. The ratepayeys ought to ba thankful, far had it not been for the great care exercise 1 by the Guardians he was afraid the rates might have been higher. _</p>
        <p>!qr. MATHER. : Wh,re does the care come in?</p>
        <p>Mr. FURNESS : That is a little conceit we have. (Laughter.) We take the credit for it. Cootirming,Nfr. Farness said the Clerk had materially helped the Board in his efforts to keep d,):vn the rates and was entitled to praise for the economical way in which he had dis- charged his duties. " The estimates were approved. THE FEEDING OF SCHOOL CHILDREN.</p>
        <p>The CLERK red the following letter from Mr. J. Moore Murray, director of education:—"Nly committee have been considering the cases of children who have been reported to be unable by reason of lack of food to take full advantage of the education provided for them. In the course of their inquiries they have. fouud number of children whosa parents are in receipt of relief from your Board. I am directed to send you a list of these cases and to nsk your Board if they have any suggestions to make in order that these children may be placed in a position to take full advantage of the education provided for them." The Clerk informed the Board that he sent the return to the Mieving officers. Mr. Ankland had brought his cases before the Relief Committee that morning and Mr. Aultllnd would bring his next Wednesday. Mr. A. BROOKES said Mr. Aukland had brought the more flagrant cases before the committee. .The women were chiefly widows and appeared most respectable, and the committee felt sure they would not send their children to school under-fed. They would go without themselves. However, they were all in very buxom condition, in fact he did not think they would meet nine or ten more' buxom women in Warrington than those who had beens brought. before the committee as having sent under-fed children to school. He did not wish to make any insinuations but he thought the Education Committee bmight be a little more careful before they brought a charge of that kind against such women as he had mentioned. 'the relief committee felt satiStled that the children were fed. They might be fed plainly, but what they had was sufficient and nutritious.</p>
        <p>The CLERK said a letter had been received with reference to one case brought before the committee, the me her being most indignant that it should be reported that her children were not well fed. '.Jr. JOLLEY said that without expressing any opinion on what had heen said, he would like to ask what proportion the ten cases which had been brought before the committee bore to the total number of cases inquired into by Mr. Auk land. The CLERK said Mr. Ank land had only thirteen cases and one or two of them referred to temporary relief only, so that he had really brought all his cases befor e the committee. Mr. Jor.Litit said he took it that what Mr. Brooks called attention to was that there had not been a single case of those referred to Hr. Aukland which ought to have been reported in that way. That was exactly what he (Mr. Jolley) anticipated would happen, because be was a member of the Sub-committee of the Education Conimitte&amp; which had to deal with the matter, and he felt very strongly that no action should be taken with any case on the list until the whole of them had been remitted to the Guardians for proper inquiry by reliev- ing officers who were trained experts in investigating cases of that kind. After all, the investigations made on behalf of the Education Committee were compara- tively amateur, and it only showed—and he hoped it would be realized—xhat a grave danger there was of quite unnecessary expenditure, not merely in Warring- ton but in other places, by people who were influenced by sentiment rushing into heavy exp•nditure which etre and investigation would prove to be quite unnecessary. It also involved a reflection in many cases on the parents—a reflection which there was DO need to make. He considered it would be desirable when the whole of the cases had been before the Committee, for a letter to be sent to the Education Committee embodying the general opinion of the Guardians on the whole question.</p>
      </div>
