      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-03-06_WidnesExaminer_art0048" n="art0048">
        <p>We are making our OUR NEW bow to-day—in fact our ISSUE. second bow. The first took place forty years ago all but a few weeks, and during the intervening period a certain stiffness has crept over us, which, fortunately, we realised in time to adopt methods of rejuvenation. It is, therefore, with unabated confidence and a sprightly feeling of youth and vigour that we again make courteous overtures to the public, well assured that we shall meet with the same warmth of welcome that was our happy lot in the earliest days. Forty years is a big slice out of the life of a newspaper as well as a man, butt . the newspaper has the inestimable advantages of being untrammelled by nature's " allotted span," and possessing also the secret of renewing its youth. We feel that our own revivification is complete: new blood courses through our veins, and we are garbed in the fashion of to-day. Within us are fresh sources of inspiration, of adventure, and of service in the interests of an ever-growing community. We have always had ideals, and the lapse of forty years has not tarnished !:them one iota. There are still wrongs to be righted, reforms to advocate, and a gleam of undiminished brightness to follow. Looking backward we cannot help being struck with the similarity of aspiration and aim in thel days of the first "Examiner," and now. Our leading article on April 24th, 1869 expressed much that will bear quotation and apply with force to-day. Several paragraphs might* be written in the present issue for they have lost none of their pertinence. "The two great social evils of the country we hold to be ignorance and drunkenness. We hope to see some well-devised scheme of national education . . . We believe that public opinion is ripe for a great change in respect to the traffic in intoxicating drink. Everybody condemns the present licensing system and is prepared to support limitations, real or pre- tended. We shall advocate restrictions, as rigid as an intelligent public opinion will sanction, in a trade which is the cause of nearly all .the misery, poverty, and crime that afflicts the country." It is just a little discouraging to realize that we are still fighting the three battles —Education, Temperance and Disestablish- ment—in which the " Examiner " first lifted its voice four decades ago. One crumb of comfort comes to us, however, as we read that "Mr. Rylands on Wednesday voted for the second reading of the Bill for legalizing marriage with a deceased wife's sister." We have moved at least one step forward. The world was noisy then with the din o angry battle. Mr. Gladstone was suffering the wrath of ecclesiastics in fullest measure, and was being pelted with epithets which are perhaps better left unrecalled.lt is the peculiar venom of these personal attacks which drew from the Editor the dignified protest with which his first leader came to a close.</p>
        <p>" We have no private antipathy to gratify, no ancient grudge to feed, and we expect therefore to keep our leading columns free from personali- ties. We shall be glad to open our pages to fair and intelligent discussion of any question of public interest, but we shall not allow the paper to be the vehicle of false and slanderous attacks. upon political opponents - even if the calumnies are paid for as advertisements Still, however, it is possible that in denouncing evil or exposing error we may wound the susceptibilities of those who profit by the evil or who are prejudiced in favour of the evil. This we cannot help and we are not able to say that we shall regret it. We intend to F-peak the truth as we believe and understand it. We hope to obtain the support of the public but we shall not conceal or disguise a single con- viction to gain it. This is true liberty, when free-born men, Having to ad vise the public may speak free, • Which he who can, and will, deserves high praise; . Who neither can, nor will, may hold his peace."</p>
        <p>Mr. A. H. Crosfield, M.P.. spoke on the second reading of the Daylight Saving Bill in the House of Commons yesterday after- noon and heartily supported the measure. He said" he thought the arguments in favour of the Bill were overwhelming and believed it would be of immense advantage to the large majority of the people to alter the present system." In a telegram late last evening the Borough Member sent us word that the second reading had been carried', but the Bill was inevitably referred again for consideration by Special Committee. We regret that the full text of Mr. Crosfield's speech did not reach us till after we had gone to press.</p>
        <p>THE DAYLIGHT SAVING BILL.</p>
        <p>The Parks Committoo BOWLING GREENS appears to be taking COMING—PERHAPS such precautions in</p>
        <p>regard to the scheme for making four additional public bowling greens that there is LI possibility of another year being wasted before the greens materialise. The latest step is in the form of a report from The Borough Surveyor to the Committee, in which he estimates the four greens to cost £520 each, exclusive of land, on the model of the one at Victoria Park, with an additional charge for mainten- ance of £36 each over and above receipts. It has taken the Committee a long time to travel this far, and we hope they will not be dissuaded from going further at a smart pace by• the cost. There is no clear indication that the report is being acted upon. The Council has authorised the expenditure and has shown no unwill- ingness to bear the burden, therefore the Committee can go ahead light-heartedly if the chairman will let them. Alderman</p>
        <p>For Cbildren's tacking Cough at night, Wood's Great Peppermint Cure. le. 1. 28. 9d.—Advt.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-03-06_WidnesExaminer_art0049" n="art0049">
        <p>Tao rizzamizior, Satzzzacas G, I 000_</p>
        <p>Bolton has however an amazing aptitude for smiling a baleful smile on these recrea- tive luxuries for the workingman that causes them to fade away in their infancy. He cannot any longer contend that grass will not grow in Warrington—and this was his chief objection ,when public bowling greens were first sanctioned in the Council Chamber. The grass will grow and the bowlers will bowl if given reasonable oppor- tunity. We,-should like to see the Committee atone for one lost year by getting the scheme through in the coming summer. Another monthly report or two will render the feat impossible.</p>
        <p>Our congratulations to A WELL-DESERVED Mr. John Upson on his HONOUR. election to the presi- dency of the Lancashire Association of the N.U.T. The honour is fully deserved for no-one has worked harder for the teachers in this district or is more competent to voice the claims and aspirations of that important pro- fession. Mr. Upson's presidential address dealt in a thoughtful and candid way with some of the questions which are agitating the educational world to-day. Chief among these is the overstocking of the market and the president rightly condemned the incon- siderate policy which lures young people into the profession with the unpleasant pros- pect that after completing their college training they may have to join the ranks of the unemployed or take up some occupation quite alien to that for which they have, by dint of years of study, pre- pared themselves. We have every sympathy with Mr. 'Upson's suggestion that the candidates' examination should be conducted on the• same lines as that for entrance to the Civil Service. It would not remove the present difficulty, but it would certainly tend to obviate such chaotic con- ditions in the future.</p>
        <p>It is a peculiarity of AN £BOO Town Council meetings BLUNDER. that the most important item often draws the least comment. On Tuesday, a most serious matter, involving the ratepayers in a loss of over £BOO, would apparently have been passed over without explanation but for the timely protest of Councillor Hawthorn, who dragged it from the depths of the "private agenda "—a modern innovation which may answer a bad purpose as well as a good one. From the facts briefly outlined</p>
        <p>t seems that in 1893 the Council -decided to increase their charge against the Newton Urban Council for the treatment of fever patients in the Warrington hospital, from 10s. to 15s. per head. It has just been discovered that the new regulation was never put in force, with the result that the town has suffered a loss of over .EBOO in the sixteen years. The Newton authorities are, of course, this much to the good, and likely to remain so, seeing that the blame is not their's. It is probably beyond the bounds of possibility to place the blame on the right shoulders at this late date, but the incident is another indication of that lax administration of corporate affairs which was the subject of so much severe criticism a' year or two ago. Errors will always creep in, but we hope the danger of such a serious blunder as this under notice has been eliminated, under the new regime.</p>
        <p>Mr. Lloyd-George OLD-AGE PENSIONS, justifies the excess of expenditure on Old-Age Pensions over the estimate made when the Government introduced its scheme in a single sentence. "It has cost," he told the House of Commons the other day, "more than we anticipated; but the greatness of the cost shows the depth of the need." We doubt whether any person who has any knowledge of the lives of the poor needed the assurance that the Old-Age Pensions Act must prove a great boon. The Chancellor of the Exchequer told in moving accents of the sadness of the cases which had come under the notice of the authorities since the Act had been in operation—of men and women more than the three score years and ten of the .Scriptural span striviAg and struggling along with the merest pittance, yet hiding their poverty and too proud to seek the shelter of "The House." These are no •new phenomena. Cases of this kind abound in every town in the kingdom, und workers among the back- ways of ' the great cities can tell stories that for sheer heroism put the gallantry of the battlefield to shame. It is astonish- ing that people should have been able to maintain the struggle, and not only that, but t o do jt without sacrificing their own self-respect/or transgressing a code of honour as high as any in history. With such cases before us, it is impossible not to feel that expenditure which will bring succour to such cases as these, that will relieve hunger and want and spare old folks the dread uncertainty of helpless age, requires no apology. The Government has had to ask for nearly a million more than it was esti- mated would be required for the Act in this quarter • but there are few people who will grudge their contribution to such a cause as that which the Government has taken up.</p>
        <p>The attempt of Lord GERMAN MIRTH AT Roberts to arouse 'fears OUR INVASION of a German invasion SCARE. of England, has awakened sever'al echoes in the Fatherland,none of them in any sense flattering to his judgment. The most practical and characteristically , German of political parties in the Reichstag is undoubtedly the National Liberal Party, whose most eloquent spokesman is Mr. Ernest Basserman. In a recent debate Mr. Basserman gave an answer to Lord Roberts forebodings and heaped upon him some scorn- ful retorts. The idea of such an invasion he described as "a delirious dream" and declared that such a concentration of troops at a German harbour "would be known in England the following day." For the trans- port of such a force 180 large steamers would be required and he reminded Lord Roberts' that Germany does not possess that num- ber of adequate vessels." After touching the moral side of the question he summed up in the words:— " How silly Lord Roberts must consider the responsible authorities of the German Empire. He does not credit them with understanding that even if such transports existed they would soon be lying at the bottom of the German ocean under the guns of the British fleet. If England wishes to introduce a general territorial army, I think she is to be congratulated. We have no right to criticise her, any more than she has to make remarks upon our army or navy. The strength or weakness of the British fleet is no business of ours. Germany is as little able to make an • arrangeinent with England concerning the development of her navy as a boy is able to arrange with a man as to their rate of growth and stature."</p>
        <p>The comments of the German papers upon this pronouncement make interesting read- ing. The Frankfurter Zeitung, the Hamburger Nachriehten and Vorwaerts agree that Mr Basserman has expressed the opinion of</p>
        <p>every sober-minded German. The leading Socialist paper says : "The German people have spoken through the tongue of Mr. Basserman. The German people loathe and abominate war. We want no naval enlargement. We desire to lift the taxes from the shoulders of our labouring and agricultual popula- tion. Let England's navy and army grow: it is , really no business of ours.',</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-03-06_WidnesExaminer_art0050" n="art0050">
        <p>LINSEED COMPOUND' for Coughs &amp;Colds. Relieve time and difficult breathing. 90., is.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-03-06_WidnesExaminer_art0051" n="art0051">
        <head>NOTES AND NOTIONS.</head>
        <p>NOTES AND NOTIONS.</p>
        <p>Councillor W. B. Roberts set his fellow- brewers an excellent example on Sunday afternoon by appearing on the platform at Colonel Whatmore's Salvation Army lecture on " Drink and its Evils." *</p>
        <p>Councillor Lord said on Tuesday that the electric lighting of the town was scandalous, and that we had far better revert to modern and economical gas lighting. The members of the Gas Committee chorused "hear, hear," and the members of the Electric Lighting Committee looked slightly supercilious. Has the future a mild revolution in store, we wonder? * *</p>
        <p>We hope the countless passers-by have noticed that our austere friend, Oliver Cromwell, has had his exterior renovated. The Guardian of Bridge Foot does duty un- complainingly in all weathers like one of his own Ironsides, and the least we can do is to provide him periodically with a new coat— even if it is only paint. * * *</p>
        <p>A Council meeting rarely passes without a joke being perpetrated. On Tuesday it was explained that an "odd man" had been doing some painting at the Police Station,and thereby raised the ire of the trades unionists. Councillor Wilkinson asked: "Was he an oddfellow ?" Councillor Pemberton replied promptly, and in the proper vein, that he was a Buffalo."—" Loud laughter," as the press reports say! * *</p>
        <p>Neither Mr. B. S. Townroe nor Mr. H. Gray has settled the Compulsory Service question quite to his liking. Mr. Townroe will "sum up" next week and Mr. Gray utter his "last word" the week following. Mean- while the Cat (the public) seems disinclined to jump. Her quiescent purr indicates a supreme indifference to the danger (?) of "The Englishman's Home" being wrecked by the foreign invader. * * *</p>
        <p>Ratepayers will soon be getting restive. On the top of the £l,OOO which the ill-advised Tory election petition will cost the town, comes the news that an error in the hospitals department has resulted in a loss of over £BOO. The only redeeming feature of the latter is that it is already covered by being spread over the past 16 years. But the fact remains that the town would have been £l,Boo—almost a 2d. rate—better off but for blunders which were avoidable.</p>
        <p>* * Our thanks are due to Mr. W. P. Rylands for giving us such a good advertisement on the eve of our ne-w issue. He devoted a whole speech at the Conservative meeting in Latchford on Thursday to the perfidiou§ "Radical organ," which, like a certain pro- prietary medicine, appears to have touched the spot. We are glad Mr. Rylands finds there are "lessons to be learned " from the petition. It has been our humble endeavour to teach the Party of which he is chairman a simple lesson of" political economy," based on the £l,OOO petition. There are gratifying evidences that it has been learnt. * * *</p>
        <p>Mr. G. C. Rees, the rising young barrister who did such good work as junior to Mr. William Madden in the recent election petition proceedings at Warrington, has been spoken of as the prospective Liberal candi- date for the West Derby Division of Liver- pool in Opposition to Mr. W. Watson Rutherford, M.P. There is no confirmation of the report which is evidently based on Mr. Rees' activity in the progressive cause in the Liverpool district. He has been giving about three Liberal addresses weekly throughout the winter, and in the course of his political work he has received and declined several invitations from outside Liverpool to become a candidate.</p>
        <p>- A curious position h*as recently arisen in connection with the contract for the painting of the Museum, and the incidental matters relating thereto have caused much discussion in the town. The Museum Committee appears to have considered the painting question from the msthetic point of view, and generously allotted the contract to a local decorator whose price was £45 more than that of another local firm equally well known. As far as could be learnt from the discussion at the Town Council the only reason for this action was that the Committee thought that A would do the work better than B. This hardly seems sufficient ground for accepting a price nearly one-third more than that of the other contractor in the running,and the General Purposes Committee seems to have been of the same opinion, for they negatived the Museum Committee's resolution and accepted the lowest tender. The inside painting of the Museum will therefore cost £lO4 instead of £l49—quite a considerable saving. *l*</p>
        <p>Councillor Pemberton had two special resolutions on the agenda for Tuesday's Council meeting which were decidedly interesting, if not of crucial importance. The first asked for printed slips to be inserted in the Corporation Year Book showing the salaries of each official and their respective staffs. This struck us as being a very proper request, and it was complied with to the extent that the information is to be given to each member of the Council. Until this year—or at any quite recently—these particulars res- pecting Corporation salaries were always printed in the Year-book, but an apparent excess of modesty on the part of the officials concerned seenis to have resulted in the information being deleted, from the current Year-book. We do not consider that the concession (?) following upon the resolution goes far enough. Each and every year- book issued should contain these important details, and moreover the public ought to have an opportunity of purchasing a copy of the Corporation Year-book if they desire to have one. The public pay the salaries and they have a right to know how much they are 'paying, and to whom and for what services the money goes. *_* •</p>
        <p>The second resolution in Councillor Pemberton's name proposed to re-instate the old standing order, fixing a certain month for the annual revision of Corporation salaries. Formerly it used to be February, and no increase could be considered except at the February meeting of the Council. Councillor Pemberton wishes " October " to be sub- stituted for "February," apparently in order that those Councillors who have the prospect of a November election before them may be duly impressed with the necessity for care in granting increases of salaries. His expressed idea was that the retiring Councillors would from their experience, be better able to judge the merits of each particular increase better</p>
        <p>RUNCORN AND WIDNES. RUNCORN BOARD OF GUARDIANS. The fortnightly meeting of this Board was held in the Dutton Workhouse on Tuesday. Mr. T. Baxter was in the chair. There was also present: Messrs. J. Spruce, S. Wright, P. Booth, P. Collins, A. Raining, W. M. Morris, C. Roylance, J. Hallows, J. Bolshaw, W. Harri- son, J. H. Davies, T. Taylor, A. R. Norman, E. Egerton Warburton, S. 4% oodward, A, Broome, T. Antwis, G. Pilling, J. L. Jones, C. E. Linaser, jun., G. F. Ashton (clerk), W. E. Hough (deputy cleik), T. Cooke, J. Pear- son and W. R. de Prez (relieving officers), T. D. Rolliuson (muter).</p>
        <p>INFANT LIFE PROTECTION. A letter was read from the State Children's Association with reference to the Infant Life Protection Act as amended in the Children's Act 1908, suggesting that women should be appointed as infant protection visitors. The Clerk stated he wonld like a committee appointed in order to consider the above. This course was adopted.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-03-06_WidnesExaminer_art0052" n="art0052">
        <head>BOYS' CARETAKER.</head>
        <p>THE BOYS' CARETAKER.</p>
        <p>Mr. Spruce stated th tt at the last meeting it was agreed to raise the salary of Mr. Boyce from £3O to £35. It would be remembered that Mr. Boyce applied for a testimonial from the House Committee because be was going to secure a situation nearer his home, and as an inducement for him to stay at Dutton it was decided to offer him 'a rise in salary of £5. It. had been stated by that Board at the last meeting that Mr. Boyce was a very efficient officer and they were desirous of keeping him if possible. He (Mr. Spruce) had interviewed Mr. Boyce, who after consideration had accepted the proposi, ion and .vould continue an officer at the House Mr, Morris seconded and the minutes were approved</p>
        <p>Silver-street Girls Club.</p>
        <p>On Wednesday evening there was a meeting at the above club of the " Y ' branch N.B.W.T.A. Mr. W. Beswick presided.—Councillor Hawthorn read several short stories, which were greatly enjoyed by all present. Songs were given by Miss Lloyd Jones, Lily Flinn and Alice Greghan. Masonic Installation. The Mayor (Alderman W. J. Porshaw, J.P.) was installed Worshipful Master of the Masonic Lodge of Charity on Thursday afternoon, at the Masonic Hall. To celebrate the event a banquet was held at the Lion Hotel in the evening, the Mayor presiding. Hospital Sunday.</p>
        <p>Su-nday, the MI; inst., will be observed as Hospital Sunday at the various places of worship in Warring- ton and district, and collections will be made on behalf of the Warrington Infirmary. Help is urgently seeded, for the institution has an income of only £9OO, and for the rest depends entirely upon voltuttary contributions to meet an expenditure of nearly £5,000 a year.</p>
        <p>ANNUAL SALE of Tea Services, Dinner Services, etc., great bargains.—J. S. GARLAND, 51, Bridge- street, Warrington.—Advt.</p>
        <p>Bewsei-road -Juvenile Choir.</p>
        <p>To c-elebrate the victory in connection with the Warrington Musical Festival, held a few weeks ago, the members of the Bewsey-road Juvenile Wesleyan Choir and friends took part in a social in the Lecture Liall adjoining the Church. The evening was enjoy- ably spent, and proved to be a source of delight to the little ones. Church Society_Addresses. _ _ _</p>
        <p>The series of Lenten addresses arranged by the W.rrington Church Society was continued at the Parish Church yesterday, when the Rev, John Wake- ford, Viciir of St. Margaret's, Anlield,.was the preacher. In the afternoon he addressed a large congregation of women on "Home and Heaven," and in the evening, when the service was ior men, his subject was "God's Workshop." Next Friday the preachers will be : Afternoon at 3 (women), Rev. W. E. Sims, Vicar of Aigburth, Liverpool; evening at 8 (men), Rev. J. G. McCormick, of London.</p>
        <p>PUBLIC nExErrr.—UNDXR THE CLOCK FOR. BEST BOOTS IN LANCASIIIRE.—Advt. Buckley-street Band of Hope. A concert was given in aid of the above on Monday night, by the Warrington good Templars. Mr. J. Richardson was the Chairman, and Mr. Roberts proved himself an able speaker. The soloists were the Misses Berney and Talbot. Two humorous songs were given by Mr. W. Shelley, in which he excelled. Mr. R. E. Lee very effectively rendered two piano- forte solos for which he was warmly applauded. Mr. J. Lloyd contributed a violin solo. Mr. Sheath gave a few gramophone selections, and the meeting was brought to a close by singidg " God be with you till we meet again." Popular Penny Concerts.</p>
        <p>A large atte-ndance and an excellent programme were again the outstanding features of the popular concert held in the Bold-street Wesleyan schoolroom; on Saturday evening. The Rev. T. Miller presided The artistes were the Misses E. Barnes and May Isherwood, vocalists ; Miss Moston, elocutionist. Messrs: Wood and Davies, prize medallists, of Wigan; humorist, Mr. David Sutton. One of the most. pleasing items of the programme was the concertina playing by Mr. Davies, who was very warmly com- plimented by the Chairman.</p>
        <p>UNSHED COMPOUND with warm water is au cxeellent gargle for sore throat.—Advt. •</p>
        <p>Home foi Waits and Strays.</p>
        <p>The Church of England Waifs and Strays Society have reeently removed their Warrington home from the premises adjoining St. George's Mission, Brick Street, to Morningside, Salisbury Street, formerly the residence of Alderman Bennett. The house has beea fitted up for the accommodation of 17 boys and the arrangements were much admired by the company who assembled on Thursday when the premises were formally dedicated to their new purpose. After a short religious service, Mr. Killey, district secretary of the Society, declared the home open, and an address having been delivered by the Rector (Rev. Canon Willis), Mr. Kiley was cordially thanked for the interest which he is taking in the institution on the motion of the Rev. G. B. Thurston seconded by Mr. Charles White.</p>
        <p>CLEARANCE SALE of Table Lamps, hanging Lamps, etc., great bargains.—J. S. GARLAND, 51, Bridge-street, Warrington.—Advt. Bewsey-road Choir Social. About 70 members and friends of the Bewsey-road Wesleyan Choir took part in making the annual social a grand success, at the Winmarleigh Cafe on Wednesday evening last. The ,evening was occupied with games and special music, and anyone in need of a change found it in the refreshment room. Items were given by 4fr. W. Pearce, Mr. and Mrs Hannan and others, and were well received. It will be remembered that a few weeks ago the Choir won the Challenge Cup presented by Mr. James Dodds at the Warrington Musical Festival. This cup, by request, wm there, and admired 14- 011. An interest:, in presentation was made by the choirmaster, Mr. William Hipkiss, to Mr. Williams, who has just relinguished the secretaryship of the Choir owing to his leaving the neighbourhood. The presentation took the form of a silver-mounted cigarette holder, Mr. Williams suitably replied to the effect that 110; had always taken a pleasure in the work of the Choir and always felt anxious for its welfare.</p>
        <p>DURING SALE, RARE BARG AINS tußrass and Irr Fenders, Coal Vases, etc.—J. 6. IiARLAND. Bridge-street, Warrington. Incorporated Permanent Building Society. The thirty-fourth annual meeting of the Borough of Warrington Permanent Building Society was held last Wednesday evening at the Manager's Office, Legh- street. Mr. George Raiford presided and amongst others present were : Messrs. T. A. Sutton, John. Andrews, James Gray, J. T. Hampson, William Milner, Henry Houghton, J. E. Richardson and William Caldwell.—ln submitting the report for the year • ending 31st January 1909, the Directors said that the nett profit amounted to £ll7 4s. 7d., which after allowing £46 ss. 7d. for manager, director's and auditor's fees and working expenses the directors recommended a dividend of £4 per cent per annum, free from income tax, which absorbed £144 10s. 11d• ; In order to meet the deficit it was proposed to transfer' the sum of £27 6s. 4d. from the reserve fund.—The Chairman in proposing the adoption of ,the report and balance sheet said he was sorry to say that business had not been very good with them during the past year. They hoped it would be better in the present year. They would see some extra charges which had come upon the Society, £l6 16s. legal expenses. Their property was in a perfectly satis- factory condition, and they had never lost a penny from the foundation of the Society to the present time, and he thought there was a prospect of them always prospering. All they wanted now was business to make it a very prosperous and safe Society.—Mr. J. P. Sampson seconded.—Mr. William Milner was elected a Director in the . place of Mr. William Roberts' and Messrs. G. Holford, J. Andrews; J. Gray, J. T. Sampson, H. Houghton and S. F. Welsby were elected Directors. The manager (Mr. T. A. Sutton) and the auditors (Messrs. J. G. Chappell and F. Daintith were , re-elected.--A vote of thanks to the Chairman con- eluded the meeting.</p>
        <p>Take Woods' Great Peppermint Cure, for Coughs and Colds; it never fails. Is. lid., 2s. 9d.—Advt.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-03-06_WidnesExaminer_art0053" n="art0053">
        <head>MR. DANSEY'S DEATH.</head>
        <p>MR. DANSEY'S DEATH.</p>
        <p>The Chairman said be was sorry to have to report the sudden death of Mr. Dansey, Poor Law Inspector for that district, lie was sure he was only expressing the feeling of the board when he said how sorry they were to lose the advice of such an excellent 611-icer. He pro- posed that a vote of condolence be forwarded to Mrs. Dansey and family. Mr. Norman seconded and said that he had always found Mr. Dansey a gentleman in all businesa trans- actions with him. He was very sorry to hear of his sudden death.</p>
        <p>The motion was passed in silence, the members standing.</p>
        <p>BIRDSWOOD FOOTPATH, SUTTON. Mr. M. C. Tait, assistant solicitor to the L. &amp; N.-W. Railway Co., wrote as follows :—I am informed by the Company's divisional enquirers that there has been considerable trespass lately on the Company's Crewe to Warrington and Runcorn lines at Birdswood, near the site of a former private occupation level crossing, connecting the several portions of the Aston estate. The trespass is necessarily attended with great danger to the persons crossing the line, and the company have taken steps to stop it, but I understand that it has been alleged that a public footpath exists somewhere about where the line is being crossed. I tnink your Council will agree that it is very desirable that the matter should be cleared up. I therefore write to inquire whether the Council as Highway Authority and Guardians of public rights of way intend asserting a public • right of way across the railway at or near the point in question. It was decided to defer the question until a later meeting in order that inquiries might be made.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-03-06_WidnesExaminer_art0054" n="art0054">
        <head>BUILDING RESTRICTIONS.</head>
        <p>BUILDING RESTRICTIONS.</p>
        <p>The Farnworth Rural District Council, Surrey, asked the Council to pass the following resolution : That the attention of the Local Government Board be drawn to the inefficient powers possessei by rural district councils to secure an area of open land around new houses sufficient for the disposal upon it of domestic sewage arising from the house, particularly as owing to the erection of houses on spaces of inadequate area the Council have met with serious difficulties and expenses in connection with drainage schemes and are apprehensive of further difficulties unless additional powers are granted. Mr. Linaker moved that the resolution should be left on the table.</p>
        <p>Mr. ROYLANCE seconded and the motion was carried with unanimity.</p>
        <p>DARESBURY POSTOFFICE.</p>
        <p>The General Post Office surVeyer wrote to the Clerk proposing the daily opening of the oiiice at 8 o'cicck insteadof 7 o'clock. The surveyor stated that it would not effect the morning delivery in the least. He hoped the proposition would be accepted. The proposition was accepted. Mr.LlAllows commeuted upon the state of the road con necting Mr. Halls residence. Birch Dale, with Mr. Vero Davies's resideuce. He stated that it was in a frightful condition and as the road was used by many of the public, it was very objectionable. He hoped some- thing would be done. The matter was referred to the Appleton Parish Council.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-03-06_WidnesExaminer_art0055" n="art0055">
        <head>EARLESTOWN AND NEWTON.</head>
        <p>EARLESTOWN AND NEWTON.</p>
        <p>URBAN COUNCIL MEETING.</p>
        <p>The monthly meeting of the Newton I.Trba Council was held on Monday evening. The chair was taken by Mr. T. Ortnand, and there were also present :—Messrs. W. Collingwood, L. Pilkington, Harwood, T. Ritchie, M. Satan, J. Higham, G. F. Clarke, E. L. Bent, E.. T. Evans, C. B. F'. Borron, Suttees (water engineer),Bowes (surveyor) and C. Cole (clerk.) The Chairman congratu- lated Mr. Harwood on his return after an absence of about six month owing to indisposition. He trusted he would long be spared to give his services to the Council and township. (Hear, hear.) The Water Committee had resolved that the Council be recommended to enter into agreement with the Leigh Rural Council to supply their requirements for water. The minutes were passed on the motion of Mr. Borron, seconded by Mr. Bent.— The Clerk having read the opinion of counsel on the case submitted to him it was resolved by the Sanitary Committee that the Clerk be instructed to report to the County Public Health Committee the evidence obtained of the pollution of Millingford Brook, and that they be asked to receive a deputation on the subject. It was also further resolved that as the Urban District of Golhorne is, to a larger degree, affected by. the pollution of the stream, the Council be asked to appoint a similar deputation to attend with the one appointed by the committee.—The minutes were passed on the motion of the Chairman, seconded by Mr' Seton.—lt was resolved, on the motion of the Chairman that Mr. Harold E. Watkins be appointed (subject to the approval of the Local Government Board) medical °Meer of Inallth tor the district for one sear from the 26th March inst., aWI a salary at the rate of £lOO per annum for his services and that a moiety of such salary be claimed out of the county fund.—Councillor Bent, Clarke and Tunstall, and Mr. James Sankey were re-elected overseers of the township. SONS OF TEMPERANCE FRIENDLY SOCIETY The annual meeting of the Earlestown Grand Division of this Order was held at Earlestown on Saturday. The G.W.P. Bro. A. Holmes presided, and delegates sere present from St. Helens, Haydock, IVidnes, Warrington and Earlestown. Bro. Holmes said he should always look back to his year of office with very great pleasure, and whilst he was sorry he could not ' report a larger increase in the membership, he was thankful for the success which had been attained. Bro. Judson G.P., said the need of the Cadets was more adult workers. Owing to the rather drastic weed- ing out of those in arrears, there was a decrease in the numbers but from what he already saw, he felt sure the tide had turned and the increase this year would far outweigh last year's losses.. The report gave n net gain of 45 adults,and a loss of 34 to the Cadets. The total membership at the end of the year was 1,527. Two hundred and nineteen members had been on the funds and had received £622 is. for 6,984 days disablement, £399 us. for 4,312 days sick- ness and £221 10s. for-2,672 days accident pay. £7O bad been paid for funerals of seven members deceased during year. The sum of £252 had been added to the Benefit Funds bringing the capital up to the sum of £3,107 10s• 3d. The sickness rate was 81 days per S.B member per year and the death rate of the Order 633 per 1,000 members per annum. A discussion took place as to the effect of the Com- pensation Act on the funds of Friendly Societies, and a proposal was made to reduce the benefits of members in receipt of compensation by 50 per cent. The dis- cussion proved that the members realized the seriousness of the question, but considered the proposal premature and preferred to wait and see what the Conference of Friendly Societies have to suggest on this question at their next meeting. A new scale for the payment of 15s. per week during disablement was negatived. The G.W.P.-elect was nominated as delegate to N.D. at Glasgow in Whit weea, Bro. Briscoe, ECU., G. Trustee to he alternative.</p>
        <p>-- Book prizes were presented to cadets for success in introducing new members, Cadets Elsie Holmes, St. Helens, and Maggie Croston, Havdock, dividing the Ist prize, John Phillips, Earlestown, taking the 2nd and A. Miller, St. Helens, the 3rd. Tbe G.D. Trophy chain was won by the Earl of Shaftesbury section, St. Helens. The election of officers resulted as follows: W. T. Harrison, Widnes, G.W.P.; G.W.A. A. Taylor, Haydock ; G. Con. Joseph French, Earlestown; G. Chap. H. Wardman, Warrington ; G. Sentl. William Briscoe, jun. Haydock ; G. Treasurer W. Clare, Earlestown; G Patron, J. Judson, Earlestown ; P.G.W.P. A. Holmes, St. Helens; G. Scribe, William Pearson, Earlestown ; auditors, .Bros. Briscoe, sen. and The next meeting is to be held at Widnes in April.</p>
      </div>
