      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-03-06_WidnesExaminer_art0064" n="art0064">
        <p>! t</p>
        <p>WARRINGTON MALE VOICE CHOIR.</p>
        <p>Concert at the Parr Hall.</p>
        <p>The Warrington Male Voice Choir has set before itself the high ideal of art for art's sake, and success at competitive festivals, far from being the goal of its ambitions, is rightly regarded as a stepping stone in the advancement of true musical culture. Not only is sound progress being made on these commendable lines, but every effort is exerted to foster the love o elevating music amongst the townspeople—a fact which was pleasingly evidenced at the eighth annual concert given by the choir in the Parr Hall on Thurs- day evening. A thoroughlf high-class programme, compiled with careful discrimination, was submitted, and what is still more encouraging is that a numerous audience received it with unmistakable appreciation. Selections by the choir, of course, took the most promi- nent place and they were so chosen as to offer scope for great variety of style and expression, ranging from massive,brilliant choruses like "The Word went forth" (Mendelasohn) and "The King of worlds" (Dard- Janin) to rich, entrancing part songs like "0 Peaceful night" (German) and " Evening " (Abt). Consider- able interest centred in the performance of one of Sir Edward Elgar's most recent compositions "The Reveille," a rugged setting of Bret Harte's striking poem, which conjures up in a wonderfully vivid way the stirring beat of the drum and the tramp of armed hosts. The dramatic intensity of the piece, as it works up to its impressive climax, thrills the listener, but an adequate interpretation involves decidedly strenuous and difficult work. It speaks well for the choir that, under Mr. W. S. Nesbitt's inspiring conductorship, the many intricacies were successfully surmounted and the general effect was entirely satisfactory. In that weird and awsome work "The phantom Host" (Hegar) the choir gave further proof of that ability to realize the atmosphere of a piece which is• one of the first essentials of success. Of the choral selections generally it is possible to speak in terms of praise, and the splendid results of painstaking work in the proper spirit must be gratifying to all concerned. Mr. Nesbitt has a fine body of voices under his control and knows how to draw the best out of his resources. The tone and blend are alike good, and there is always an intelligent response to the conductor's baton. The inclusion in the programme of Sir C. V. Stanford's five sea songs with chorus was a happy innovation which proved particularly accept- able. Mr. Hamilton Harris, the baritone soloist, soon ingratiated himself with the audience, for he seems to revel in these typically English songs which smack of the salt sea breeze, and sang them with a whole-heartedness that was as refreshing as it was eminently appropriate. In the choruses he was admirably backed up by the choir, who also entered into the spirit of the compositions with evident zest, and the series went with a delightful swing Mr. _ _ _ . •</p>
        <p>Haryis is fortunate in. the possessicin of a robusi voice of excellent quality and later he added to his laurels by the spirit and piquancy which characterised his vocalisations of" Son of Mine" (Wallace) and" Why so pale and wan" (Sir C. H. H. Parry) respectively. Madame Annie Walker (soprano) charmed everyone by the sweetness and naturalness of her singing. The light fancifulness of "Nymphs and Shepherds"(Purce//) was finely realized,as was also the pensive air so beautifully expressed in" If I built a world for you" (Lehmann). Miss Walker was heard to the same advantage in"ln my garden" (Liddle) and the bright and sparkling "A May-day morn" (Slater), her enunciation being especially praiseworthy. The audience expressed deligh t by according a hearty encore. Madame Walker and Mr. Hamilton Harris were associated in the merry duet" In spring time" (Ernest Newton) which proved one of the gems of the evening and had to be repeated. The appearance of Mr. Arthur Catterall, who has risen to the front rank of violinists, did much to enhance the interest of the concert, and his renderings of Airs Russes " (Wieniawski), " Melodies " (Tschaikowsky), and Zigeunerweisen " (Sarasate), evidenced real genius and brilliant technique. Mr. Catterall infuses individuality and soulfulness into his playing and he succeeded in mc% ing the assembly to such a pitch of enthusiasm that he was constrained to respond to a recall. Mrs. A. H. Crosfield had kindly acceded to the choir's request to give a pianoforte solo and fully confirmed previous high opinions of her musical talents by the brilliancy of her performance of a difficult Polonnaise by Chopin. Responding to an encore, she gave a Valse by the same composer, with great delicacy and artistic expression. Mr. E. Bennett North, deputy conductor of the choir, dis- charged the duties of accompanist with taste ans skill. We are glad that the promoterd had the courage to dispense with the almost inevitable humorist on this occasion„ for the familiar "comic element" would have been out of place at such a high-class concert.</p>
        <p>FACTORY ACT PROSECUTION AT WARRINGTON.</p>
        <p>At the Warrington Borough Police Court on Tuesday, before Messrs. H. Roberts (in the chair), and C. Jack- son Holmes, Messrs. John Edelston and Co., pin menu. facturers, Museum-street, were prosecuted under tbe Factory and Workshops Act for allowing dangerous machinery to run unfenced.—Mr. E. Draper (instructed by Messrs. Robert Davies and Co.), represented the defendants. Mr.J.E. Ashworth, district factory inspector, stated that Mr. Warren, a colleague, visited the factory and found a large fly wheel working unfenced. He was also told by the engine man that the wheel bad been running in that manner for about nine months.—Mr. Draper, in defending, said that the machinery was entirely separated from the rooms in which the employees worked and the only men who had right of entrance was the engineer who had the key. The day after the inspector bad visited the works the fencing was completed. He thought it was only a technical offence and the bench were perfectly justified in taking a lenient view of the rasa—The bench imposed a fine of 10s. and costs.—A case of a similar nature in which Messrs. Whitecross and Co. Ltd. were the defendants was then proceeded with. Mr. F.G.Taylor (instructed by Messrs. Greenall, Co.) defended and pleaded not guilty. Mr. Ashworth stated that Mr. Warren visited the above works, with a view to investigating an accident in which a boy had been injured by being caught in a shaft. Mr. Warren found the shaft was situated over one of the boilers and was connected with other machinery to work a mechanical feeding aparatus. Mr. Warren had ascertained that the boy was endeavouring to reach an oilcan on the top of this boiler" and in doing 8,, clambered to the ton by means of a ladder and was caught by the shafting. Mr. Warren stated thit the shaft was only really dangerous when workmen went on the boilers. He ascertained that valves used for blowing up the fires by steam power were placed on the boilers. Witness admitted that the shafting was perfectly safe for those at work on the floor. The revolutions of this shaft were only 30 per minute which he did not consider excessive. Mr. Taylor emphatically stated that there was not the slightest evidence to be submitted with regard to the machinery beiag dangerous. Mr. Warren had produced facts which he had no right whatever to use in that court. The magistrates did not know the date or any- thing concerning the accident, and he was instructed that it would never have happened if some mischievious boy had not climbed on the boiler. The only danger there appeared to be was when men c.ime from the back of the boiler. Concluding he said there was not a tittle of evidence against his clients.—The Chairman said the Bench concluded that the summons was hardly justified., and that the defendants might have been asked to fence the shafting. They would dismiss the case and leave it with Mr. Taylor and the inspector as to the fencing of the shaft.—Mr. Taylor remarked that the shaft was fenced the day following the inspector's visit.</p>
        <p>TELEGRAPH WIRES CUT DOWE.</p>
        <p>ACCUSED WARRINGTON 111 EN ACQUITTED,</p>
        <p>At the Liverpool County Sessions on Wednesday before Mr. W. Scott Barrett and other magistrates and a jury, William Clarke, ladder maker, and Thomas Massey, wheelwright and metal broker, 101, Liverpool. road, Warrington, were charged with receiving at Warrington a quantity of copper wire the property Of the London and North-Western Railway end also wit'i stealing same. Mr. Samuel Pope, Bar rister' Manchester, prosecuted on behalf of the Company and Mr. William Madden and Mr. Phillip Walton instructed by Mr. S. Steel defended.—Mr. Pone said that on the night or the 26th January, a signalman got no answer to a message announcing the arrival of the London express. Investigations were made and it was discovered that 220 yards of copper wire had been cut troll the telegraph posts between Walton Arches and Bank Quay station. This part of the lire was not o er- looked by the signal box. However enquiries were made and it was discovered that the prisoner Clarke acting on the instruction of Massey had sold a quantity or wire which tallied with the missing wire to Messrs. Howard Bros., metal brokers, Bennett-street.—Williron Busses, signalman, in the service of the company, corroborated this statement.—Sanmel Twigaer, Westy-litee,Latchford, said he was a sub-inspector of the telegraph-department of the London and North-Western Railwey at Warring- , ton. On Wednesday 27th January he received a report with regard to the telegraph lines between Walton Arches and Bank Quay. He examined the lines and found that six spans of copper teleeraphio wire were missing Each span would cover 70 yards. He bad the cut ends removed and afterwards attended at the Police Station and compared the end with the wire there produced. They were both of the !same gauge and in two instances be found that the ends exactly met and joined.—Croas- examined by Mr. Madden he Said it was a most. difficult. matter to identify the wire and the Railway Cornriany had not a monopoly of that gauge.—John Howard,metal broker, Bennett-street, spoke as to the wire being sold to him by prisoner Clarke. He bad know him for over 20 years and had always found him to be honest, and straightforward.—The manager for Allan Webb, metal dealer, St. Helens said he sold a quantity of brass and copper to the prisoner, also there was as well a little copper wire amongst it, but 'there was none of the kind preduced in Court. Cross-examined .witness admitted that the copper sold to Massey had not been entered in the hooks of the firm. - Sergeant Stewart spoke to arresting the prisoners and was sub- jected to a long cross-examination by Mr. Madden. —Prisoners elected to give evidence on their own behalf.—Thomas Massey said that the wire produced, so far as be knew, was part of the metal purchased by hire from Mr. Webb on January 18th. At that time he pur- chased scrap iron and other material to the value of £36, and it was impossible for him to say whether he actually got the wire from Mr. Webb or not, To the best of his recollection it came from there.—William Clarke corroborated Alassey's evidence as to what took place at St.. Helens. On the 20th Jannary he nss7sted a man named Gleave to sort the heavy brass and copper in Mastey's 3ard. Amongst the same was about 221hs. of copper wire and that was the wire produced in Court. He sold the same to Messrs. Howard Bros. for £2 3s. d. —Mr. Madden, in his address to the Jury pointed out that for 30 years Massey had been in business in War- r ington,and this was the fir at charge that had beer4brought against him. The police and the prosecution bad had plenty of opportunity to prepare their case, hut the only identification that had been brought forward was the evidence of the Sub-Inspector, he contended that that ought not to be sufficient for the Jury. The prisoners had always told the same story. Everything had been straight with there and as far as they knew the wire had been purchased from Mr. Webb of St. Helens. It was not for the prisoners to prove where they got the wire from, but for the Railway Company to prove that the wire was theirs.</p>
        <p>After a short absence the Jury found the prisoners not guilty and they were accordingly discharged.</p>
