      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-03-06_WidnesExaminer_art0065" n="art0065">
        <head>Warrington Borough Court.</head>
        <p>Warrington Borough Court.</p>
        <p>MONDAY.—Before the Depnty-Mayor (in the chair), Messrs. W. Roberts, 'l'. Locker, G. Pilling and W. Furness.</p>
        <p>A SATURDAY NIGHT SCENE.—Joseph Clattcey and Catherine Clancy, of no settled abode, husband and wife, were charged with assaulting P.C. Steel, while acting in the execution of his duty. There aas a further charge against Mrs. Clancey of having been drunk and disorderly at 7.30 on Saturday night.—P.C. Steel stated that about 8.20 on Saturday night he was on duty in Haydock•street, when he had occasion to take the female prisoner into custody for cresting a disturbance. She tugged at his whistle chain and tore ' his tunic from shoulder to shoulder. The man then came up and struck witness in the ribs. A civilian named Thomas Birchall responded to witness's request to assist him until the arrival of P.C. Dawson. With great difficulty they succeeded in taking the woman to the police station. The male prisoner followed and was immediately taken into custody.— P.C. Dawson corroborated the last witness's statements, and said that a crowd of 1,000 people followed them to , the police station.—ln answer to the charge, the I male prisoner said that he did not strike P.C. Steel, but went to the constable with the intention of telling his wife to go down quietly.—Both Prisoners were then charged with neglecting their four children.—Mr. B. Shearer prosecuted on behalf of the Guardians, and said the wife and children were admitted into the Workhouse on the 11th of last month. She, however, left the Union, the work not being to her satisfaction, and the children remained in the institution up to the present time.—Both prisoners were sentenced to one month's imprisonment, and the Deputy Mayor thanked Thomas Birchall for his assistance to the police. WOMAN AssuLTED.—Frank Ryan, 43, Lain. Street, was charged with assaulting Elizabeth 'Matthews on February 21st.—He pleaded not guilty.—Mrs. Matthews stated that the prisoner went to her house and wanted her to apologise for " scandalising " him. She told him to go away and he did so, but returned soon after and entered by the back door. Witness went to him, and he thereupon struck her, knocked her down in the yard, te,arinh her blouse. Prisoner said that he did not strike the woman. He was only freeing himself from her in order to defend himself against her husband, who attempted to strike him.—Prisoner was ordered to pay the costs of the case (165.) or 14 days' imprison- ment.</p>
        <p>--- MISCRIMANSOUS.—CharIes Henry Jones, Rose and Crown-street was summoned for using oLscene language In Rose and Crown-street,on the 19th February and was finsd 3s. ed. the costs of the case.—P.C. Clarke proved the case.—Thomas Healey, Clare-street, John Oakes, Clare-street, George Houghton, Ashton-street and Richard Smith, Louis-street, youths, were charged with playing football in Foundry-street on 23rd February.—Aealey and Oaks were fined 2s. this being their second offence, while Smith and Houghton who made their first appearance had to pay Is. each.</p>
        <p>TUESDAY.—Before IL Robeats in the chair and John Holmes. _ _ _</p>
        <p>ARREARS OF MAINTENANCE.—Henry Chilton, Rodney Street was summoned for arrears of maintenance in</p>
        <p>respect of his son Samuel who was sent to an industrial school.—An order was made for payment of Is. 6d. per week 27 weeks ago but the defendant had not paid a halfpenny pf this. He was further summoned for arrears in respect of his son Henry in whose case an order of Is. 6d. was made and no attempt had been made to pay.—He was sentenced to one month's imprisonment.—James Clare 12 Turner-street and William James Back Dallam Lane were each sentenced to one month's imprisonment for similar offences. FALSE ATTESTATION.—Patrick Carroll, Liverpool was charged with making a false attestation while enlisting in the 4th Battalion King's Liverpool Regiment. The prisoner asked if he was married replied" No "but it was subsequently found that he was.—He was sent to 7 day's imprisonment. WEDNESDAY.—Before Mr. C. White (chairman), Alder- men, Smethurst and Ellison, and Mr. J. W. Hayward. _ _</p>
        <p>NEdLECT CASE.—Daniel SiXsmith, Manley-street,was charged with neglecting his two children, aged two and six months respectively, in a manner likely to cause them unnecessary suffering and injury to health. He pleaded not guilty.—Mr. A.Browne, who prosecuted on behalf of the N.S.P.C.C., said that prisoned was 30 years of age and he was formerly employed at Sankey Lead Works but left there through no cause whatever. Afterwards he obtained work at the Bishop's Wharf and while there injured his hand. He was off work for six weeks. He then found employment at Messrs. Whitecross Co., Limited as a labourer, and through his inattention to duty he was discharged. Inspector Cox, who had the case in hand, kindly visited the foreman' (Mr. Warburton) and persuaded him to give the man another chance. Mr. Cox arranged with the prisoner to commence work at a oertain time, but Sixsmith never turned up and had never been seen from that day until thepresent.His wife was a hard-working woman and was very respectable, but owing to prisoner's habits the children were improperly fed.—Mrs. Sixstnith said that her husband could earn £1 per week. Her children were improperly fed, and she had had to bee, from the neighbours. She also had to pawn a bedstead in order to pay the house rent.—The Bench decided to give prisoner another chance and adjourned the case for a month, Mr. Warburton agreeing to do his best to find prisoner work, _ _</p>
        <p>TH lIRSDAY.—Before Messrs. Charles White (chairman), W. B. Rvtherts, Rymer Young. THEFT ON BOOTS. —James Harris, a youth of no settled abode was charged with stealing two pairs of mens boots. valued 12s. 10d., the property of Messrs. Stead and Simon, Horsemarket on February 23rd. Prisoner pleaded guilty. The Chief Constable stated that about Ave o'clock on the day in question the boots were seen safe, hanging outside the shop, but at 5.30 they were missed. Information was given to the police and Inspector Forster and Constable Holland made inquiries. The stolen boots were subsequently found, one pair at Messrs. Tyrer's, Winwick-streetjand the other pair at Mr. Brigg's, pawnbroker, Powys-street. The prisoner was arrested and brought to the police station where he admitted the offence. He (the Chief Constable) thought the prisoner was worthy of some consideration having been brought up in very bad surroundings. He sug- gested that, if the matter was put in the hands of Mr. Firth (police court missionary) and a situation found for him he might mend his ways.—This course was adopted by the bench and the case was adjourned for a month in order to give prisoner another chance. ARREARS OF "NIAINTENANCE. —AI be r t Hamb let t, last from. Manchester, was charged wiih arrears of mainten- ance due to his wife (Elizabeth liainblett);aruounting to ‘.CI 2s. Prisoner pleaded guilty. He was ordered to pay ihe arrears, or one month's imprisonment in •default.</p>
        <p>A G ENS It At. RIOT.— Philip Cheaters, Lythgoes-lane, was charged on remand with assaulting and beating Catherine Roberts, Turner-street; also with assaulting Charles Roberts on the same date.—Mr. A. Browne appeared for the complainants and Mr. I'. Steel defended the defendant Chesters.—Mr. Browne stated that the Teale complainant Roberts on returning home on the 20th February found his door smashed in and a Ihrge crowd outside. The defendant was also on the scene. Roberts asked Chesters what he had done it for, wheretipon defendant struck at hirn with a poker and hit, him right across the nose and felled him to the ground. ' Whilst there the female complainant came up and remonstrated with the defendant, who therewith rushed at her and kicked her in the left eye. Both complainants corroborated these statements.—Mary Ann Smith and Mary Mead both spoke to seeing defendant strike the two complainants.—Mr. Steel in defence said that the defendant had instructed him that he was not iu Lythgoe's-lane that night and that the injuries which the two complainants had incurred were received from being connected with a street brawl the following night. Tho story of the alleged assault was pure fabrication.—Defendant stated that on the niebt in question be was nowhere near Lythgoe's- lane.—William Wall and defendant's father also gave evidence to this etteot.—After lunch deliberation the Bench decided to give defendant the benefit of the doubt and dismissed the case.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-03-06_WidnesExaminer_art0066" n="art0066">
        <head>ROYAL COURT THEATRE.</head>
        <p>ROYAL COURT THEATRE.</p>
        <p>" Second to None."</p>
        <p>Mr. Walter Howard has a great reputation as a writer of military dramas, and "Second to None," which is presented at the Royal Court Theatre this week by Mr. T. Edward Ward's principal company, is one of his most popular works. It is an enthralling story of the Royal Scots Greys, with a picturesque setting and plenty of dash and excitement. As on the occasion of its •previous visit to Warrington, the play has been enthusiastically welcomed by large audiences. Miss Ward is a charming Nell Greystone, and makes a powerful appeal by her realistic pbrtrayal in which vivacity and pathos both have a part. Mr. Leonard Williams is a bright and impetuous Harry Wilding and Mr. Clifford Roan is well placed as his noble-hearted brother Frank. Mr. C. W. Spencer makes a soldierly and dignified Sir Reginald Greystone, V.O. and Mr. Frederick F. Lloyd gives a convincing study of the unscrupulous Captain Philip Mannering. One of the cleverest pieces of acting in the play is Mr. J. G. Mayne's wierd presentment of Sergeant Jack Horseman. Miss Grace Harlington scores a success as May _Greystone and Mr. Frank R. Gwyn Richardson as Lieutenant Archer Ballater soon establishes himself in the favours of the audience. There are plenty of humorous touches for which Mr. George Davies (Joe Ruggles), Mr. H. Macauley (Private Flutter) and Miss' Clarrie Yandell (Norah) are largely responsible and the performance provides a very pleasant evening's entertainment. •</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-03-06_WidnesExaminer_art0067" n="art0067">
        <head>Next Week's Pr3gramme.</head>
        <p>Next Week's Pr3gramme.</p>
        <p>NEW VERSION OF HALL CAINE'S POPULAR PLAY" THE CHRISTIAN."</p>
        <p>A play that sets the Pit and Gallery thinking over a problem which they have probably never considered before must be reckoned as a worthy achievement. And this is vhat Mr. Hall Caine does in the Revised Version of" The Christian" which will be performed at the, Court Theatre on Monday next for six nights. It had already created a sensation in London and the provinces. The revised version is more concise and more emotional than the older form of the play. It is a play with a purpose and in it Mr. Caine casts at the heads of his audience scores of facts. The truth of which every man knows, every woman regrets, and to which every prig is wilfully blind. There is plenty. of " thrill in the play, and high colour. "The Christian " in its altered form is in short a play of the strongest interest and, as presented by Mr. Went- worth Croke's Company it is calculated to send people away with lively recollections and decided opinions. Mr. Henry Renouf is the John Storm, and Miss Doris Gilham the Glory Quayle.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-03-06_WidnesExaminer_art0068" n="art0068">
        <head>FAIR WOMAN'S FOE.</head>
        <p>FAIR WOMAN'S FOE.</p>
        <p>Fatness Easily Conquered.</p>
        <p>The more beautiful a woman is the more distressing it is for her to discover that she is rapidly getting too fat, and has not been able to find the proper cure. Still worse it is when she imprudently tries fasting, exercis- ing, tight-lacing, and so forth. Both health and good looks soon fly away when such worse than useless methods are employed. The beauty-destroying enemy can be easily and harmlessly conquered by tbe most inoffensive of weapons—cheaply, too, and without the least bother. TLis is how : Go to your chemist, and ask him for the following, and either let him mix them for you or do so yourself :—One half-ounce of Marmots, one ounce or fluid extract of Glycyrrhiza 8.P., one ounce of pure Glycerine BP., and Peppermint Water to make six ounces in all. The mixture must be taken in doses of two teaspoonfuls after each repast and at bedtime. Continue this simple treatment for a few weeks, without even a pretence of starving or "gymnasticating," and you will soon recover your charms of form, figure, and feature. Don't worry: the treatment will do the thing properly.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-03-06_WidnesExaminer_art0069" n="art0069">
        <head>ARE WIDNES WORKERS A "DRUNKEN DISSOLUTE LOT.?'</head>
        <p>ARE WIDNES WORKERS A "DRUNKEN DISSOLUTE LOT.?'</p>
        <p>There is much indignation in Widnes in consequence of a statement made by Mr. T. P. Davies, manager for Messrs. Davies, Limited, of Liverpool and Widnes, before a Home Office inquiry in Loudon to the effect that on the whole the men were "a drunken, dissolute lot." Many protests against the statement were made on Saturday by men and also by employers' "Scandalous and absolutely unwarranted," was the Mayor's description of Mr. Davies's attack. "I have had thirty-five years' experience of the Widnes working man," he continued, "and I say that a finer type of the worker is not to be found in any town or village in the kingdom."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-03-06_WidnesExaminer_art0070" n="art0070">
        <head>nth</head>
        <p>nth</p>
