      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-03-06_WidnesExaminer_art0071" n="art0071">
        <head>Bewsey Road Wesley Guild.</head>
        <p>Bewsey Road Wesley Guild.</p>
        <p>On Monday eyening last the monthly devotional meeting in connection with the Bewsoy Road Wesley Guild was held in the Lecture Hall. There was a large attendance, and Mr. A. Williams occupied the chair. A short paper was read by Mr. George B. Keeling on "The witness of great men to Jesus Christ," and was very much appreciated, owing to the able manner in which he portrayed the testimonies of literary scholars, who expressed the high value of Christ's teaching. The roll was called, and the members, in responding to their names, gave quotations from eminent writers. A hearty vote of thanks to the essayist and the Chairman concluded the meeting.</p>
        <p>Wycliffe P. 5.11, The usual meeting was held .on Sunday, and was presided over by the Secretary. Rev. R. E. Letheren gave the address upon " The Common People heard him gladly." When Abraham Lincoln was made President, as he was passing through the crowd,a man was heard to remark," ()h what a common looking mon," whereupon Lincoln said to him" Yes, that is the reason God made so many of them." What, continued the speaker, would the railway companies do if it were not for the common fares or common people? They would soon have to, wind up. The Great Christ knew the world and knew the people He moved amongst. With a great knowledge of the people's needs He spoke to them well. Touched with their feelings, He dealt with them feelingly and sympathetically. That drew the affections of the common people so that they heard Him gladly. Mr. R. Mills also took part in the meeting. Miss Amour sang Hymns 103 and 91 and the Brethren joined in the refrain. The Chairman spoke of the anniversary to-morrow (Sunday), which would be the 16th, and referred to the good accomplished in thoge years. An open meeting would be held under the presidency of the Mayor of Warrington.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-03-06_WidnesExaminer_art0072" n="art0072">
        <head>Isold-street Wesiesan Church.</head>
        <p>Isold-street Wesiesan Church.</p>
        <p>The subject of the Rev. T. NI flier's sermon at Bold street last Sunday evening was Christ's sacrifice and human life "and was based upon 1. Peter i. 18-19. Life untouched by Christ's sacrifice was described as vain and empty. Satisfaction of soul could not be found in temporal things. This statement was illustrated by references to the "Memorials of Sir Edwin Burne- Jones" and Tolstoi's "Con fe,sion."Both these men could be counted successful in their respective spheres, yet what happiness they bad came, not through their art, but from their attachment to Christ. A sinful life was one of bondage-bondage to one's past and also to con- vention as represented by tradition. The atomic theory of sin was contrary to human experience. Man's will was not a! free after a laspse from goodness as before it. Physical results of sin were not as serious as the moral ; transgression might not cause physical suffering hut it always resulted in spiritual weakness. Freedom from dulness of life could only be found in Christ's service. St. Peter declared that freedom was through blood—the precious blood of Christ. Modern teachers were sometimes afraid of the terms which were con- stantly used by the Apostles. Christ's death was more than martyrdom, it was more than loyalty to truth even to death. It showed forth God's love but it showed forth His judgment of sin. It revealed the wrath which it removed. It was the aspect of Christ's love as described in Dora Greenwelre " Pitman and his wife" which would win men from the empty, useless, fruitless life of the world.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-03-06_WidnesExaminer_art0073" n="art0073">
        <head>Legh-street Primitive Methodist Church Anniversary Services.</head>
        <p>Legh-street Primitive Methodist Church Anniversary Services.</p>
        <p>Special services wete held last week-end at the Legh- street Primitive Church, when the anniversary was celebtated. The preacher both morning and evening was the Rev. James Trsvis, of Cisester. Preaching in the morning to a good congregation the rev. gentleman to tit as his text " There is a strength of beauty in the tabernacle of Hosts" and said that being a Christian meant thas they bad strength and beauty. A musical service was held in the afternoon, and was presided over by Mr. J. Elwell. There was a fair tatendsnce. Miss E. Hewitt, a promising girl soprano, tastefully rendered "The Children's Home" and "Father Eternal." Miss Mary Davenport, who possesses a voice of great power and sweetness, was highly suc- cessful in 0 rest in the Lord " and " The Better Land." Mr. W. Hughes gave a good interpretation of "My hope is in the everlasting" and Sound an alarm." Mr. G. Giusti was heard to advantage in "The road to Light" acd " Nearer, my God, to Thee." The choir, undsr the conductorship of Mr. A. Spruce, scquitted themselves admirably in Mozart's motet " 0 God, when Thou appearest," Nicholl's " 0 worship the King," and Dudley Buck's Lead, kindly Light." During the e% ening service the choir gave n fine rendering of Woodward'spopular anthem 'The Radiant Morn," and Miss A. Bridge gave an artistic rendering of "A Dream of Parsdi,e," her fine rich voice having pleasing effect. Mr, A. W. Clatworthy resided at the organ, and executed the various accompaniments with taste and ability. - - -</p>
        <p>The Rev. James Travis preached in the pvening to a large congregation, and took as his subject" Do not love the world." He said that it did not mean that they should not love the beauties of nature, nor if any pleasures came in their path they could not par- take of them. The Apostle meant that they should made God the ruling power of their lives and give Christ the pre-eminence. • On Monday evening a meeting was held under the presidency of Mr. William Roberts, who was supported by the Rev. :James Travis, Rev. E. Blanchard Keeling and Mr. J. Whitley. _ _ _</p>
        <p>The 'HA IRMAN' said he had always felt very strongly with the warm-hearted fervour of Primitive Methodists ~hich was most congenial to his excitable nature and disposition. (Laughter.) He had gone there to be quickened in his work for Jesus Christ. Some of the dearest friends of his life had been associated with that place of worship. If some of the younger men and women only realised the devotion which those gone before 'displayed,they would put more zeal into their ork. He bad always had a great affection for the Primitives for several reasons. One was because of the unmistakable support anti sacrifice they had always given to the cause of Temperance. He did not remember one of their ministers for the last forty years .ho ens not a staunch advocate of Temperance. The great service the Primitives had rendered to the Free Church Council showed their lo3Blty. He was glad that the churches were notseparated as they used to he. There was more unanimity than he had ever known before. Reforms would not have advanced so far had it. not been for the Free Churches. (Applause.) The Rev. E. B. KEELING said the Primitive Methodist and the Wesloyans parted many years ago on the hest of terms. There were three great elements in life. The first was God, the second other people, and the third their own character and well being. Their own personal salvation must go last. God first, a man's duty to others next, and his self interest at the end. He was convinced that was the right order. Christ said they should love one another as He had loved them. Ths Rev. JAMES TRAVIS said that when he began to preich about fifty years ago they had 1,857 chapels, very small, inn, tistic and inexpensive, They had to-day close upon 5,000 chapels, many of them large and costly. During the last 20 years they had contributed more to their churches and schools than their foonders had done. IWhen he began to preach they had 145,000 scholars and teachers, now they ht.d over half a million. The most important feature was the work with their young people. They needed in a large measure the spirit of evangelism,' which dominated their fathers. 'Evangelism was never needed in England more than it was to-day. Notwithstanding the growth of the churches—and they had grown far more than they got credit for—they had not kept pace with the increase of population. Their fathers aimed at conversion, and were disappointed if they did not get them. They needed conversions,and they must try to get them. The evangelical Tevival of the 18th century created life and quickened the whole of the Protes tent world,and their cburcbes had been created by it. They would decline if they neglected the work of converting sinners. He saw signs of a soul converting revival coming. They needed their father's spirit of abandonment to God. (Applause.) A vote of thanks was accorded to the Chairman and the speakers.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-03-06_WidnesExaminer_art0074" n="art0074">
        <head>WARRINGTON AND DISTRICT FREE CHURCHES.</head>
        <p>THE WARRINGTON AND DISTRICT FREE CHURCHES.</p>
        <p>Intended Erection of New Buildings</p>
        <p>Several of the Free Churches in Warrington in th,, near future propose to erect new places of worship and Sunday schools.</p>
        <p>The Wesleyans of Wa rrington are appealing for funds for the purchase of land near Orford-avenue, on which it is proposed to erect a Chapel. The land has already been secured and £l7O has been contributed but a further sum is requir ed to wipe off the balance due.</p>
        <p>The Congregationalists have purchased 'a site off Padgate-lane on which they intend to erect a new Chapel to take the place of Cockhed ge Mission in Brick-street. We also learn that the Wesleyans of Padgate are finding that their Sunday school is too small to accomo- date efficiently the children who attend and therefore it is proposed to erect a larger one. As has already been announced a Bazaar is to be held this month in the Parr Hall by the Baptists attached to Golborne-street Chapel in order to raise further funds to enable them to erect a new Chape 1 and Sunday school—they find the' present ones most unsuitable for present day requirements. Altogether it will be seen that the Free Churches in Warrington are exceptionally active with respect to new buildings at the present time.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-03-06_WidnesExaminer_art0075" n="art0075">
        <p>A PUBLIC-HOUSE ASSESSMENT.</p>
        <p>Works Clubs Reduce the Business.</p>
        <p>The monthlv.. meeting of the Warrington Union Assess' ment was held at the Workhouse yesterday. Mr. Billiuge presided and there were also present :—MessrS, A. Brooks, G. Mather, R. Bolton, ,W. Gill, R. Forshavit E. L. Bent, W. Houghton, W. H. Woodcock (assistant overseer for Warrington), C. Cole (assistant overseer for Newton). J. Dickenson (assistant overseer for Haydoek (J. Smith (assistant overseer for Sankey), F. R. Meacock (valuer) and A. Bottomley (clerk). Mr. T. S. Silty, appealed on behalf of Mr. W. f' Longraore against, the assessment of the Railway Ins, 2, Factory-lane, Warrington, which stood at £BO gross, and £6B ratable. Ho said that in order to get to the Railway Inn from Bank Quay Bridge they had to g°, down some steps, or p,o tight down to the bottom s' bridge and come back again. The house VMS veil inconveniently situated for trade. and the only trac.le that was done was obtained from the residents in, Factory-Is no, from the workpeopi6 of Messrs. Crosfields and the Whiteeross Company. Sometime ago there were something like a hundred houses in Factory-lave, but owing to the extension of Crestields' works and the London and North-Western Railway Co., there were now only ten houses and the tenant had to look to 000 for support.. Crosflelche and Whitecross had got clubs of their own and the workpeople instead of using thsV house used their clubs, The tenant had undoubtedll suffered. Two or three years ago the police objected to a semi-billiard table there and its use was discos', tinned. The present tenant took the house in 1001 as° the rent at that time was £BO a year. In 1902 it co° reduced £6O and last year to £4O. In 1903 for sos3e reason or other his assessment was raised from £53 t° EP. the house was too highly assessed and his (gr' Steers) application was that they should reduce it. In reply to questions put by members of the Co' mittee he said it was a tied house of Messrs. Greene!' ‘Vltitiey and Co., Ltd. Since 1992 the truant had pal° to Messrs. Greenall £7OO less than was paid during tle six years before 1902. It used to be a very large busr ness. Mr. Hot.NiwroN-: It in--mot &lt;Tithe—the speech yo° would have made if you had been appealing for corn' pensati on. Mr. STEEL: Circumstances alter oases. (Laughter.) The case was referred to the valuer.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-03-06_WidnesExaminer_art0076" n="art0076">
        <head>Death of a Warrington Lady Abroad.</head>
        <p>Death of a Warrington Lady Abroad.</p>
        <p>The sad news has come to hand this week that Mrs• George Milne (the eldest (laughter of Mrs. J. Ewart, of Latchford), died at Suez on Fridal last week from an internal malady. Her mother received cablegrams on the 24th January which resulted in her immediately setting out fol. Suez -by the .quick over-land route, but she did D 0 arrive before Wednesday this week, and in the mewl; time Mrs. Milne had died and been buried.. One</p>
        <p>the saddest features of the occurrence is that Mrs.; Ewart would not know the worst had happened untu she arrived at Suez. Mrs. Milne was married se recently as August last at Christ Church, Latchford' and went abroad with her husband, who is in the Eastern Telegraph Service, in October. She Wat; only 25 years old, and was well-known and esteenied by a large circle of friends in Warrington and Latch- ford.</p>
      </div>
