        <p>ta.:111; i 8 one of the great Napoleon's picturesque el,ga. He meant that a soldier, to be effective, LT;anle, must be fed properly, and able to digest his —'(mla• His remark applies to every man, woman and child in every-day life. If the organs, nerves, tr,..Bel„ea, of your body are to work properly they must 'tulY nourished. And it is not merely what you eaw t that nourishes, but what you digest. Indigestion as."cilkens your system, fills your blood with impurities, ". renders you liable to all manner of diseases. You agr,"r dull, weak, and ailing, work of any kind becomes - "48k, and life itself a burden. Take Mother Seigel's Syrup. It restores stomach, 4Yer and bowels to natural activity, ensures perfect , 'Reation, perfect nourishment, and therefore perfect. oealth. Morton, of 233, Lymington-avenue, Lord- -4„ltelane, N., writing on June 17th, 1908, says : B binPai I suffered after eating was unbearable, and ,allious attacks increased so much, and were so While they lasted, that my sight was affected, and toings became blurred and confused. I was advised ,trY Mother Seigel's Syrup, and before I had taken of left the second bottle, the pain after eating had Ine• The continued use of this medicine steadily Proved my health until my ailments completely left ' nd Igestion is now no more to me than a painful -.ternary."</p>
        <p>IVI ether Seigel's Syrup iS Dr nOvi also 4ZDared in Tablet f .rm, and sold under the name of Mother Seigel's Syrup Tablets. Price 25. 9d. per bottle. One Size only.</p>
        <p>Mr. J. Falconer, whom the electors of Forfarshire have just chosen to represent them at Westminster, is a Writer to the Signet in Edinburgh. He is a native of Carnoustie, and still keeps a farm there among his constituents. The vacancy was caused by the elevation to the peerage of Mr. John Sinclair, the Secretary foi Scotland, who is now known as Lord Pentland There were very exciting scenes when the poll was announced, although, in view of the immense majority gained by Mr. Sinclair at the General Election, a Liberal victory was generally anticipated. The defeated candidate was Mr. R. H. Blackburn, K.C.</p>
        <p>The British climate has beaten its own reputation for unreliability. A very short time ago people were enjoying what they called an early spring. This week toboggans, skis, sleighs, and skates have been in requisition, and the country has enjoyed winter weather of the kind which, one had almost begun to believe, existed, so far as this country was concerned, chiefly in the imagination of the writers of Christmas stories. The cold has, no doubt, made matters harder for some unfortunate poor people, but on-the other hand, many willing unemployed have been glad of the chance of a little snow shovelling. Londoners have taken plenty of advantage of the unexpectep opportunity to practice winter sports, and at Hampstead Heath, Parliament Hill, and °trier favourite resorts, the joys of tobogganning and ski-lug, including the spills, have been experienced by hundreds Of young people.</p>
        <p>A great deal of interest is being shown in the First Aid Nursing Yeomanry Corps, Which was to have paraded the West End of London last Saturday, to aid in the recruiting of the Territorial Force. Owing to the snowstorm, however, the parade was postponed for a week. The ladies are commanded by Captain C. Baker, and among the officers is Lady Ernestine Hunt, daughter of the Marquis of Ailesbury, who holds the rank of lieutenant. Their uniform, which is a becoming one of distinctly military appearance, consists of a tight-fitting scarlet tunic with white facings, a navy blue skirt with three rows of. white braid round the bottom, and a scarlet peaked cap, having some resemblance to the Army pattern, with a white band. The object of their corps is to provide trained nurses in time of war, and every member has undertaken to leave the country for active service at twenty-four hours' notice if necessary. At present the corps numbers only fifty, but it is hoped to raise the strength to 1,000. In the illustration members of the Corps are seen at work.</p>
