      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-03-06_WidnesExaminer_art0081" n="art0081">
        <head>PICTURES OF THE WEEK</head>
        <p>PICTURES OF THE WEEK</p>
        <p>THE NEW MEMBER FOR FORFARSHIRE.</p>
        <p>THE RETURN OF WINTER.</p>
        <p>YEOMANRY NURSING CORPS AT WORK.</p>
        <p>AN ARMY</p>
        <p>MARCHES ON</p>
        <p>THE RELIGIOUS WORLD.</p>
        <p>The coming week will witness the greatest Free Church gathering of the year. The season opens at Swansea on Tuesday and the programme outlines a round of meetings which last until Thursday. Perhaps the greatest interest will centre in the addresses on "The Free Churches and Politics" which are to be given by F. B. Meyer and Sylvester Horne. The subject has been freely dis- cussed in the press as the result of a volume published by a South of England Free Church minister whose strictures upon the "ordained politicians" of Nonconformity have been widely read. It is not difficult to anticipate the answer that will be given. Nonconformity has always stood for principles which have brought it into conflict with the existing order of things. "In a state which triedto legislate it out of existence by the Test and Five Mile Acts it was obliged to take notice of things political. It has stood for the application of ethical principles to public affairs; it has aimed at the removal of evils which could only be dealt with by Act of Parliament. With such aspirations Non- conformity must always be at work to influence the electorate. One might reasonably ask how long we should have waited for the abolition of the Slave Trade if it had not been for the "ordained politicians." Confronted with the evils of the Drink Trade and innumerable social wrongs the inheritors of Puritan tradition • must be actively " political " or abandon their ideal for ever.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-03-06_WidnesExaminer_art0082" n="art0082">
        <head>, WARRINGTON CHAPTERS. i k</head>
        <p>, WARRINGTON CHAPTERS. i k</p>
        <p>LEWIS CARROLL AND HIS WONDERLAND. " Alice in Wonderland " is the children's classic. Whether in the future some more engaging figure will be created by the poetic fancy we cannot tell, but for a generation and a half" Alice" has kept her spell. As the writer of so charming a volume Lewis Carroll gained a plate in the annals of literature and drew to himself the interest and affection of unnumbered readers. In a special degree our own interest goes out to him, for his birth place and early home was the Vicarage of Daresbury and his Wonder- land is our own beloved Hill Cliffe. Since everyone has read " Alice " there may be many who will like to read something of his life and of the place where he dreamed the dreams which have made him famous. • _ .</p>
        <p>Charles Lutwidge Dodgson, for that was the true name of" Lewis Carroll," belonged to a north of England family. His father, Charles Dodgson, was born in 1800 and entered the minstry of the Church of England. In 1830 he married Frances Jane Lutwidge and there were born to them eleven children. The living of Daresbury fell to. him and he appears to have zealously discharged the duties of the office. He took to heart the needs of the watermen of the Bridgewater Canal, and on one occasion when walking with Lord Francis Egerton he spoke of his desire to provide some sort of religious meeting place for the men. "If I only had £100," he said, "I would turn one of those barges into a chapel," and at Lord Egerton's request he went on to say how he would construct and furnish it. A few weeks later he received a letter from Lord Francis to say that his wish was fulfilled, and that the chapel was ready. In this curious and decidedly uncommon church—possibly the first of its kind—Mr. Dodgson conducted service and preached every Sunday evening Lewis Carroll was blessed in his parents. In his mother he had a woman of sweet and patient temper, who made sunshine through his childhood days and joined with his father in giving the best that could be given in help and training. DARESBURY VICARAGE. _ _</p>
        <p>Such were the woithy pair who occupied the Daresbury Vicarage in 1832, and here on January 27th their first son was born. _ _</p>
        <p>The Vicarage stands a mile and a half from the village, and its seclusion was complete. To the children even the passing of a cart was a matter of great interest, and they were left to form their own diversion. Always of playful imagination Charles invented the strangest of pastimes. He made pets of the most odd and unlikely animals, and surprised his parents by count- ing certain snails and toads among his closest friends. He carried them toys and gave them the means of warfare in the event of their desiring to enter into quarrels. Earthworms and other burrowing creatures were his especial joy,and he conjured up in imagination thei r doings in their underground houses. Life passed uneventfully at this unusually quiet Cheshire manse. It was well for future generations of boys and girls that it was so, for the boy filled up the gap by an invented Wonderland which will be the delight of children to the end of time.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-03-06_WidnesExaminer_art0083" n="art0083">
        <head>HILL CLIFFE.</head>
        <p>HILL CLIFFE.</p>
        <p>As he grew into boyhood he increased the radius of his travels and reached the charm- ing heights of Hill Cliffe. Thenceforward the pretty hill was his favourite haunt. In the wild life that abounded there he found his paradise. To watch the rabbits and catch a glimpse of the fleet fox were his highest delights and his quick imagination filled up the other scene with other birds and beasts until the hill top teemed. with life. The homes of the true and original Brer Rabbit, the March Hare and the Cheshire Cat, which all the world has smiled over, are on the wooded slopes of Hill Cliffe. Through the sunny years of youth he lived in his Wonderland, re-creating it in after years to set upon the printed page. Lewis Carroll never forgot the scenes of his early life. In a poem which appeared in 1860 in "All the Year Round" he described the sunsets he watched from Hill Cliffe.</p>
        <p>I watch the drowsy night expire And Fancy paints at my desire • Her magic pictures in the fire. An Island farm 'mid seas of corn Swayed by the wandering breath of morn The happy spot where I was born.</p>
        <p>At the -d.ie of twelve he was sent to school at Richmond to be a scholar of Dr. Tait and from thence sent regular issues of a journal he edited under the title " The Rectory Umbrella" for the benefit of his brothers and sisters. He filled his pages with quaint drawings and equally quaint veres much, doubtless, to the delight of those who read them.</p>
        <p>It is interesting to' trace in the works of the child the budding of the genius which marks the man. In Lewis Carroll we see it in the invention of diversions for the younger children of the home, in• the marrionette show he made and manipulated and the maze—said to almost rival that at Hampton Court—laid out in the vicarage grounds. Such are the glimpses one gets of the youth of Lewis Carroll. One would gladly say more, but we must turn to his own "Wonderland "to know the fairy world he built for himself in his childhood years. How WONDERLAND WAS WRIITEN.</p>
        <p>After a successful college course Lewis Carroll took up the teaching profession and in 1855 became tutor in mathematics at Christ College, Oxford. During the summer of 1862 he was enjoy- ing a trip up the river. Three ladies were of the company and were presenting an oft- repeated petition " Tell us a story." Lewis Carroll was a fine story teller and could on the spur of the moment adapt his tale so as to bring in the company to whom he spoke. On this warm afternoon under the shadow of a hayrick at Nuneham he began to spin out the story of " Alice," Mrs. Reginald Hargrraves was Alice; and she" and her two sisters sat and listened to the recital. "Alice's adventures underground" was the title ; later it became " Alice's home in Elfland." Two years later he was prevailed upon by Miss Liddell—one of those who heard it—to write the story out, and it Was then that he attached The title " Alice in Wonderland." He had no idea of publica- tion, but the manuscript came into the hands of George Macdonald, who insisted that it should be shown to a publisher. The circumstance of the summer story on the river are recalled in the poem which stands as a preface; All in the golden afternoon Full leisurely we glide For both our oars, with little skill, By little arms are plied. Thus grew the tale of Wondeiland : Then slowly, one by one, Its quaint events were hammered out And now the tale is done, And home we steer, a merry crew, Beneath the setting sun.</p>
        <p>But Alice and her Wonderland were not created on the Thames. The story crystallized like a flash, as he himself told, but it was the crystallizing of what had long been in mind and heart. Youth's dreams reconstructed themselves and " Wonderland was put into words. •Nothing that be afterwards wrote had any- thing like the freshness or genius. He tried later to frame another such book, and "Alice's Looking Glass" was the result. Of this we need only quote the opinion of a small girl, " Like Alice, but more stupid." ,And that is the last word about it. '</p>
        <p>We will attempt no description of" Alice in Wonderland." Let those who have not read it make amends by a speedy perusal. With the exception of Shakespeare there is perhaps no book so frequently quoted in the daily press, and every one knows what rich fields it has offered to the caricaturist. In 1898 when the " Pall Mall Gazette" arranged an enquiry into the popularity of children's books they said " The verdict is so- natural that it will surprise no normal person. The winner is "Alice in Wonder- land." Though the "Looking Glass" is in the first twenty it is much lower down. Lewis Carroll died on January 14th, 1898, after an attack of influenza. Preaching at Christ Church the Sunday after his death Professor Sanday said • " The world will think of Lewis Carroll as one who opened out a new vein in literature, a new and delightful vein which added at once mirth and refinement to life. May. we not say that there has flowed into the literature of our time a _rill bright and sparkling, health-giving</p>
        <p>and purifying wiierever it</p>
        <p>That cheering rill has flowed through the literature of many nations. Translated into most European languages it has carried joy to children and to all who have child-like hearts, and even in Arabia the children are enraptured by the sparkling story of Alice. If we judge by the American press Alice is as widely known there as here, and we need not say that her name is a household word in the Colonies.</p>
        <p>Nothing perhaps can make Hill Cliffe a better place to us, but if anything can make it dearer it is surely the memory that here on the hill top Lewis Carroll found his Wonderland.</p>
        <p>[An edition of "Alice in Wonderland" and "Alice's Looking Glass" are published by Mac MacMillan at one shilling. They have Sir John Tenniel's illustrations].</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-03-06_WidnesExaminer_art0084" n="art0084">
        <head>OUR MISCELLANY. E „,0</head>
        <p>OUR MISCELLANY. E „,0</p>
        <p>THE FIRST ISSUE OF THE "EXAMINER."</p>
        <p>New eras in the history of a newspaper naturally turn the mind back to its beginning and early history. The p. sent, therefore, may be a fit occasion to record the fact that the " Examiner " began its career almost forty years ago on April 24th, 1869. Strengthened by the Liberal victory in the election of 1868, the party resolved to issue a journal for the advocacy of Liberal prin- ciples. Straightforward advocacy it was to be, as the leading article made clear, and as was blazoned forth in the Shakespearian quotation which followed the title: "Nothing extenuate, nor set down in malice."</p>
        <p>** * *</p>
        <p>In view of the programme to which the "Examiner" has always steady adhered, it is significant that the two outstanding news items are the report of a lecture on "Universal Peace" and of the annual festival of the Stockton Heath Total Abstinence Society. This last-named society after forty years of strenuous and difficult work retained its place as the head and centre of the Temper- ance Movement and the reports of the crowded meetings and eloquent addresses in old Providence Chapel reveal the vigour it still possessed. The only surviving member Of the team of temperance orators who addressed the multitude that day is Mr. John Naylor. _</p>
        <p>** * *</p>
        <p>Those festival days of the Abstinence Society were occasions much more stirring than our modern celebrations—the Licensing Bill notwithstanding. The teetotallers used to march in defiance round the brewery and compass its walls as the walls of Jericho were compassed. This was not to be taken quietly, and a small army of employees and devotees were engaged to line the road and ostentatiously swallow beer from great jars as the procession passed. On one occasion a brewery drayman performed the feat of balancing a huge jug of beer. upon his head. A leader of the procession flirted a stray marble at the jug and managed to hit it in the centre. Strange to say the jug split and left the acrobat deluged in his favourite liquid. And thi was not the only occasion when festivity narrowly escaped becoming tragedy. ** * *</p>
        <p>Antiquarian taste was met by a • very interesting article which enshrined the autobiography of Mr. William Leigh, an adventurous Warringtonian, born at Arpley House in 1770. After an apprenticeship to sail cloth weaving the courageous youth joined the Navy and served through the Napoleonic wars. One is glad . that the narrative is preserved, and appreciates the wisdom of the Editor who gave space to the stirring life story of this courageous youth. ** * *</p>
        <p>The faculty for perpetrating a bull is not the peculiar property of the Irishman. At a Warrington meeting the other evening a speaker was expressing vigorous regret that the "Daily News" should have inserted an advertisement of Scotch whisky. "It is a thing to be deplored" he said "and we must put in a scotch." The idea of putting in a scotch to keep out the Scotch greatly tickled the audience, but the speaker knew exactly what was meant. * * *</p>
        <p>The difference between United States traditions and our own receives constant and curious illustration. An announcement that a British Prime Minister would immediately upon relinquishing his high office become contributing Editor of a weekly journal would fall curiously on English ears. This, however, is what happened in the case of Mr. Roosevelt, who left the White House on Thursday, and we have before us a copy of the " Outlook " containing his first articles. A contribution on "Statesmanship and Character" comes very happily from such a man and makes good reading: "A nation must be judged in part by the character of its public men, not merely by their ability but by their ideals and the measure in which they realize these ideals; by their attitude in private life, and much more by their attitude in public life, both as regards their conception of their duties toward their country,and their conception of the duty of that country, embodied in its government, toward its own people and toward foreign nations. One function of any great public leader should be to exert an influence upon the community at large, especially upon the young men of the community."</p>
        <p>•,,,.,\.,.,.• MY LIFE STORY</p>
        <p>THE MAYOR (ALDERMAN W. J. FORSHAW, J.P.)</p>
        <p>It is a source of pride to me to be able to call myself a Warringtonian. I was born in a cottage in Church-street, opposite the National Schools, in the year 1855, and was the eldest boy of a family of nine, my parents being James and Rachael Forshaw. For some time my father was employed as an engineer at Cockhedge Factory and afterwards he did similar work at Messrs. Platt's, Manchester, and also at the old Warrington Foundry, now owned by Messrs. Smethurst, in Foundry-street. Before her marriage my mother was a teacher at the National Schools. Her father, George Muir, came to Warrington from Muirkirk along with Mr.Alderson, glass manufacturer, and Mr. Peter Walker who established the brewery in King-street. My grandfather, George Muir, was also an engineer and brought out a patent which he called Penketh's patent tue iron. He also provided the moulds for the bottles which were sent out to the Army during the Crimean War. Church-street in my boyhood days presented a rural aspect, being somewhat similar to the main street of Frodsham. Milk carts were not used at that time, the milk being taken round in tankards carried by donkeys. There was a farm in Church-street called Mather's Farm at which I used to spend many pleasant hours. In view of the great development of the town and its local government, it is interesting to me to recall the days when Warrington had only three policemen. Mc. Michael, who lived on School Brow, was the chief and his second officer was nick-named "Jog-along." Practi- cally the whole of the cottages in Church- street were thatched. There was a green- grocer's shop kept by "old Tommy Traddell," and other prominent tradesmen were Quarmby, the shoemaker, Schofield, the glazier (whose son achieved distinction in the world of art), Gerrard, the sign painter, "Toffee " Lythgoe, who, as the nick-name implies, kept a sweet shop, and Cooper, a blacking-maker. On the site now occupied by part of Messrs. Rylands Bros.' works stood the public baths—a great contrast to the present up-to-date structure. The Workhouse was also in Church-street, close to where Mr. Gandy's shop is now. An event of which I have a vivid recollection was the Church-street fair. It was the custom in those days to open it with a fight between Tommy Traddell and old Peter Lawless, who kept a fish shop, and exciting bouts they were! The fair was quite the red letter occasion of the year and the booths used to extend from the big lamp near the Parish Church to the running pump at the top of the Bongs (now Mersey-street.) My early education was received at the National schools, Mr. Grey being the head- master during the first part of my stay there. He was a very good schoolmaster, though somewhat severe, and we all respected him. It will be interesting to many of the teachers and scholars of the present day to know that one of the pupil teachers—John Percival— was the elder brother of one of his Majesty's Inspectors of schools who is well known in Warrington. Other pupil teachers I recollect were James Gandy, now an architect in St. Helens, and Benjamin Senior who holds a rectory near Dublin. I well remember Mr. Dewhurst's advent at the school, his great interest in cricket and the early morning walks which he enjoyed with James Morris (the Pawnbroker) and his dogs. There is one boyish adventure which still lingers in the memory. At that time there was a wooden belfrey in the Parish Church yard and one winter's night I went there, probably to help the ringers. Hours passed and I had not returned home, so naturally my parents became very anxious as to my whereabouts. They searched high and low, but it was not until three o'clock in the morning that they found me, fast asleep, in that old belfry,</p>
        <p>The year 1864 saw a change in my pros- pects, for I was elected a foundation scholar, of the Warrington Blue Coat School. I have always looked upon that institution as a benign mother and cannot sufficiently express my indebtedness for the excellent training I received there. Dr. Bowes, the headmaster, was a splendid teacher and disciplinarian. He was well-known throughout the king- dom as a compiler of several geographies and "Bowes' catechism." One of the greatest pleasures of my school life was derived from membership of the band, of which I ulti- mately became leader. As a boy of sixteen, I also played in the Volunteer Band, Mr. Oakden being the bandmaster. Camping was very different then from what our present "citizen soldiers" are accustomed to, for instead of visiting some distant resort, our tents were pitched in Lodge-lane. As to the educational side of my work at the Blue Coat School, I succeeded in carrying away many- first class prizes at the examinations, especi- ally for mathematics and composition. A great celebration in which I took part as a National schoolboy was the processsion of school children on the occasion of the marriage of our present King in 1863. We all assembled in a field right opposite the Town Hall, where the old Post Office was subsequently built. Referring to this field reminds me that the first Warrington Football Club, of which I afterwards became a member, used to play there, and some fine games we had ! For five years .I remained at the Blue Coat School, and then came the important step of deciding the lines of my future career. For six months I lived at the R •ctory and Mr. Queckett offered me er y facility with a view to ntering the Church. Both the Rector aid Dr. Bowes had a desire that I should ome a clergyman, but .my own inclinato -s wrsr,- for a commercial life. The late Al 1, /. Joseph Davies, a friend of our family. w o carried on a large accountancy</p>
        <p>practice, was favourably' impressed with my composition and other school work,and offered me a place in his office, telling me that "if I would be a good lad, he would make a gentleman of me." I entered Mr. Davies's office in September 1869, and during the nine years spent there I received a sound com- mercial training which has stood me in good stead ever since. Your readers will perhaps be interested to know that I assisted Alder- man Davies in the negotiations for the pur- chase of the Warrington Town Hall and also of the Corporation Gas Works. In 1878 Mr. Brook (a former Town Clerk of Warrington) offered me an appointment as accountant to the firm of Messrs. Brook and Davies, solicitors, and I accepted it with a view to taking up the legal profession. In 1890 I became a solicitor,—the first from the Blue Coat School.</p>
        <p>Municipal work had long had an attraction for me, and in the year 1900 I entered the Council as a representative of the Town Hall Ward, after a contest with my friend Coun- cillor Singleton. I do not mind confessing that as a youth I had the ambition to become at some future time the Mayor of my native town, and I was naturally proud and grati- fied when the Town Council in November last did me the great honour of electing me to the Civic chair.</p>
        <p>Warrington has made great strides within my recollection—in fact it is sometimes difficult to trace in the busy commercial town of to-day the old Warrington as I knew it in years gone by. I remember when there was no such thoroughfare as Parker-street, and when Bank Quay Station was an exceedingly primitive affair, the accommoda- tion consisting of a wooden platform on each side of the line. The Patten Arms Hotel then occupied the building now used as a Railway Institute. In Buttermarket-street, Allen's factory stood on the site of the present St. Mary's Church. As further showing how the town has progressed, I can recall the day when Warrington had a very small market place, only one newspaper, "The Advertiser," and about four doctors. Lovely-lane, too, was a lovely lane indeed, and Bewsey was regarded as the "West End" of Warrington. Church matters have, of course, occupied a large share of my attention and I sang in the Parish Church choir when the present Town Clerk was a member and Mr. Eaude was the organist. Becoming more closely associated with St. Paul's parish, I used to conduct services in the old barn which served as a mission before St. Barnabas' Church was built. When that •church was opened I frequently read the lessons there.</p>
        <p>If I were asked what is the gTeat hope of my Mayoralty, I should say that it is to see the provision of a good Warrington Bridge— one that is worthy of the town and of the people—because I feel sure that an adequate structure would attract more business to Warrington. The greater facilities we offer, the greater prosperity we shall enjoy. But if we tinker with these questions and stint the town,we can only expect to be dealt with accordingly. Another of my strong desires is to see a Law Society formed in Warrington, because I believe it would be to the advan- tage of the administration of the law and bring the professional brethren into closer touch with each other.</p>
        <p>[The above auto-biographical sketch may be supplemented with the following particulars :—The Mayor was for many years a Churchwarden at Holy Trinity Church, and is now people's warden at Christ Church, Latchford. After his election as Mayor he appointed the Vicar (Rev. G. C. Briggs) as his chaplain. He is president of the Blue Coat Brotherly Society, having succeeded the late Alderman Davies, and has been continu- ously in office since that gentleman's death in 1895. He is the first president of the Warrington Law Clerks Society, president of the Warrington Cricket, Bowling and Tennis Club, a member of the Board of Management and a life governor of the Infirmary, a governor of the Grammar School and manager of the Warrington Sayings Bank. He has been identified with Freemasonry for several years, now occupying the post of W.M. in the Lodge of Ch:rity. In September, 1881, he married Miss Bessie Barlow, and his family con- sists of one son (who is a solicitor and a partner in the firm of Forshaw. and Forshaw, Suez-street) and two daughters. With regard to the Mayor's work as a solicitor, it is interesting to note that he carried through the first public flotation of a limited company in Warrington—that of Messrs. Fletcher, Russell &amp; Co., Ltd., gas engineers.]</p>
