      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-03-06_WidnesExaminer_art0085" n="art0085">
        <head>Song of Starting</head>
        <p>A Song of Starting</p>
        <p>Onee more 1 seize my willing pen, My Pegasus is waiting, And boldly I go forth again With wonder una bating; My books are round me, as of old, In the soft firelight gleaming, To point me to the realms of gold, And answer to ms dreaming. No bolts or bars restrain the steed ; Nis willing feet may wander From manger sweet and dewy meed</p>
        <p>Up to the still stars yonder ; His shining hoofs their sparks may fling From local boulders gritty, Or terribly, like Sleipner's, ring Right through the Golden City. And, so untiring is the breed, His willing feet will take me Wherever my young dreams may lead,</p>
        <p>And, for the- moment, make Me— A humble poet, village born— The heir of al the ages, My lamp the olden light of morn,</p>
        <p>-The linstain—ed stars my pages. And I may learn great songs unsung, May visit worlds untravelled, And hear, by the archangels young,</p>
        <p>Great ray-steries unravelled; My room is small, the world is broad,</p>
        <p>-The universe is wider, And shining hosts of worlds untrod,</p>
        <p>These all-await the rider. Alas! my faltering tongue will fail</p>
        <p>To tell' one hall'ihe siory ; It cannot paint the lily pale, Or tell the sunflower's glory, Or find the magic silver words To frame one ivory bosom; I envy the unconscious birds</p>
        <p>_ Who sing on every blossom. And still I cannot choose but sing While Dawn reveals its splendour,</p>
        <p>While swallows pass on silent wing, And women's eyes are tender, And babies lisp, and bluebells bloom, And lovers' vows are. plighted, And ocean prophesies of Doom, And the great stars are lighted. 0 Thou, whose suffering eyes look down And read my inmost being, Inspire my faltering words, and crown My soul with subtler seeing; From Thee my feet have wandered far,</p>
        <p>My heart, sweet Master, never ; Accept my songs, for Thine they are, And make me Thine for ever ! ARTHUR BENNETT</p>
      </div>
