      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-03-06_WidnesExaminer_art0092" n="art0092">
        <head>PHOTO6RAPHIC NOTES.</head>
        <p>PHOTO6RAPHIC NOTES.</p>
        <p>By Veronica.</p>
        <p>THE PHOTOGRAPHIC CONVENTION OF THE</p>
        <p>UNITED KINGDOM'S</p>
        <p>The preliminary circular has just been issued of this year's Convention which will meet at Canterbury from July sto 10. The President this year is Mr. H. Snowden Ward, F.R.P.S. Excursions have been arranged for Tuesday, Thursday, Friday and Saturday, including many interesting towns such as Rye Rochester and Winchelsea, all of which abound with subjects for the camera. Special facilities will be given to members for photographing in the enclosed parts of the Cathedral. This will be a great acquisi- tion as, under ordinary circumstances, a fee of is. is usually charged. The Convention is opened to all photographers for the nominal subscription of 58. 'Those who can see their way to include this time in their holiday should write to the Honorary Secretary, Mr. F. A. Bridge, East Lodge, Dalston Lane, E.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-03-06_WidnesExaminer_art0093" n="art0093">
        <head>WASTE 'PfUNTS,</head>
        <p>WASTE 'PfUNTS,</p>
        <p>The accumulation of unmounted prints is a fairly frequent occurrence with many amateurs, and what to do with them is a- subject that sometimes arises. There are many purposes to which can be put, one being by mounting them for the decoration of the top of an occasional table. To do this it is necessary to have a table that can be rendered quite smooth, and after planing should be rubbed down with glass paper and then varnished. The prints should be trimmed into various sizes so that they may be formed up into some mosaic style, or a very good effect can be obtained by jumbling a lot of photo- graphs together, one overlapping another. To mount these thin glue does very well. After the monteud prints are quite dry they should then be given saveral coatings of varnish. Another use to which these prints can be put is the making of small pin- trays. For this purpose glass bottomed trays can be obtained at any photographic dealers. The photo- graph being squeezed into contact with he bottom of the tray, and as glass is on top of the photograph the use of this as a pin-tray does not affect. the photo- graph. Many years ago whdt were known as opaline views were very much in vogue. These consisted of photo- graphs being mounted in optical contact with glass, and afterwards mounted on plush covered blocks. No better way for the preservation of a photograph can be found as the surface of the print is protected from the air, and consequently of all gas fumes which so often have a deleterious effect. A modification of this opaline process is very useful when anyone has spare unmounted prints, although special glass, known as opaline glass, is obtainable, quite effective mounting can be made with any ordinary glass or waste nega- tives may be cleaned and used up in this way. A solution of gelatine is required made up of Bozs. of soft gelatine dissolved in 20ozs of water. Liquify this with the aid of gentle heat by standing the vessel in hot water, then thin down with warm water until the solution is only slightly thicker than ordinary water. Both print and glass must be immersed in the solution, left there for a little time for the print to straighten out and then take both print and glass out of the solution while they are still in contact and squeeze any surplus solution off with a flat rubber squeegee. When dry bind the edge of the glass with passe part out binding, not forgetting to put a protective backing of card board, to which rings with tapes have been affixed for hanging up by. COMBINED TONING- AND FIXING- BATH.</p>
        <p>The combined bath appeals to many workers on account of its simplicity and also because of its saving of time. There is, however, one great draw- back, and that is the risk of exhausting all the gold while the bath continues to tone, but it is a sulphur tone and is very unstable. There are many photo- grapher who consistently use the combined bath and by carefully estimating the number of prints they are toning are a ''rle to mix their bath with enough gold to ensure corect work. In a recent article in" Photo- graphic Scrips," a little monthly booklet issued free by Ilford Limited, Mr. Henry W. Bennett deals with this subject. One thing that he specially emphasises is that the bath should only be used once. The formula that he recommends is as follows ;</p>
        <p>, 'A. Hypo 1 lb. Water 30 ozs. B. Ammonium sulpho-cyanide Bozs. Water to C. Lead acetate 1 oz. Water Bl. ozs. D. Gold Chloride l5 gr. Woter to . E. Strong liquid ammonia Woter</p>
        <p>Take one ounoc of A.and one draclun each of B.C.D. and E. adding sufficient water to make the bulk up to 31 ozs. Care must be taken that these solutions are mixed in the order given. This is sufficient quantity to tone 10 quarter plate size pieces of P.O.P. The prints are put in direct without any washing and kept moving about turning them over and over for twelve to fifteen minutes. This will give a good purple tone. Should a warmer tone be preferred it can be obtained by using a little less of B.C. and D. A note here may be appropriate as to measuring gold chloride. No doubt many amateurs are ignorant as to the purchase of gold chloride. It is usually sold in 75 gr. hermetically sealed glass tubes or bulbs, and it is very easy to measure out small quantities when it is mixed in solution. The simplest way is to have a glass stopper bottle, and after having soaked the paper label off the tube, drop it into the bottle, then shake it until the glass breaks. Add to this, thirty drachms of distilled water. The gold is readily dissolved, and can afterwards be measured out in a graduated measure, for two drachms there- fore represents one grain of gold. This stock solu- tion of gold should be kept out of the daylight as otherwise the gold is likely to be deposited on the side of the bottle.</p>
        <p>rr IHE PHOTOGRAPHIC AI ONTHLY.</p>
        <p>PRICE 3D.</p>
        <p>POST FREE, 4ip.</p>
