      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-03-06_WidnesExaminer_art0095" n="art0095">
        <head>DEATH OF THE VICAR OF GREAT BUD WORTH.</head>
        <p>DEATH OF THE VICAR OF GREAT BUD WORTH.</p>
        <p>At Great Budworth, near Northwich, on Sunday the death took place of the vicar, the Rev. Arthur Phidias Holme, one of the oldest clergymen in Cheshire. He was ordained 50 years ago by the Bishep of Norwich, and took a great part in educational and other publio work in Cheshire. He was educated at trinity College and took his degree in 1857, in which year he rowed bow for Cambridge, this being the memorable race in which for the first time a keelless boat was employed. After his ordination Mr. Holme was for eleven years curate at St. Nicholas's Great Yarmouth. and subsequently became curate-in-charge of St. Andrew's Great Yar- mouth. In 1870 he was appointed rector of Tatten- hall, near Chester, and for three years was rurel dean of Malpas. In 1892 he was appointed honorary canon of Chester, and the same year he succeeded the Rev. Dr. Moberley (son of the Bishop of Winchester) as vicar of Great Budworth. Canon Holme was an able educationist. For 20 years he was secretary of the Cheshire Diocesan Board of Education and correspon- dent of the Chester Training College, resigning his position in Eebruary last owing to enfeebled health.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-03-06_WidnesExaminer_art0096" n="art0096">
        <head>LATE MR. T. R. SHEPPERDS' ESTATE.</head>
        <p>THE LATE MR. T. R. SHEPPERDS' ESTATE.</p>
        <p>Search for a Will.</p>
        <p>In the Probate Court on Monday Sir John Bigham heard an application by Mr. Baytord kinstructed by Messrs. Robert Davies and Co., Solicitors, Warrington). in regard to the estate of the late Mr. Tholuas Richard Sbepperd, of Warrington, who died on August 14th, 1908, a bachelor, leaving considerable property. Counsel said that on Mr. Shepperd's death search was made and a will dated June Ist, 1958, was found. The persons interested under that will and the executors were all dead. Advertisements were published to ascertain if there was any other will, and a Mr. John Lea, Keighley, has communicnted sith the solicitors saying that he remembered attesting a will for Mr. Shepperd about the year LBBO. No trace of the draft or anything could be found, and further inquiries would be necessary before the matter could be pro- ceeded with. The question involved was whether the testator left a will or died intestate, und the tracing of the next of kin would mean going back a good distance in the family pedigree. The estate was very substantial one, consisting of stocks and shares, a balance at the bank, and real estate producing about £6OO a year, together with lessehold property which was let. Some of the property was required for street improvements, and in these circumstances it was necessaiy that a grant should be made, one of the three persons suggested being s Walter Shepperd, a relation, the only person who had anything to do with the dead man or his estate and the other a Mr. Wolsteuholme and a Miss Renhani.</p>
        <p>Sir John Bigham made a grant to three persons named in the application.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-03-06_WidnesExaminer_art0097" n="art0097">
        <head>HAUNTING FEAR OF OPERATION. – Woman's Joyful Rescue by Zam-Buk.</head>
        <p>HAUNTING FEAR OF OPERATION. - Woman's Joyful Rescue by Zam-Buk.</p>
        <p>" Zam-Buk has saved my leg and made me one of the happiest women in the Potteries," said Mrs. Toole, of 4, Orford Street, Porthill, Staffs., to a " Crewe Chronicle" reporter. "Eighteen years ago," continued she, "when I had a young family, my legs became very painful and the viens much inflamed. They remained in this dis- tressing condition for some time, and ulcers formed on my right leg. One sore spread to another, until my leg from knee to foot was a mass of discharging ulcers.</p>
        <p>"A doctor gave me ointments and lotion's, and I afterwards tried all kinds of ordinary preparations; but my leg got so bad that I went into the infirmary. I was a puzzle to the cleverest doctors. After lying on my back for weeks in the infirmary undergoing special treatment I was allowed to go home. "When I got home I was put straight to bed, and after another private doctor had attended me without any good result I was taken back to tha infirmary. 1 had further special treatment, and wets then sent home again, but I was soon back in the infirmary for the third time. "My leg was then as black as charcoal. I was told dint mortification had set in, and that my only hope was to have the limb amputated. The haunting fears of the operation reduced me to desperate straits. "It occurred to me that as I had often heard of Zam-Buk I should at least give that balm a trial before consenting to the proposed operation. I got a large box and smeared Zam-Buk all over the ulcers, which practically covered my leg. That night I slept for the first time after many weary weeks. The intense irritation and smarting pain were lulled, and in a week or so had passed away altogether. I used Zam- Buk very freely, for the nightmare of amputation haunted me continually, and I spared no pains to save my leg. Slowly but surely Zam-Buk checked the disease, the mortified and discoloured flesh was cleared away, and new healthy skin was made to grow. The great holes in my leg closed up and were then covered with a new skin until the limb was healthily built up again: _ _</p>
        <p>"The neighbours could hardly credit it when they saw me once more getting about. They marvel to see me doing my housework as though my legs had never ailed anything."</p>
        <p>INFLUENZA EPIDEMIC. NUMEROUS CASES REPORTED FROM ALL • OVER THE KINGDOM. Reports from all quarters indicate that the influenza scourge is once more in our midst We all know the serious nature of this disease, and it is therefore hardly necessary to warn our re,adersagainst treating it lightly as of no more consequence than a severe cold. The advent of influenza is always sudden and is usually attended with high temperature. The only safe rule for those attackeJ is to go to bed at once and to send for the doctor. It is the part of wisdom to take all posible precaution to guard sgainst influenza, and nothing is better for this purpose than a good general tonic, especially for those who are delicate. One of the best tonic-preventatives is Angier's Emulsion, which, as the advertisements proclaim, is both "healing and strengthening." This remedy is easy to take, is most soothing to the lungs, and is a tonic to appetite and digestion. It is a great favourite with the medical profession, especially for the cough of influenza,and for building up after the attack. A few simple rules for aaerting influenza are : Wear warm clothing, eat nourishing food, avoid the close, stuffy atmosphere of crowded rooms.</p>
        <p>The Great Eastern Railway Company have given orders for the whole of their steamers on the Harwich- Antwerp service to be fitted with the same system of Wireless Telegraphy as is on their Hook of Holland steamers.</p>
      </div>
