        <p>OFFICIALS' SALARIES. leYbe omission ft om the year book of the Corporation 'tied at the beginning of this yeir of the usual list of tnlnties of officials, prompted Councillor Pemberton to tiuntice of the following motion : "That slips be hted suitable for insertion in the Year Bonk and -tPlied to each member of the Council showing the ies of each official and their respective staff." he.TowN CLERK said that if the book was simply tostributed amory. the members of the Council there nuld he no objection to the course suggested, but many uldhe books were sent outside. sh n the undertaking that every member of the Council ..luoluclnebieturrnished with the slip required, Councillor In Ordno ' .ernberton withdrew the resolution PKMBERTON then move'd "That Stand- and r,„ et's Nos. 38 and 29 as appear in Standing Orders, for on November 11th, 1901, shall again come line e,.,", except that .the word February 'in last effect o'r der 38 shall be altered to October.' "—The Intertitse the amendment would be that all proposed Bof salaries would come up for consideration Pernh o,3„tnher meeting of the Council. Councillor Orders the PiOinted out that under the present Standing the stimates a r a -n-g-eY,ueandt salaries raised at different times and e made a considerable difference to the he . If -- would be far better at the question of v I salaries Shouldthat come forward at one time as before. "I:t.uLipvatiesnncit(l)iinieltrules in other towns and would be much wore o ifriodrodptseedcoinndieNtda.rrington. or L S°uncillor BIRTLES said he could not understand Wrist • the C ouncillor Pemberton was driving at. This was time for the Borough Treasurer to make up his tleounts for the ensuing year, and it was,treforien, oento°erotuneevteimr etifieorret‘hveolsiteiaLleessotnoleb writing discussed. on the nts• r, however, The elections would be coming on the salaries would be something to talk about. ,atighte t ,0. TINNION contended that it would be better to the present Standing