        <p>"AN ODD JOB." itted" Lae nlinutes of the Watch Committee were sub taihieci councillor LORD said the private agenda con- fSociet:Al.ference to a letter from the Amalgamated O.! Carpenters and Joiners as to some joinery Rothl at "al Police Station. He presumed there was „Zile 14(),t a private nature in the letter. joICI'OR : It was a case of an odd man doing an The tn. inaltes were approved.</p>
        <p>4, SHOP HOURS ACT. „Lee ti -"eeting of the Health Committee a letter frpru urde artle °nice with reference to the proposed Closing it Nui:lleder the Shop Hours Act, 1904, %vas.read,. and, the ,7' re6elved, that owing to the objections received te `4"eniittee are of opinion that it is not expedient , troeee.., . with the ,Order, and that the the Home ThAltbe informed accordingly. wer to Councillor Lord liter - N CLERK said that some of the hairdressers cou? dissatisfied and objected to the Order. They dealing 5I 11(4 nil agree, and the Committee were tired of , with e matter. the minuteths of the Committee were approved. thThe fo 1 lowiM mePort of t °EtE BOWLING GREENS. Prop , rhe Borough Surveyor on e ...A • 110,1: sites for bowling greens, inspected by the Viet lug pCheea , Sub-conirnittee, was read :—One in 00_,:erpoasttiorn. ntft, Latchford Ward ; one at the recreation cooeed, Live riD°ol-rond, Sy Austin's Ward ; one on land, Winwidc-road, formerly Grace's mLtec!Y, Orford Ward e •on on land St. Elph in's Park, Le ro ..,................2d al F • .2_ ;aeld Ward. In each case the</p>
        <p>- A CHILDS' PARALYSIS- Little Boy Helpless. Three tiospitais fair. Cured by DR. CASSELL'S TABLETS. ----- Mrs. Watts, of Sheep-street-lane, Etchingham,Sussex, 'Writes Eighteen months ago my little boy developed Infantile Paralysib. He was so bad that he could not stand, so I took him to a hospital, /vas when he Put in a plaster of Paris jacket, but he was too Verik to wear it. Then I got him into another hospital, 'Where he remained a long time., and yet another, but he TAMSgot 110 better, so I took him home. He was practi- ' helpless when I began Dr. Cassell's Tablets, but II three months he could run about, and is now quite All consider his cure a miiacle." ndr•reettarr,ssewleras.it'ul'aessbl,ets can be got at any chemists for ls. lid. and 2s. 9d.,. and are a perfect cure for troubles. and nerve stomach and kidney</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-03-06_WidnesExaminer_art0102" n="art0102">
        <head>Examiner, Saturday</head>
        <p>The Examiner, Saturday</p>
        <p>ground is suitable when the sites are levelled and formed. I consider the cost of each green,the same size as the one in Victoria Park, including fencing round, pavilion, planting and approaches, will cost about £520, each exclusive of land. According to the return at Victoria Park Bowling Green, the equipment will cost about £22; the annual expenditure £B2, and the annual receipts £46. The actual expenditure will be £36. In moving the adoption of the minutes, Alderman BOLTON said that if the Council would let the Com- mittee know what they wanted, there would now he a chance of going on with the work and completing it.</p>
        <p>Councillor Pemberton said he did not mind having , all the blame put upon his shoulders—in fact he would accept it as an honour, but it would not be fair. He would be taking too much credit to himself. The General Purposes Committee very properly would not allow the course suggested by the Museum committee to be done. He would feel very flattered if he thought he had the command of the Council that Coun- cillor Birtles attributed to him. Explaining the reason for his min-attendance at meetings, Councillor Pember- ton said he was thoroughly disgusted with the Museum Committee. 'I hat institution was not run—and he said it fearlessly—in the interests of the reading public. The committee was there to cater for the public, to find books for them, and they ought to give the public what they wanted instead of spending very large sums in valuable books which were placed on the shelves. The Mevort : Tint ie not the question before the meeting. Councillor PEMBERTON : You have allowed COUR- oillor Birtles to attack me and my conduct as a mem- ber of the committee. Continuing he said tlAt hundreds of pounds worth of books had never been taken down from the shelves from the year they were placed there until now. Then people came repeatedly for other books which were in demand and could not get them. The committee was there to give the public what they wanted and not what some of the fanatics wanted. (Laughter). With regard to this tender, as a custodian of public money he could not vote for the placing et a contract with a man who wanted .£4O more for the same job. He would have taken the same action in the case of any other firm. The minutes were approved.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-03-06_WidnesExaminer_art0103" n="art0103">
        <head>CONCESSION TO THE SLOT METER USERS.</head>
        <p>CONCESSION TO THE SLOT METER USERS.</p>
        <p>At a meeting of the Gas Committee it was resolved that the Gas Engineer be authorized to make such alterations to the slot meters within the borough as will enable them to participate in the recent reduction in the price of gas. Alderman TINNION, in proposing the adoption of the minutes, said the Engineer's department were going on with this work, so that it would be completed as early as possible. Alderman BOLTON seconded and the minutes were agreed to. CHILDREN'S GYMNASTIC DISPLAY.</p>
        <p>Alderman TINNION gave a cordial invitation to the members to attend the children's display to be held at the Gymnasium on March 29th. The committee had resolved that the Mayor be invited to take the chair and the Mayoress to distribute the medals.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-03-06_WidnesExaminer_art0104" n="art0104">
        <head>LIGHTING OF THE MAIN STREETS.</head>
        <p>THE LIGHTING OF THE MAIN STREETS.</p>
        <p>Councillor ASHCROFT called attention to the lighting of Sankey-street, and said several tradespeople had complained to him about it. For seven or eight weeks past the street had nearly been in darkness. Councillor LORD said there was no doubt that the lighting of the town by the Electricity Committee was scandalous for the price they paid fcir it. If some striking change was not forthcoming, the Street Improvement Committee would be strongly inclined to revert to modern gas—(hear, hear)—which would be found more economical.</p>
        <p>- Councillor Dr. JOSEPH said to judge from the com- plaints the Electricity Committee was the most incompetent they had. There ought to be a certain amount of loyalty and such complaints should be made to the committee, and not blazoned out in the Council. If he were thinking of having electricity in his house such statements might influence him in .coming to a decision. Councillor LORD said his remarks only alluded to public street lighting, not private lighting. Councillor PEMBERTON said there had been repeated complaints huring the past five years and there was a strong opinion that it would be better to light the streets by gas. Councillor WILKINSON thought matters like these ought to be b-ought to the notice of the chairmen of committees, and members should not come the, e for the sake of being a wind bag. (Laughter). The committee were almost doing the lighting for nothing, but if they presented the Street Improvement Com- mittee with the standards and the electricity free of charge, he believed they would ask for a bonus. (Laughter). The minutes were adopted. A CONTRACT QUESTION.</p>
        <p>The Electricity and Tramways Committee resolved that the offer of Messrs. Walter Scott and Company Of ten tons of.rails at £7 per ton be accepted. Councillor LORD asked why tenders had not been advertised for, as the amount was over £2O. The DEPUTY MAYOR explained that the rails of the Warrington tramway wore of a certain gatige and they could not be purchased in less quantities than 100 tons. Another Corporation, however, had a surplus after sup- plying their requirements and the firm asked the Com- mittee if they would purchase them. The Committee decided to buy ten tens and store them until they were required. Alderman BOLTON said he did not know that there were any extensive repairs in prospect. ' The TOWN CLERK: If ie the course of the next five years we wanted ten tons of rails we could not get them unless we placed an order for 100 tons. Councillor WILKINSON.—We can by paying extra. In answei. to Councillor Broadhurst, the DEPUTY- MAYOR said the rails were of British manufacture. Councillor LORD moved that the minute be referred back. Why in the world they had been purchasing special gauges .and confined themselves to one firm, he could not understand. There was a mystery about it. _</p>
        <p>The DEPUTY-MAYOR.—That is your ignorance. (Laughter). If we wanted to extend the tramway to the top of Bridge-street, and required a few tons of rails, we should have to buy 100 tons. Councillor PEMBERTON considered the suggestion of the committee was preposterous. He did not believe they had got a type of rail which they could only obtain irons one firm. _ _</p>
        <p>The DEPUTY-MAYOR said that when the rails were laid down the Government btandard had not been fixed. Now it had been adopted, but unfortunately their gauge was outside it. Councillor PEMBERTON.:—BUt there are any amount, of people making rails and ours is not the only tram- way in the world with this gauge.</p>
        <p>- Councillor WILKINsoN said several towns were experiencing a difficulty in replacing rails. Alderman BURTON said he could not understand why the manufacturers should not keep a stock of the rails.</p>
        <p>Councillor BROADHURST, while not opposing the present purchase' said it ought not to be taken as a precedent for the future. Councillor PEMBERTON asked for the Town Clerk's ruling as to whether the contract should have been advertised.</p>
        <p>The TOWN CLERK said this case did not come within the meaning of the Standing Orders. The Orders could not apply to their largo commercial undertakings, as they were compelled by law to carry on those concerns. Councillor LORD: • Where is the urgency 'in the matter?</p>
        <p>The TOWN CLERK: There is no urgency. It is an offer made to the Committee.</p>
        <p>The voting was as follows :—For the amendment: Aldermen Bolton and Burton, Councillors Barton, Skelland, Chantler, Lord, Pemberton, Lashford, Broad- hurst., Singleton-10. Against: The Mayor, Aldermen Smethurst, Tinnion and Ellison, Councillors Ashcroft, Wilson, Fulton- Smith, NVilkinsen, Dr. Joseph, Sankey, Hawthorn, Birtles and Rogers-13. The amendment was therefore defeated, and the minutes were approved.</p>
        <p>A COSTLY CLERICAL ERROR. RAtERAY hRS LOSE £876. _</p>
        <p>On the minutes of the General Purposes Committeel Councillor HA wmtroßre drew attention to a report of the Town Clerk contained in the minutes on the private agenda, on the agreere ent with the Newton-in-Makerfleld Urban Council with reference to payment for fever patients in the Corporation Hospital from the Newton area, it was not much use crying over spilt milk, but the ratepayers were entitled to know of the Councillors' faults as well as their virtues. Owing, however, to this matter being placed on the private agenda, there was no chance of the public becoming acquainted with it as there would be were it on the ordinary age,nda. By a clerical mistake the ratepayers had lost over £BOO in regarci to the cost of maintenance of fever patients in the hospital. In 1893 the Council decided to increase their charge to Newton from 10s.. to 15s. per bead per week, but since that date the Corpoiation had not made that charge through some misunderstanding. The result was the ratf.•paye.s had to suffer for that neglect to the extent of nearly a penny rate. If such an oversight had happened in a private firm drastic action would have been taken. 'The Corporation's loss had been the Nee ton Council's gain, and Newton could congratulate themselves on the way they had got out of it. It was difficult, after this lapse of time to apportion the blame. it was a serious mistake, but steps had been taken to prevent a recurrence of it. THE PAINTING OF THE MUSEUM. The General Purposes Committee resolved that the following tenders ior the painting cr the Museum be accepted :--Mr. William Winstanley for the inside at £lO 7s. Gd., Mr. W. Wilcock for the outside at £l3 18s. Councillor BIRTLES pointed out that at the Museum Committee it was decided that the tender of Messrs. Hesketh and Sons for painting, Stn., be accepted as follows :--For inside work, £149 us.; for outside work. .215 10s. This, however, had been withdrawn in view of the General Purposes Committee's action. The tenders sent in to the Museum Committee were con- sidered by eight competent judges who knew what work was required to be done. Councillor Pemberton was a member of the Committee but had only attended four times in sixteen months and he was not present when the Museum Committee considered the tenders. After- wards, however, in the General Purposes Committee, as was usual with him, he upset the whole thing. (Laughter.) All things being equal it did not matter to him (Councillor Birtles) who got the contract but he was not going to he hound to accept the lowest tender regardless of con- dition!. He maintained that the Museum Committee was quite capable of deciding the matter. He did not think it would be possible for a man to execute the work at the loe est price without sustaining loss,and the Committee ought not to encourage that. The unnniraous feeling of the Committee was that Messrs. Hesketh should have the contract. but the General Purposes Committee, though the instrumentality of Councillor Pemberton, over-ruled them. It showed very poor res- pect for a Committee when a man who was a member of it would go against the ruling of his fellow-members. especially without Wing some explanation or attending the committee meeting, -- as he was in duty bound to. He (Councillor Birtles) had a great feeling that since be entered the Council there were only one or two men capable of doing anything—(laughter)—and it was a matter of driving the others. He was not. going to be like a piece of "dumb driven cattle." He must have independence in the Council. Alderman BOLTON said there was no real straight- forward reason given why Messrs. Hesketh's tender was accepted and if had the appearance of favouritism. If the Council had not the option of acepti irg the lower tender, what was the use of advertising? They ought to study the interests of the ratepayers as well as those who tendered. He did not think Councillor Pemberton had anything to do with it. He might have started the discussion by saying the tender was not right but the committee itself should be credited with the responsi- bility for the change. It should not go forth to the public that the committee had been influenced by any single member.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-03-06_WidnesExaminer_art0105" n="art0105">
        <head>LADYSMITH DAY.</head>
        <p>LADYSMITH DAY.</p>
        <p>THE SOLDIERS' MEMORIAL DECORATED.</p>
        <p>Territorials' Church Parade.</p>
        <p>Sunday was the anniversary of one of the most memor- able and joyfully welcomed events of the South African campaign—the relief of Ladysmith. It will be recalled that General Sir George White and his forces and the Naval Brigade, under Rear-Admiral (then Captain) Lambton, whose naval guns rendered great service, were shut up in the beleaguered town from isfovember' 2, 1899, until relieved on February 28, 1900, by the Natal Carbineers and Imperial Horse, under Lord Dundonald. The South Lancashire Regiment played a great and important part in the operations leading to the relief of the garrison, and on Satui day the memorial in the Queen's Gardens to the officers and men who fell in the war was appropriately decorated; the base being covered with moss and wreaths while festoons of ivy were placed round the pedestal. Floral tributes were sent by the following : Mrs. McCarthy O'Leary (widow of the gallant soldier who fell at the head of his regiment at Pieter's Hill), Miss McCarthy O'Leary (daughter) ; Lieutenant- Colonel J. D. Fairclough and officers of the 4th Battalion Prince of Wales Volunteers, South Lanca- shire Regiment; warrant officers, staff sergeants, depot of the • South Lancashire Regiment • warrant officers, staff sergeants and sergeants of the South Lancashire Regiment, Lahore, Punjanb, Captain F. M. Appleton ; officers depot South Lancashire Regiment; Sergeants 3rd (special reserve) Battalion South Lancashire Regiment, Warrington; warrant officers, staff sergeants and sergeants, 2nd South Lancashire Regiment, Buttevant, Ireland ; members of the Sergeants' Mess, 4th Battalion South Lancashire Regiment; an anomyous tribute in the shape of a harp, bearing the words "in memory of." _</p>
        <p>Lacrysmith Day was app' ropriately selected as "Territorial Sunday" and the members of the 4th Battalion (P.W.V.) South Lancashire Regiment, in common with the Territorials throughout the West Lancashire Division, attended divine service in the morning. The church visited—St. Peter's—stands in a working-class neighbourhood and the Territorials marched to it by a circuitous route with a view to attracting recruits. The weather was far from encouraging. There had been snow during the night, the streets were in an unpleasantly sloppy dondition, and when the time for parading arrived a persistent downfall of sleet added to the discomfiture of being out of doors. The muster was consequently somewhat smaller than had been expected, but it speaks well for local Territorial enthusiasm that 250 could be found to brave the elements. At ten o'clock a start was made from the Drill Hall, the band under the direction of Bandmaster Richardson, leading the way and cheering the spirits of the pro- cessionists and onlookers, with some stirring marches. Lieutenant-Colonel S. W. Fairclough was in command and the other officers present were :—Major B. Fair- clough,Capta ins Crosfield,H. G. Roberts and Williamson Lieutenants Monks and Egerton, Fairclough, Second Lieutenants McClure and Henshall, also Captains J. T. Picton and R. M. Roberts of the Transport-Supply Division, Captain and Adjutant G. R. Mott and Sergeant- Major Ingram. The route followed was :—Sankey- street, Liverpool-road, Green-street, Priestley-street, Froghall-lane,Tanner's-lane,Pinner's-brow and Nqrman- street. At the church a large congregation had assembled, including the Mayor (Alderman Forshaw), the Deputy-Mayor (Alderman Smethurst) and Alderman Bennett. The Territorials were met at the church door by the clergy and choir who preceded them up the aisle, the processional hymn "0 God, our help in ages past " being sung. Mr. E. A. Padmore being at the organ. The service was conducted by the Rev. B. Percival (vicar) and the Rev. J. H. Garnett. The following hymns were heartily sung, to the accompaniment of the band, "Soldiers of Christ arise," "Fight the good fight " and " Onward, Christian soldiers." The Rev. B. PERCIVAL preached on the words "Be of good courage and let us play the man for our pe,iple and for the cities of our God, and the Lord do that which seemeth Him good." (11. Samuel, x., 12.) The service concluded with the National Anthem. The Territorials returned to the Drill Ball by way of Norman Street, Orford Lane, Battersby Lane, Church Street, Mersey Street, Bridge Street and Sankey Street.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-03-06_WidnesExaminer_art0106" n="art0106">
        <head>CHILDREN'S SOMMER CAMP.</head>
        <p>CHILDREN'S SOMMER CAMP.</p>
        <p>The Season Reviewed.</p>
        <p>The annual meeting of the committee of the Warring- ton Children's Summer Camp was held in the Meeting Room of tho Old Academy on Tuesday evening, the Rev. W. Bracecnmp presiding.</p>
        <p>TREASURER'S REPORT. The HON. TREASURER (Mr. J. A. liansome) read the balance sheet which shoe ed a balance in hand of £66 12s. 9d. which was a little better than last year. Mr. Ransoms stated that they had during the past year been able to take more children to the camp. The expenditure for each child including food, railway fares, etc.,was 15s. 2d. Some substantial huts had been 'erected which would withstand the 'winds and the sea. The cost of the structures ,were £1 ss. each complete. He would be •pleased to receive donations at any time and he urged the speedy arrival of same. A new feature that was put into operation last summer was the sending of week-end parties to the camp. These parties comprised working youths and boys who were employed at local works. This outing had helped to brace them up and he had heard that they were eagerly looking forward to the next season. On the proposition of Mr. J. LoNGLAND, seconded by Mr. J. H. SmanuttsT, the report was adopted. SECRETARY'S REPORT. The SECRETARY (Mr. W. H. J. Jenkins) 'then read his annual report. It stated that the past season had from almost every p9int of view, been a successful one, ' the number of children sent to camp had been increased from, 385 to 399, comprising 199 girls and 200 boys. The most satisfactory feature of the season's work had bean the greatly increased use of the camp by adult visitors and convalescents from Warrington. The number of these had tisen from 24 in 1906 and 50 in 11907 to 180 in the present season. It was hoped that ' as the advtintages of the camp became better known it would be more widely used and appreciated. Last sear the experiment was tried of opening the camp ft r visitors at Easter. The result was disappointing owing in a great measure to the cold weather. At Whit- suntide and Walking Day, however, a largo number of visitors availed themselves of the privileges offered. As the deficit incurred for establishment expenses had been considerably reduced, and this, in spite of the fact that several amounts had beet' spent on capital expendi- ture. A new and larger cooking stove was necessitated, owing to the additional cooking required for visitors. In addition, owing to the inroads of sand on the Camp ground caused by the north-westerly gales, and in com- pliance with the obligations of the lease, it became necessary to plant the westerly bank with shrubs and evergreens, and make alterations to the fencing on the northerly side adjoining the sea. A considerable sum had been spent in these directions, some 700 shrubs having been planted. This not only affected a per- manent improvement, but afforded additional shelter to the Camp. Personal attention had been given to this, and the work had been done economically and efficiently.' The Committee tendered their grateful thanks to the voluntary superintendents and their staffs :—Girls' superintendent: Mrs. Wilkinson. Assistant officers. Misses Birch, Bottomley, Emson; McGeachin, Pennington, Povey and Whitley. Boys' superintendent (first week) : Inspector Cox; (second week) : Mr. William Bamber. Assistant officers: Misses Cox and Misses Turner, and Messrs. P. F. Boardman, A. Cox, W. Farrar; B. Jolley, C. J. Newton,</p>
        <p>F. W. Povey, S. Ryan and W. Turner. The weather was somewhat broken during part of the season, but many enjoyable walks and excursions were taken part in by the children and officers. The holiday was quite free from accident, special precautions being taken during bathing operations. The Committee desire to express their thanks to the Director of Education (Mr. J. Moore Murray) and his staff, who had again rendered every assistance in the selection and despatch of the children, and to Mr. Charles White, who had again examined and passed the children. Thanks are also due to those who in various ways have assisted the funds of the Camp by special efforts. A pleasing feature of the Camp work was the interest the children themselves were taking. One young lady who took a collecting book handed over no less a sum than .£4 10s., representing contributions from 402 different persons. In other ways, too, the children had shown that they took a real interest in those less fortunately circum- stanced than themselves. The report was adopted. ELECTION OF OFFICERS.</p>
        <p>Lady Greenall was again elected president and the following vice-presidents were chosen :--The Mayor (Alderman W. J. Forshaw), Mrs. Ziba Armitage, Miss Phyllis A. Lee Captain G. R. Crosfleld, Messrs. J. F. Armitage, S. R. Armitage, G. F. W. Armitage' W. T. Bennett, J. J. Crosfield, James Dodds, F. A. Frost, William Griffiths, James Harding, W. N. Hutchinsg, 4. H. Lee, William Long, K. E. Markel, William Owen, J. Charlton Parr ; Committee, Mrs. George Dakin, Mrs. J. H. Ewan, Mrs. W. J. Forshaw, Mrs. T. E. Gibson, Mrs. Roberts, Rev. W. Bracecamp, Inspector Cox, J. Longland, Messrs. J. A. Ransome, J. Wilkinson, T. H. Ashcroft, A. H. Lee, J. Moore Murray, J. W. Parker, J. H. Sinethuist, F. G. Taylor, Clegg, E. J. Holt, and E. Wallington, Mr. John A. Ransoms) was re-elected hon. treasurer, and Mr. NV. H. J. Jenkins, hon. secretary. LETIING THE HUTS. _</p>
        <p>Mr. JENKINS reported that Mr. A. H. Lee had written to him asking if it would not be advisable if the War- rington buts at the Summer Camp, were let to,outsiders while they were vacated by Warrington people. Mr. Jenkins stated that the rules of the Summer Camp when it was inaugurated were that the buts could not be let for any other purpose than for the Warrington people, and considering that fact,they had no voice in the matter now. It was resolved to send a letter to Mr. Lee explaining the orig inn!, arrangements.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-03-06_WidnesExaminer_art0107" n="art0107">
        <head>VOTES OF THANKS.</head>
        <p>VOTES OF THANKS.</p>
        <p>Votes of thanks were accorded to the Secretary and the Treasurer and also to Mr. Craddock, clerk to Mr. Jenkins, and the Chairman.</p>
        <p>A SUGGESTION.</p>
        <p>Mr. J. MOORE MURRAY said that before closing the meeting he would like to make a suggestion, and be thought it would be a good thing if it could be acted upon. It was with reference to workingmen who had just recovered from an illness or an injury and who at present had to go straight to their employment before they were really strong again. It would be well if arrangements were made to send such people for a week or a fortnight to the Camp in order to brace them up, so that they would be in a fit condition to resume work.</p>
        <p>..-.... After some discussion the matter was referred to the Executive Committee.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-03-06_WidnesExaminer_art0108" n="art0108">
        <head>WARRINGTON RURAL DISTRICT COUNCIL.</head>
        <p>WARRINGTON RURAL DISTRICT COUNCIL.</p>
        <p>The Sankey Bridge Question.</p>
        <p>The monthly meeting of this Council was held on Wednesday last in 13ewsey Chambers. Dr. Murray pre- sided, and others present were Msssrs. R. Forshaw, A. Brooks, J. Backhouse, W. Furness, G. Mather, W. Houghton, J. C. Sutton (clerk), [Jr. Szhton (medical officer of health), B. S. Newcombe surveyer) H. H. Burnage (assistant surveyor), and 1. W. Kerfoot (inspector).</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-03-06_WidnesExaminer_art0109" n="art0109">
        <head>SANKEY BRIDGE.</head>
        <p>SANKEY BRIDGE.</p>
        <p>A letter was read from the Clerk of the Lancashire County Council informing this Council that the report of a meeting on the 2nd inst. of the sub-committee appointed to confer with the representative of the Council and Warrington Corporation, was considered by the Main Roads and Bridges Committee of Lancashire County Council at their meeting on the 10th inst, when the following resolution was pused :—"lhat until the Warington Corporation expresses their willingness to contribute a fair proportion of the cost of carrying out an approved -scheme for reconstructing Sankey Canal Bridge, the Committee are reluctantly compelled to take no further action in the matter." Mr. MATHER. Can you tell us why the Corporation will not take any steps in the matter? Mr. FURNESS said the bridge was not within the province of the Corporation, because it was outside the boundary. He was sorry the County Council were taking such steps in trying to throw the responsi- bilities on to the Warrington Corporation. lie thought the County Council ought to be brought to recognise and realise their position as a County Council. and they were shelving the matter. He moved that a letter be sent from this Council protesting against the want of action of the County Council with respect to the bridge. Mr. ATHER seconded.</p>
        <p>The CHAIRMAN said he quite agreed with Mr. Furness that the question was being shelved. He thought they ought to protest against the County Council putting it on one side. Messrs. MATHER, BACKHOUSE, and FORSHA W agreed with the iernarks of Mr. Furness, and Mr. FORSHA W said that the County Council were getting away from what was obviously their moral duty, through a legal quibble, and he strongly protested against their getting out of it in a questionable manner. It was decided to send the following resolution : "That this Council expresses its regret that the County Council cannot .see its way to proceed with the widening of Sankey Canal Bridge, as we are of the opinion that the responsibility of this should be recognized by the County Council and on them should devolve the duty of this much needed improvement. We, therefore, respect- fully ask our Council to reconsider the matter.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-03-06_WidnesExaminer_art0110" n="art0110">
        <head>BRIDGE IMPROVEMENTS WANTED</head>
        <p>BRIDGE IMPROVEMENTS WANTED</p>
        <p>Mr. FORSHAW said the Clerk of the Burtonwood Parish Council had seen him in reference to C•tuseway Bridge at Burtonwood. The Parish Council wanted this Council to try and endeavour to have the bridge widened. Mr. HOUGHTON said tha bridge was the boundary between Winwick and Burtonwood. It was a terrible place. The improvement would be of more advantage to Winwick than to Burtonwood. It was decided that the work should be proceeded with, as soon as they had the money in hand. THE HALF YEAR'S RATE.</p>
        <p>... The CHAIRMAN moved that they should levy a rate for the half yearly rate of 6d. Mr. MATHER objected and said if they adopted a 10d. or a is. rate, it could easily be spent, but if they did not adopt the rate it could not be spent. The CLERK: It cannot he spent without the Council agree. It was decided that there should be a 6d. rate for the half year. ALKALI WORKS INSPECTION. The SURVEYOR reported that he had received a letter from Mr. Pickmere, Town Clerk of Liverpool, with a statement of salary and expenses of the new additional Inspector under the Alkali, etc., Works Regulation Act, 1906.—The amount payable to the Local Government Board, by the contributing sanitary authorities was £l2l 17s. 10d.. and the amount payable by this Council would be £7 ss. 10d. for 15 weeks ending December 31st., the limit of the Council's contributions being £25.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-03-06_WidnesExaminer_art0111" n="art0111">
        <head>LORD ELCHO ON BRAZIL.</head>
        <p>LORD ELCHO ON BRAZIL.</p>
        <p>Lord Elcho, who has hitherto been generally known in the political world, and in connection with his work upon the London County Council, contributes a:nost interesting article to the March issues of the" Financial Review of Reviews" upon the prospects of Brazil. Lord Elcho weighs with care the various evidences in favour of Brazil and its neighbour, Argentina, and he arrives at the conclusion that the Brazilian Republic is at the present time more advantageous as a field for the investment of British capital than any other Republic in South America. We have not space to quote at length any particulars of Lord Eicho's valuable contribution to financial literature, but it assists us in showing that Brazil is rapidly occupying a position among countries suitable for European invest- ment, and therefore commands the careful attention of every investor who desires to spread his capital over the world's area with a fair certainty of safety.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-03-06_WidnesExaminer_art0112" n="art0112">
        <head>PHYSICIANS AND ALCOHOL.</head>
        <p>PHYSICIANS AND ALCOHOL.</p>
        <p>Speaking ate conference in connection with the Lancashire and Cheshire Band of Hone Union in Man- chester on Saturday, Sir William P.Hartley urged the need for temperance teaching in the day schools. He said he had arranged for a special edition to be pub- lished of Sir Walter Horsley's book "Alcohol and the Haman Body," and intended to distribute about 18,000 free copies. Sir Victor Horsley and Dr. Bickerton (Liverpool)delivered verpool)delivered addresses condemnatory of alcohol as being prejudicial to national health and physique.</p>
      </div>
