      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-03-06_WidnesExaminer_art0124" n="art0124">
        <head>QUESTIONS FOR TARIFF REFORMERS.</head>
        <p>QUESTIONS FOR TARIFF REFORMERS.</p>
        <p>XX.—lf Protectionists (1) believe that it ruins a nation to dump manufac- tures on its shores,and (2) desire the Colonies to prosper, why do they ask the Colonies to Prefer " our manufactured goods?</p>
        <p>We are the chief exporters of manufactured goods to the Colonies. The Tariff Reformers believe that, if manu- factured goods are not kept out, a country must be industrially ruined. Yet they always talk of the Colonies as " markets," and urge that we can by the "Preference" sell more goods to the Colonies. IF PROTECTION IS TRUE THE "TARIFF REFORMERS" ARE TRYING TO RUIN THE COLONIES BY BREAKING DOWN THEIR TARIFF WALLS. It is only the Free Ilader who can honestly desire to increase British exports to the Colonies. If tic Tariff-Reformer desves to do so, he con- fesses that he desires to gain advantage by the industrial ruin of the Colonies.</p>
        <p>.A. I .04t 0 (4 Itt I°s 117 - t„,ter, • 115 431., to the -, "-. • , _ t • • E 3' ITO • • 34w.5t4.•</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-03-06_WidnesExaminer_art0125" n="art0125">
        <head>Distress.</head>
        <p>The Distress.</p>
        <p>Sir,—Kindly allow me to thank those kind friends for the gift of 100 loaves of bread, which were given out at the Ragged School on Thursday to the 'deserv- ing poor. I wish to make a special appeal for contributions of bread, money, or clothing. It is heart-rending to see poor women half-clad, crying for bread in this age of plenty.—Yours &amp;C., JAMES SKELLAND, 63A, Bewsey-street.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-03-06_WidnesExaminer_art0126" n="art0126">
        <head>Proposed Tariff Reform Debate.</head>
        <p>The Proposed Tariff Reform Debate.</p>
        <p>[COPY.] Dear Sir,—ln deference to your wishes we are prepared to nominate our first President and present vice-President, Mr. Joseph Howes, to meet a repre- sentative of the Tariff Reform League in a set public debate. Although we are willing to nominate one of our officials we would point out to you that we in no way tie you in your choice of a champion. but we are will- ing that you should select the most able man you can find in the country, so that the people of Warrington may have both sides of this question put before them and discussed in a genuine and lucid manner. • If you accept, we would suggest that a joint com- mittee be at once formed to carry out the necessary arrangements.—Yours truly, JOHN MORTON, J. HOWES, R.N., HON. SECS. Progressive Labour League. Tariff Reform League, Warrington.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-03-06_WidnesExaminer_art0127" n="art0127">
        <head>Church Parade for the Unemployed.</head>
        <p>A Church Parade for the Unemployed.</p>
        <p>Sir,—With unemployment and poverty being so prevalent in the town at present I -would deem it a favour if you would insert may appeal in your valuable paper. I am instructed by the Independent Order of Rechabites (Wycliffe Lent) to appeal to all 'Friendly Societies to co-operate in organising a church parade in Warrington district. Collections for the object to take place en route and at the church, which I am sure would be heartily responded to by the generous public. lam sure these societies would only be too pleased to help in allevating the sad distress which at "present prevails in our town if they Only had • the opportunity presented to them. I trust secretaries of the various societies will co-operate with me in making this appeal a success, and any information with respect to forming an organising committee will be thankfully received.—Yours truly,</p>
        <p>J'. A. BRADLEY,</p>
        <p>171, Longford Street.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-03-06_WidnesExaminer_art0128" n="art0128">
        <head>Invasion Danger.</head>
        <p>The Invasion Danger.</p>
        <p>Sir,—Mr. \Yardman ridicules the warnings as to the danger of attack on this country on the score that they are a mere scare. May I remind him of that historic document which ,appeared in the "Daily Telegraph " some few months back in which the Kaiser stated that it was onlY his intervention which prevented the coalition of Russia and France being made against us during the Boer War. Your correspondent condemns the action of the Alliance Assurance Company. In this question I am glad to be able to agree with him. I believe in com- pulsion for everyone and not for mere isolated cases. Moredver the men who want to be compelled more than anyone else are, as Lord Derby said the other day, those ,who have Money and leisure. The Alliance Assurance Co.'s scheme hits the toiler more than any- one else.</p>
        <p>With reference to the shorter hours, I may also say that I believe in the policy of concentrated work in short hours, and I believe that if our political leaders really knew the state of the physique of the country (which they would do if we had national military training, for every youth would have to go before the doctor) we should have a far greater effort made to ameliorate the condition under which the great mass of the people live. Yours truly, GEORGE CROSFIELD.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-03-06_WidnesExaminer_art0129" n="art0129">
        <head>Mr, A. H Crosfield, MP., and National</head>
        <p>Mr, A. H Crosfield, MP., and National</p>
        <p>Dance</p>
        <p>Sir,—Mr. A. H. Crosfield, M.P., in his interesting and sane letter on our home defences writes that he is sanguine that most of those who have urged a com- pulsory system, will be satisfied, if once Mr. Haldane obtains the number of Territorials asked, for, for "their chief object—reasonable national security—has been attained." Many members of the National Service League, although serving personally in Mr. Haldane's army, cannot feel real confidence in a force whose real training will only be given after war breaks out. Let another Liberal M.P., Mr. E. N. Bennett, voice their convictions on the necessity of adequate training. He writes :</p>
        <p>"But, try as we will, the haunting question always remains with us—can amateur troops success- fully face the disciplined valour of professional armies ? I have seen irregulars fight in various parts of the world, and some of us. remember the degxtiring cry of the British General in South-Africa as train-load after train-load of raw, undisciplined men, English and Colonial, was sent up country—" If this is all we have to depend "mon in any great national emer- gency,, then God help England ! " I once saw 40 lurk ish redifs keep at bay ],200 Cretan irregulars (with three field guns) for the greater part of a day. In very few walks of life can the amateur expect to hold his own against the professional. Can such a claim be made good in the case of our citizen troops, however brave our lads may naturally be, however obstinate and dogged our national character? That is the question. leb not presume to answer it. These things lie upon the knees of the gods." The National Service League does not wish to appear uncompromising, pigheaded, or fanatic. Their faith is not the creation of a passing whim, but of deliber- ate conviction that the peace training of four months proposed by the League is the irreducible minimum, which will suffice to give sufficient stability and cohesion to render the second line of defence fit to fulfil its function. Fighting value as well as numeri- cal strength is essential to a home defence army.— YlKur, etc., B. S. TOWNROE. 4, Winmarleigh-street, Warrington.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-03-06_WidnesExaminer_art0130" n="art0130">
        <head>Germany and Protection.</head>
        <p>Germany and Protection.</p>
        <p>Sir—ln amplification of the remarks made at a recent Free Trade meeting on the greater prosperity of the German workman I enclose a copy of a letter sent to the "Observer."</p>
        <p>"I notice in your notes of last week's issue you use the remarks which I made the other night with reference to the prosperty of the German workman as another proof of the value of Protection. Were the only difference between this country and Germany a fiscal one your conclusion would be perfectly logical. But since, as anyone knows who has studied the two nations, there are many other differences of a funda- mental character in the lives of the both, these must be taken into consideration before the main causes of prosperity can be determined. To state very</p>
        <p>briefly some of the chief institutions regulations to be found in Germany and not in this country:-- 1. Compuulsory insurance against old age, accident and disease.</p>
        <p>2. Compulsory Military Training. The value of this in adding to the happiness of the German people is best given in M. Dawson's words in " The volution of Modern Germany." "Whatever be the need and value of such service from the national defensive standpoint, the disciplinary and educative results are by universal testimony most beneficial, while the spirit of order, the habit of working together with others which he practices, enable the discharged soldier to fit naturally into the highly orgadised mechanism of modern industrial undertakings."</p>
        <p>3. A happy blending of professional with amateur municipal government, which results in better town administration as evidenced by systematic laying out of towns and by provision of healthy recreation areas within easy reach of towspeople* 4. Labour exchanges in all districrs so that labour is transferred at the cost ol the State where ever it is required. 5. General extension of the Elberfield system whose aims are the same as those of the league of Help recently started in the town. These it seems to me, are some of the reasons why there is a higher average level of happiness amongst the German working classes than here, despite the grave disadvantages of a tariff which causes the necessities of life to be in many instances twice as costly as they are in this country, and despite the facts that hours are longer and wages less and that there is less, individual liberty.—Yours etc, GEORGE CROSFIELD.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-03-06_WidnesExaminer_art0131" n="art0131">
        <head>4, Winmarleigh st. Remedy for Unemployment. A Statue for Market Gate.</head>
        <p>4, Winmarleigh st. Remedy for Unemployment. A Statue for Market Gate.</p>
        <p>"Instead of Temperance reducing unemployment it would increase it. Temperance is said to make efficiency, and experiments by Sir Thomas Brassy in the construction of the Great Western Railway seem to support this contention. His conclusion was that teetotal gangs of workmen were 15 per cent. betters worker than others. Which means that 85 teetotalers can do as much work as 100 alcohol drinkers. That proves clearly then, that if teetotalism is adopted we shall have another 15 per cent. added to the already 9 per cent. unemployed." LABOR OMNIA VINCIT.</p>
        <p>Sir,—On reading these —words, like Archimedes I cried "Eureka." This philosopher who was in a 1) th when his discovery dawned upon him, ran home half naked to get Hiero s crown to test his ; method. I however conducted myself with -decency, and straight- way realized that the unemployment difficulty was at last solved. For surely if by turning drinkers into teetotalers would increase unemployment by 15 per cent.,then we have only to reverse the process and turn teetotalers into drinkers to abolish the present 9 per cent. of unemployment.Ve have the bestthought of the country anxiously directed to this pressing problem, and here at our hands is a simple and effective remedy. But I Cannot claim the discoveryas altogether my own, because it is clearly suggested by your correspondent, and on his brow must rest the laurel. Like m4ny discoveries it suggests more than it first reveals, for if by increasing- drinking for the purpose of lessening efficiency more labour is demanded to produce the same results, it logically follows that anything that will reduce; efficiency will have a like effect. We my therefore couple with this, other methods for reducing efficiency. _ _ _</p>
        <p>-., - (1) More drinking. (2) Ca' Canny. ,(3) Fiscal Reform (Protection). (4) Destruction of labour saving appllinces. Thus the value of the discovery increases because we have several alternative methods. Surely it will make the year and the" good old town " of Warring- ton famous. Is the discovery not worthy of some per- manent recognition in the Borough.? Why not com- memorate it by the erection of a monument in the centre of Market Gate ? Four representative figures could be placed on a handsome base. The chief figure will of course be your correspondent under which might be cut 'the above sentences ;he would face Sankey Strek. Facing Buttermarket we should fittingly place the President of the Warrington Tariff Reform League, whose fine mobile face would be lifted towards the dawn, and beneath his figure might be placed "Cement the Empire by , taxing the people's food." To his left we should place the, figure of John Barleyoorn looking don Horsemarket with beaming face and viewing the many public houses which are to play the important part of loNver- ing efficiency that idle labour might be absorbed. In the fourth place looking to the Bridge that is to be, we would place the figure of Ned Lud, the.Leicester- shire youth, who gave his name to the Luddites, or machinery smashers, one hundred years ago. Strange that Ned should have to wait one hundred years before receiving public recognition, but his time has come and to Warrington be the honour of rescuing his great idea from oblivion. For if it be a good thing to make the workers less efficient by drinking with the object of causing/ more employment, surely the same end could be ) gained through reducing efficiency by smashing labour saving machinery. I trust the gifted Alderman will not feel uncomfor- table in the proposed association. His comrades might be able to prove to him that his methods (I mean his Protection methods) are akin to theirs. To exchange labour products by-buying from abroad because they are made at a less cost than at at home is clearly an economic fallacy, because if made at home more labour would be employed. Turn labour into inefficient channels as long as the end is gained i.e. more work. The amount of work being a fixed quantity and not governed by spending power and cost of wealth pro- duction, the more it can be spread out the better. Ignore the out-put and let the main consideration be the amount of labour employed. Work is an end, not a means. The producer is of more importance than the consumer, because we produce that we may con'.- sume, and not consume that we may produce. We live to work, not work to live. To which wisdom we might add that we see things best by standing on our heads, and that a good method of raising ourselves in the world is to tug at our boot-laces, and that the advice given to a man who wanted to cure his red nose "Drink more and it will soon be blue" is good in economics as well as in pictorial effect. CHARLES E. PARKER. Penketh, March 3rd, 1909.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-03-06_WidnesExaminer_art0132" n="art0132">
        <head>PALACE AND HIPPODROME.</head>
        <p>PALACE AND HIPPODROME.</p>
        <p>The Goddess of Wisdom was never known to be locked up in a water barrel, but this is Minerva's special fate—or feat—nightly at the Warrington Hippodrome. The lady who bears the title of Minerva is an intrepid person, and the audience gazes spell- bound as she is slowly immersed right overhead in a huge tub which has been previously filled with water in full view of the audience. Then the lid is put on the tub and fastened down with four substantial look- ing padlocks. A curtain is drawn round the tub, and in two minutes Minerva appears soaked and breathless but triumphant. More remarkable 'still the tub is found apparently intact and still securely locked. The trick—for of course it is nothing else—is a remarkably good one. We could give the secret away, but that would not be fair. The facts which cannot be disputed are that Minerva does enter the tub, goes beneath the Water, is lidded down, and escapes in two minutes. If you think you can solve the mystery go to the Hippodrome to-night and watch with all your eyes! In all respects the programme is a very good one this week. Winnifred Ward fairly -brings down the house as "the immaculate bay." Her male impersonations have the hall mark of talent, and she comes nearer to the high standard of Vesta Tilley than anyone else we have seen in the role. Other items which give satis- faction are: Ether Sheffer, an illustrated songstress, better than most; The Arntes, a very smart pair of coloured dancers and vocalists; T. C. Jaques, versatile comedian; and the Lydia Neilsoa boy and girl troutie in "An Impromptu Rehearsal." Special note must be made of the Howards, who give an extraordinary and puzzling display of thought reading and mental telepathy.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-03-06_WidnesExaminer_art0133" n="art0133">
        <head>NEET WEEK'S PROGRAMME.</head>
        <p>NEET WEEK'S PROGRAMME.</p>
        <p>Next week's bill is headed by Master George Wood, the world's greatest juvenile \artiste. The Bouncing Dillons (Graceful Hilda and Bouncing Willie), comedy gymnast, will present a novel turn and they also announce a bouncing convetition for cash prizes. Other artistes will he :—Cosgrove and Burns, comedy entertainers, in "The Singing Academy " ; Jack Hayworth, Scotch comedian; J. W. Rowley, the people's comedian; Sybil Montague, vocalist; Seven Wulfrunas,in their comedy acrobatic speciality act," Fun in a Cafe."</p>
        <p>There are nine wonders in the' world, and surely our Postal system is one of these; to send a message 16,000 miles for a penny is a triumph of organisation. There's another fact more wonderful still,Woods' Great Peppermint Cure, that invincible remedy for coughs, colds, influenza can be bought throughout the world; all races, be they white, brown or black, know its value. —Advt.</p>
      </div>
