        <p>A SECTION OF THE CROWD AT THE WARRINGTON v. LEEDS CUP-TIE. The original of the portrait marked with a X will receive the " Examiner" prize of is. 6d. on calling at the "Examiner "Office, Bewsey-street, before Saturday, March 13th.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-03-06_WidnesExaminer_art0135" n="art0135">
        <head>G] DISTRICT NEWS.</head>
        <p>G] DISTRICT NEWS.</p>
        <p>Risley.</p>
        <p>INDEPENDENT METHODIST CHAru..—Special mission ser% ices in connection With the above place are be'ng held commencing on Sunday the 7th hist and continuing every evening during the week until Friday the 111.11 inst. The preaclier will be Mr. T. E. Cook of Bina°, near Coble. •</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-03-06_WidnesExaminer_art0136" n="art0136">
        <head>Hatton.</head>
        <p>Hatton.</p>
        <p>Dr.aTir or Miss A. TAYLOR.—Considerable gloom was cast over the inhabitants of Hatton by the death of :Nils.; A. Taylor, only durghter of Mr. .1. Taylor, of Oak Trce• Farm. lhe deceased NA ho passed away on M6nrlay morning, at 11 o'clock bad been ailing for a period of two years, ane her end was not altogether unPxpected. The young lady was greatly esteemed in the neighbourhood and will be much missed.</p>
        <p>BILLIARD HANDICAP.—A billiarl handicap in con- nection with the Parish Room, has :just terminated. Prizes to the amount of £2 being kindly given by the Vicar of Daresbury, Rev. T.l'. Lee-Jones. The success- ful players were :—1 Mr. Houghton, 2 Mr. W. W. Leach, 3 Mr. L. Leann, 4 Mr. J. Maddock, 5 Mr.D.Done.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-03-06_WidnesExaminer_art0137" n="art0137">
        <head>Cuicheth.</head>
        <p>Cuicheth.</p>
        <p>DEATH OF AN OLD RESIDENT.—The death occurred on the 24th ult. at Manor House, C,lcheth, of Mrs. Rutter, who for over 40 years had been a resident in the neighbourhood. Tho demetsed lady whose remains were laid to rot in the Parish Church, Newohurch, on Satur- (by last was 07 years of age and deeply respected, evidence of which was shown in a marked degree by the number of beautiful wreaths which almost covered the hearse. She leaves two sons and a daughter to mourn her loss.</p>
        <p>THE SCARLET FEVER. EPIDEMIC.—Owing to an epidemic of scarlet fever the Sunday Schoels belong- ing to the Parish Church, Newohurob, and the Inde- pendent Methodist Chapel have been closed for several wf eks but will be re-opened again on Sunday as usual The day school has been closed for 3 weeks, and is expected to re-open again on Monday the 18th inst., providing there is no further outbreak. Moore.</p>
        <p>LENTEN SERVICES.—The first series of weekly ser- viises held during Lent, was conducted by the Vicar, the Rev. T. T. Lee-Jones, in the Milner Church Institute, on Friday evening, February 26th, and was well attended.</p>
        <p>PRIZE DISTRIBUTION.—On Thursday evening the annual Prize distribution, to scholars of the Moore Wesleyan Sunday school, took place. The prizes, which as usual, took the form of books, were distributed to the successful scholars by Mr. E. J. Guest, B.A. of War- rington, who also acted as chairman in the absence of the Bev. T. Miller. Mr. Guest said he did not wish to giye them the usual Chairman's address, but he gave a little helpful advice to the children' on books, as they are to-day, and the progress which has been made in their production during the last three hundred years (A miscellaneous nrogramme, comprising recitations, songs, and dialogues was provided by the scholars, and was very much enjoyed by all present. Special hymns were sung by the choir and children under the-leader- ship of Messrs. J. Banner and James Antrobus. Mr. Guest was accorded a hearty vote of thanks for distri- buting the prizes, which numbered about seventy. ".</p>
        <p>SOCIAL IMPROVEM ENT SOCIETY. - The members and friends of the above society gathered together on Monday evening for "an evening with Longfellow"; the lecturer being Mr. Harold Jones Of Frodsharn. Great interest was manifested in the lecture, owing to the fact that Mr. Jones, unfortunately is blind. He read the lecture with he aid of his fingers, the char- acters being indicated by a series of small holes punched in the paper. Mr. Jones gave a short biographical sketch; and read various selections from some of the poet's best works, which were warmly applauded. He also commented on the fact, that, although Longfellow's poetry is not classed among the greatest of that time, his works have a simplicity and beauty which appeals more to the mass of the people, than the more profound works of such poets as Robert Browning. At the close of the lecture, the Vice- president of the society, Mr. Davies, spoke of Long- fellow as resembling Ruskin in his great love of nature, and reverence for the Creator, he also spoke very highly of the able way in which Mr. Jones had handled his subject. A very hearty vote of thanks was accorded to the lecturer on the proposition of Miss Dutton, seconded by Mr. Jackson.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-03-06_WidnesExaminer_art0138" n="art0138">
        <head>Penketh.</head>
        <p>Penketh.</p>
        <p>WESLEYAN' CHOIR. ENTERTAINED.—On Saturday evening the menThers of the choir and friends were invited to a social by the choirmaster, Mr. George Weaver. In the first part of the evening an enjoyable programme was given consisting of pianoforte solo, vocal solos, quartettes, duets and recitations by members of the choir, Misses Moores, Johnson, Cooke, Ashton, Bates, Rowledge, Ireland, Diggory and Messrs. W. Foden' Johnson, J. Ireland, Budden, Barber, A. Flumpton, Phcenix, N. Ray. After the musical pro- gramme refreshments were handed round. Games and dancing were then indulged in until 10.30, when a vote of thanks to the host was carried wiith acclamation and brought to a dote an evening which will long be remembered by those present. DEATH OF MR. A. BIRCH.—Many residents of the districtwill deeply regret to hear of the death of Mr. Arthur Birch, Tower View, Great Sankey, who passed away very suddenly on Wednesday morning. The deceased gentleman, who was 61 years of age, was a member of the Great Sankey Parish Council and a manager of the Sankey Council school. He wai a regular worshipper at the Penketh Wesleyan Church and he will be much missed by the congregation. A Liberal in politics, he at one time took an active part in political contests at Warrington and had acted as chairman or speaker at many meetings. He supported Mr. J. A. Seddon, M.P., the Labour member for the Newton Division at the last general election. Mr. Birch was an iron worker by trade and whilst working at Wigan a fortnight, ago he met with a slight accident. He had been unwell over since, but the end came unexpectedly. Mr. Birch was an enthusiast cyclist and delighted 4n taking long tours. Much sympathy is felt with the widow, son and daughter in their bereavement. TEMPERANCE MISSION.—A three days' Temperance Mission was held in the Penketh Wesleyan schoolroom on Monday, Tuesday and Wednesday, commencing each night at 7.30. The sileaker on Monday night was Mr. Garside of the U.K.A., Manchester, who came to take the plate of Mr. Thompson, who had been called away to Scotland. Mr. Garside proved himself a good substitute in the address he gave on "Why abstain." Mrs. Robert Garnett presided, and in a few well-chosen remarks said how pleased she was that those meetings were taking place and hoped a good deal of good would result. The Bev. F. W. Kernick, M.A , also spoke and said bow he would like to see a temperance hall in Penketh so that the young people would have some- where to spend their evenings. On Tuesday evening the meeting was presided over by the Rev. A. M. Mitchell, vicar of Burtonwood, who is well known and liked in the district. He gave a rousing addresa and was well received by those present. Mr. Alexander Thompson, superintendent of the United Kingdom Alliance, gave on address on." Shall we support the public-house or the home "and in his opening remarks said be was sorry not to be present on Monday, but he knew that in Mr. Garside, who had taken his place, they had had a good substitute. In his address he said that if the money that was spent in the public-house was spent on the home in the way of better food and clothes, it would help to find employment for those who at present were unemployed. He instanced how that the brewing trade paid only a very small amount in wages in the coal trade out of every £1 worth of coal Ils, was paid back in the way of wages while in the brewing trade, out of every £1 worth of beer brewed, only 13s. 4d. was paid in wages. At the close the Rev. J. Gunnell moved a vote of thanks to the chairman and speaker. This was seconded by Mr, James Dodds. and heartily carried. On Wednesday evening Er. Thompson again addressed the gathering, on "Who shall be master, the uncle or the people ? " This was a very fine address, and Mr. Thompson was quite at home with his subject.—Mr. James Dodds presided, and taid how pleased he was that these meetings had been held,, and asked those present to do their best for the cause of temperance-- At the close a meeting was held with a view to form- ing an adults' Temperance League.—Mrs. R. Garnett was elected president and a number of members enrolled.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-03-06_WidnesExaminer_art0139" n="art0139">
        <head>Stockton Heath.</head>
        <p>Stockton Heath.</p>
        <p>BAND OF HOPE.—A concert in connection with the Hill Cliffe and Stockton Heath United Band of Hope was held in the Wesleyan School room on Monday evening. Mrs. Simpson, of Warrington, a vice- president of the 14 arrington Band of Hope Union, presided, and was supported by Mr. Arthur .Mounfleld, President of the society, and Mr. G. Whitfield, secretary. The programme consisted of a good selection of drills, action songs, dances and solos, by the pupils of the Misses Hankey, of Warrington, each item being exceedingly well given, and reflecting great credit on both performers and trainers. The humorous element was in the hands of Master Richard Lawton, who delighted the audience with his funny sketches and was encored for each item. Miss May Isherwood's song, "Tit for Tat" was well rendered and much appre- ciated. Miss Elsie Hewitt contributed "Father Eternal" and "The children's home," proving herself to be a very promising vocalist. The duet I won't ask you to my party, by Elsie Whitfield aud Dolly Cain, was very well sung and was encored. One of the principal items of the evening was the dialogue "Paddy's Pledge"given by Misses Hall and El lison,Messrs G. Gould and Walter Wilkes and Miss Elsie Whitfield, which created much amusement and was thoroughly enjoyed. Mrs. Simpson gave an address and a vote of thanks was, on the motion of Mr. G. Whitfield, accorded to all who had taken part in the meeting. Mr. G. A. Whitfield and Miss Hankey acted as accompanists.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-03-06_WidnesExaminer_art0140" n="art0140">
        <head>Lymm</head>
        <p>Lymm</p>
        <p>SNOW EITECTS.—Lymm has been no exception in the matter of weather during the week. A heavy fall of snow on Tuesd.iy night bad the effect of transforming the leafless trees and shrubs into things of beauty. The whole countryside is in a really very charming mood. MAY QUEEN OR No MAY QUEEN ?—This question is agitating the minds of many in and around bymm just now. It is certainly a day of great, rejoicing for the children and if only on that account it is to be hoped that the attempt to revive an old-time custom will be persevered with. HOCKEY.—LYMM LADIES V. THE GRAMMAR SCHOOL.—The former were in better trim for the return engagement which was played on the school ground on Baturday last, for the score of 3-0, the previous was turned into one of a goal each. The play of the ladies was very good their formation being excellent. Miss Wright in goal, Miss L. Pearson a centre forward, and Miss E. Dalton were conspicuous in a good lot.</p>
        <p>Socf.m..--A very enjoyable social evening was spent on Wednesday night at the Wesleyan schools, a large number being present who thoroughly enjoyed the capital musical entertainment provided. The officials of the Sunday school are to be congratulated on the success of their initial efforts in this direction to assist the funds of the scheol. The following artistes took part in the entertainment: The Misses A. U. Rothwell, Isherwood, Whitfield, Ciin, Jones and Powell ; whilst Mr. F. Whitfield contributed two recitations. FREE TRADE MEETING.</p>
        <p>A Free Trade meeting was held on Tuesday evening last in the Congregational schoolroom. Mr. A. Watkin presided, and the principal speakers were Miss Dorothy Hunter (Free Trade Union) and Mr. Councillor Middle- ton, of Oldham, supported by Mr. Watkinson. On the platform were the Rev. A. airrett, Mrs. W. E. Thomp- son, Messrs. A. E. de Parmiter, G. L. We!ford, W. E. Thompson, W. J. Wright, A. H. Gotobed, D. G. Smith non-attendance were read, including one from Mr. and, J. D. B. Smith. Mr. J. B. Smith reported that he had received letters from the following gentlemen regretting their inability to attend the meeting and supporting their object : Messrs. F. W. Steel, G. P. Dewhurst, J.P., F. H. Smith, J.P., C. j. Miller, J.P., Swanwick, also a telegram from Mr. Wm. Long, J.P., wns read.</p>
        <p>The Chairman said they met that night for the sup- port of Free Trade, and so far as they were concerned they were met neither as Liberals, Conservatiyes, Tories, Radicals nor Socialists. They were Free Traders pure and simple, determined to maintain the present fiscal policy until the final came when it was clearly proved beyond doubt that the doctrine of Free Trade had failed. He should like to speak to the tradesmen of Lymm and also to the workingmen of Lymm. By workingmen he meant the men who earned their living by manual labour. We were all dependent upon one another. He recalled the fact that many people in Lymm were engaged in business in Manchester and other parts of Lancashire, particularly in thee otton trade. If they did pot prosper, the people of Lymm would not prosper. He wanted to put one point before them, If the Tariff Reform party came into power they would place an import duty upon all foreign manufactures. 'that inclurled, of course, cotton goods. At first blush that seemed a nice sort of suggestion. They in Manchester were suffering very seriously indeed from the effects of bad trade. In India the people alresdy manufaetuted a very large amount of cotton goods for themselves, and this was increasing year by year. If this country went in for Protection, could they honestly refuse the same privilege to those people in India who demanded the preference which was given to the Colonies? Lancashire at the present tinie exported to India 40 per cent, of its vast amount of cotton manufactures. If Protection were introduced into this country we were bound in honour to grant the same concession of preference to India. Therefore, what would happen? India was at present manufacturing about 30 per cent of their cotton, and in a few years it would be 70 per cent. England was now exporting 40 per cent. and in a very short time it would be 10 per cent. Unemployment would be ten times worse than it was at present. The business would be in India and not in England. He was willing to admit that our fiscal arrangements in this country were not perfect. They must remember they could not get their own way; and it would be better to hear the ills we had than fly to others we knew not of. (Applause.) They were not prepared for this tremendous leap in the dark, that was being pressed upon the country at the present time with so light a heart. (Applause.) Miss Hunter said that it seemed to her that Free Trade was the most important political question before the country at the present time, for the reason that whatever politics had been, whatever they were going to be, at the present moment there were practically only two parties in the country who could be, or had the slightest chance of forming a majority in the House of Commons, the Liberal party and the "Protectionist" party. She said Proteccionist' because, nowadays, they were that section of the Conservative party, who bad any chance of forming a Government. Whatever other questions were before the country at the next general election, there was no shadow of a doubt, but that their votes would be taken,as a direct verdict, upon the question of whether they wanted Tariff Reform or wanted to keep to Free Trade. Thera was no doubt that a large number of people had been won over to Tariff Reform during the last few years. They wanted to try to find out why and how they could win some of them back, and prevent any more going over. There were a groat many motives from which Teeple became Protectionists. Whatever the Protectionists called themselves their policy was the same. It simply meant putting taxes on goods received from abroad. There were a considerable class of people who were Protectionists from purely selfish motives. That class thought that if they could get a tax on that particular class of goods, so that other goods could be shut oil; of the country they would be able to get a higher price for the same sort of goods at home. The Protectionist wanted to be protected from foreign competition. if we were protected from., foreign competition we would have to be satisfied with inferior articles at a high price when we could get better articles at a low price. From the moment we introduced any system of Protection we would give our country over to those men who wanted Tariff, Reform. They heard a great deal of scientific tariffs but there was no such thing as preference in any protected country. There were seine who became Tariff Reformers from Imperial motives, for they believed that it would be a way of welding the Empire together. She thought that Imperial motive was only to cover the selfish one. They used those Imperial sentiments, merely to hide the sordid motive behind. At the time we had protection there was a very great deal of fric- tion and ill feeling between ourselves and the Colonies. There was a feeling in our country then, that the Colonies were a burden to us. There had been no such friction since the days of Free Trade. It was impossible to give a preference to the Colonies without taxing food and raw materials. If we did not tax raw materials in order to give preference to the Colonies we must tax food; because the Colonies only sent us raw materials and food. We could not have Colonial preference without a tax upon corn and meat, and a tak upon those would raise the price in this country. Some were Tariff Reformers</p>
        <p>because they believed we were not being treated fairly by other nations. The best way of fighting those hostile tariffs was by free imports. From the Imperial point of view and the Retaliation. point of view Pro- tection would be a hopeless failure. The Protectionist argument was, that if we kept out foreign goods we should get more employment for our own people. They must always look at the whole question and not merely at the side picture that was presented to them by the Tariff Reformers. Even if we paid in gold— which we dq not—for our own imports it would mean that British labour would have to be used. We could not buy foreign articles without employing British labour. The whole result of Protection would be' simply to make us pay more for what we wanted Landlords and manufacturers might gain, but everybody else would lose by the simple process of having to pay more for what they wanted. (Applause). Councillor Middleton, of Oldham, then addressed the meeting. He said that we had in this country a certain food product of which we exported to Germany a great deal—herrings. The Germans had a fancy for herrings, because they could not buy beef and mutton as we could. (Laughter). We exported £1,600,000 worth of herrings annually to Germany. In return we got pianos and other useful things. The way to keep trade was not by Protection. That was the vtay to make trade what it was before we had any good trade The Protectionists wanted to put a 2s. duty on corn.. It was explained that it was only a little one; but it was like a little child, it tended to grow. The Germans began with a shilling and now it was 12s. The answer they had got to make at the General Election was: Don't you touch the children's bread. (Applause). . .</p>
        <p>On the proposition of Mr. G. L. Welford, seconded by A. de. C. Parruiter, supported by Mr. 0. G. Smith, a hearty vote of thanks to the Chairman and speakers concluded the meeting.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-03-06_WidnesExaminer_art0141" n="art0141">
        <head>LICENSING MATTERS.</head>
        <p>LICENSING MATTERS.</p>
        <p>ADJOURNED ANNUAL SESSIONS AT WARRINGTON.</p>
        <p>The adjourned annual Licensing Sessions for the borough of Warrington were held at the Police Court yesterday morning. The Mayor (Alderman Forshaw) presided and there were also present :—Aldermen Smethurst and Bennett, Councillor Wilkinson, Dr. Fox, Messrs. John White, H. Roberts, R. T. Fairclough, W. Roberts, S. Wright, W. Furness, Edwin Woods, C. S. Burgess, J. W. Hayward, C. Jackson Holmes, A.J.Jolley, and H. Thornton.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-03-06_WidnesExaminer_art0142" n="art0142">
        <head>BRIDGE INN TRANSFER.</head>
        <p>THE BRIDGE INN TRANSFER.</p>
        <p>Mr. A. Brtowni applied on behalf of Henry Ashton for confirmation of the removal of the licence of the Bridge Inn to the Orford Hotel to be erected in Gorsey Lane.He stated that at the recent sessions he pointed o ut that the Bridge Inn was required for local improve- ments and proved to the satisfaction of the Bench th at a new house was required at Orford. The offer of Messrs. Greenall, Whitley and Co., was that they would give the Bridge Inn buildings and the whole of the freehold land, 562 square yards in area, to the Corpora- tion, surrender the licence of the Little Horse Shoe, Horsemarket-street (attaching the stables to the Blue Bell Inn), and also an off-licence in Brick-street. The Little Horse Shoe was a very valuable licence and, according to Mr. Justice Kennedy, must be worth from £4,000 to £s,ooo.There was no opposition to the transfer at the last hearing, so there could be none now. The Bench confirmed he transfer and &amp;ordered that 21 days notice of the final grant should be irven.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-03-06_WidnesExaminer_art0143" n="art0143">
        <head>ROSE AND CROWN.</head>
        <p>ROSE AND CROWN.</p>
        <p>The renewal of the licence of the Rose and Crown, Bridge-street, was objected to on the grounds that., the licence was not required to meet the wants of the neighbourhood and that its inclusion made the number of houses excessive. It was stated that within a radius of 440 yards there were 6,326 inhabitants and 69 licensed houses, making one licence for every 92 inhabitants. The average for the town was one licence for every 363 inhabitants.</p>
        <p>Thomas Hockenhull, the tenant, stated that he had no objection to the house being placed on the compensa- tion list and the Bench renewed the licence provision- ally and referred it to the Compensation Authority.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-03-06_WidnesExaminer_art0144" n="art0144">
        <head>BIRD IN HAND.</head>
        <p>THE BIRD IN HAND.</p>
        <p>The licence of the Bird in Hand, Bank-street, was objected to on similar grounds, the number of licensed houses within a 440 yards radius working out at one for every 101 inhabitants.</p>
        <p>Tlie tenant, Patrick William Roddy, offered no objec- tion to the licence being referred to the compensation authority and this course was sanctioned by the Bench, it being understood that the house would be closed as soon as there was sufficient money in the compensation fund.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-03-06_WidnesExaminer_art0145" n="art0145">
        <head>STAR INN.</head>
        <p>THE STAR INN.</p>
        <p>The objection to the-r-e;;;;;I-Oi the licence of the Starlnn, Longford Street, on the ground of redundancy, was resisted by the tenant, William Corker, for whom Mr. A. Browne appeared. Mr. Crompton, of the Borough Surveyor's department stated that the inhabitants within 440 yards radius were estimated at 6657, and the licensed houses numbered 16, making one licence for every 41(1 inhabitants. The nearest public house was the Horse and Jockey, 83 yards distant, but there was an off licence (Joseph Tavlin's) only 17 yards away. Inspector Williamson said that when he served the notice of objection, Mr. Corker told him that he did a" fairly moderate "trade. Witness's visit took place in the afternoon and there were five men in the house. The population in the neighbourhood of Longford had increased considerably of late years. Mr. Corker had held the licence from October, 1886. Mr. Browne My clients are very anxious to know how this opposition has arisen, because none of the Licensing Committee have over visited the house.</p>
        <p>Witne,:s : I have no knowledge on that point. Ins pector Forster stated that he had frequently visited the house and as a rule there were only three or four customers in. On rare occasions there might be six or seven. The house was well conducted. Mr. Browne: Is there a better conducted house in Warrington? Witness: I don't think so.</p>
        <p>Mr. Browne, addressing the Bench, stated that the Cheshire Lines Committee were the owners of the houst. and neither they nor Mr. Corker could imagine why It should be proposed that the licence should be placed on the compensation list. The Licensing Sub- committee had not visited the house and did not know N. hat it was like. It was one of the cleanest and best-conducted houses in Warrineton and moreover it was a free house, both Messrs. Greensll's and Messrs. Walker's beer being supplied. He contended that it was required to meet the needs of the neighbourhood and the accommodation was good in every respect. It would be iniquitous—in fact it would be robbery—to take Mr. Corker's living from him. He was injured in the railway accident at PeListone Junction but did not claim damages and in consequence the Cheshire Lines Committee allowed him to have the house at a cheaper rent. The premises were, bought to allow of the con- struction of a loop line, but they would not be required for many years. With regard to the trade of the house the barrels sold of recent years numbered :-1906, 209; 1907, 231 i ; 1908 201 i ; the average working out at 44 barrels per week. The quantities of bottled beers sold. were :-1906, 326 dozens 1997, 356 dozens; 1908, 510 dozens. No fewer than 267 householders who were customers at the Star Inn, had signed a petition in favour of the continuance of the licence. It would he a monstrous shame to deprive Mr. Corker of his living when he had been the tenant for 23 years. Several witnesses were called in favour of the renewal of the licence, including Councillor Cunnington who stated that in his opinion the house was required to meet the needs of the neighbourhood and he would be sorry to see it done away with. . , Mr. W. Roberts: Are you a customer? Witness: I am. I like a glass of beer as well as anybody else.</p>
        <p>M. ilrowne : DO you know any other house in Warrington where you can get both Greenall's and Walker's beer? Witncs!:_ I don't think so.</p>
        <p>The Chief Constable said he was of opinion that there were other houses in Waeington which could be better spared than the Star Inn. Not the slightest complaint had been received as to the way in which the business was conducted. He thought the Bench would be well advised in renewing the licence. Mr. 11. Roberts: Perhaps you will prepare a list for next year of the licences you think can be spared. The Chief Constable: It would be ridiculous for me to say that any hour could not be spared, because any house can be spared for that matter. After half-an-hour's private discus,sion the Bench dezided to renew the licence.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-03-06_WidnesExaminer_art0146" n="art0146">
        <head>STRIKE AT tqaUUNGTON.</head>
        <p>STRIKE AT tqaUUNGTON.</p>
        <p>The moulders of ,rain-water goods employed by Whyman's Foundry Co., Ltd., Dallam-lane, Warrington, are at present on strike. The men, to the number of 100, left work on Tuesday and up to last evening there was no prospect of a settlement of the dispute. It appears that eight months ago the moulders in the Falkirk district (Scotland) submitted to a 21 per cent, reduction in their wages, but Messrs. Whyman have continued to pay the full amount. Owing, however, to depression and the low prices that were being obtained, the firm have recently considered it absolutely necessary to ask the men to accept the 21 per cent. reduction but they declined to do so and ceased work on Tuesday last. Messrs. 'Whyman state that they offered to discuss the matter with the men, but their overtures were declined because they refused to meet the committee of the Central Moulders' Association of Scotland—the trade union to which the moulders belong. The firm maintain that they will only deal with their own workpe ople and they resent any outside interference.</p>
        <p>For Influenza and Bronchial Coughe,Woods' Great Peppermint Cure. ls. lid., 2s. 9d.—Advt.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-03-06_WidnesExaminer_art0147" n="art0147">
        <head>ORFORD WARD VACANCY.</head>
        <p>ORFORD WARD VACANCY.</p>
        <p>Liberals Select a Candidate. The annual meeting of the Liberal Committee for the Orford Ward was held at the Liberal Club on Mon day evening. Councillor Wilkinson presided over a good attendance which included Messrs. T. Pemberton, Peter Peacock, A. H. Sanders and J. Howes, jun. (Liberal agent). The following officers were re-elected :—Chairman, Councillor Wilkinson; vice- chairmen, Messrs. J. Upson, and Peter Peacock; secretary, Mr. A. H. Sanders. The representatives on the Exemtive Committee and General Council of the Liberal Association were selected and a number of names added to the Ward Committee.</p>
        <p>The question of the vacancy was discussed and it was unanimously resolved that a candidate be found to contest the ward in the Liberal interests. A cordial. invitation was extended to Mr. Joseph Wilkinson, brother of the chairman, to be the candidate and every member present promised him whble-hearted support. Mr. Wilkinson has, however, declined to stand. An an enthusiastic meeting last evening, Mr. J. Halliwell was adopted. The election of a Councillor for Orford Ward te fill the vacancy caused by the unseating of Councillor Peter Peacock will take place on Tuesday, March 16th. Monday next is the last day for receiving nominations. Mr. James Halliwell, boot and shoe dealer, Whitefield Road, Stockton Heath, is the Liberal candidate and he is receiving most enthu- siastic support. Nomination papers on his behalf. were handed in yesterday. In the first paper Mr. Halliwell is proposed by Peter Peacock, seconded by Elijah Cannington, and supported by Thomas Hardman, Teddy Spittle, George Samuel Heath Gillin, Joseph Hughes, Benjamin Wisedale, William Winstanley,. Thomas .Marchant Roden, and William Sheldon. In a second paper Mr. Halliwell is pro- posed by William Smith, seconded by James Edward Crushaw, and supported by Isaac Bower, William. PilkingtOn, Albert Edward Baker, Peers Turner' - William Blackledge, William Fisher, Joseph Armitage, Charles C. B. Hughes, and William Spence. Mr. W. McNeil Sloane, manager, 57, Orford-avenue, who styles himself an" independent "candidate, has • also been nominated. •</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-03-06_WidnesExaminer_art0148" n="art0148">
        <head>MR. RYLANDS AND THE ELECTION PETITION.</head>
        <p>MR. RYLANDS AND THE ELECTION PETITION.</p>
        <p>Incensed with the " Radical Organ."</p>
        <p>Under the auspices of the Conservative AssocTation a public meeting was held on Thursday evening in St. James' School, Latchford. Councillor W. B. Roberts presided over a good attendance, -which included Mr. Robert Pierpoint, the prospective candidate for the borough. Mr. W. P. Rylands, in supporting a resolution, alluded to the recent election petition, and remarked that he had nothing whatever to do with it, so he could speak in n detached way, like any othe- rAtianiyer. There were lessons to be learned from it.. and those lessons ought to be taken to heart. For his part, he regarded it as a lamentable occurrence, and the sooner it was forgotten the better it word(' be for the town. He would not, have referred to it again had it not been for the fact that the organ of the Radical party—perhaps voicing the feeling of the Radical party —was trying to use the conduct of the petition as a vehicle for an attack on the Conservative party. A most deplorabb thing connected with the incident had been the fact that apparently the leaders of the Radical party, instead of joining hands with the Conservatives in expressing their hatred of improper conduct, which was so roundly stigmatised by the Commissioner, were practically condoning the action of Mr. Peacock's agents and suggesting that the petition should never have been brought. He hoped that any condonation of that kind would not find a response with the electors of this town. There could be no doubt that Mr. Rushworth and his supporters would never have undertaken that frightful expense of those proceedings unless they believed they had really strong ground for doing so, They did not want men's votes purchased by the price of a glass of beer or any- thing else, and Radicals and Conservatives alike ought to condemn it. (Applause).</p>
        <p>Wycliffe Young People's Society. Monday evening's meeting of this Society took the form of a "musical evening" arranged by Mr. W. H. Payton, Mils. Bac., organist of the church. There was a large attendance, and the admirable programme submitted was highly appreciated. Miss Farrar and Mr. W. H. Payton gave the pianoforte duets. " Morning" from the Peer Gynt Suite" (Greg) and " Mazurka " (Greg) very effectively, and Mr. Payton played the following selections from `• Songs without Words " (illendolsaohn) in his usual tasteful style :—" Duetto," " March Fnnebres " and " Spring Song." Mrs. E. A. Sanders sang "A Laschia pi anga," " 0 peaceful England " and "Annie Laurie" with fine power and expression, and Mrs. Griffiths was beard to advantage in " When the heart is young." " A duet "0 that we two might wander" lov Miss E. Brown and Mr. A. Griffiths was well received. Mr. Griffiths' interpretations of " Watch me love, to-night " and "Good company " were much enjoyed, and Mr. W. H. Wallington sang "To Anthea " and "The old black mare" very pleasingly. On the proposition of 10. W. Brolvn, seconded by Hr. B. Farrar, a hearty vOte of thanks was accorded Mr. Payton for arranging the entertainment, and to all the artistes who gave their services. For all Chronic Chest Complaints, take Woods' Great Peppermint Cure, is. lid., 2s. 9d.—Advt.</p>
        <p>Warrington.—Printed and published by iORN WALKBR k Co., (Warrington) Ltd., at The Examiner General Printing Works, Bewsey-street, and published by them at the earns place, and also at The Examiner Offices, Victoria-road WIDNRS, 32, George-street, ST. EIELF.Ns: 3, Market Street EARLBSTOWN ; Church-street RUNCORN Mar. 6th1809</p>
      </div>
